window.DATA={"topics": [{"name": "Kubernetes", "slug": "kubernetes", "icon": "☸️", "color": "#326ce5", "blurb": "Pods, probes, rollouts, networking, EKS. Most-asked topic in DevOps interviews.", "diagrams": [{"t": "Pod troubleshooting flow", "steps": ["kubectl get pods", "Status? Pending / CrashLoop / ImagePull", "kubectl describe pod (Events)", "kubectl logs --previous", "Fix: probe / limits / config / image", "kubectl rollout status"]}, {"t": "How a request reaches a Pod", "steps": ["Client", "Ingress / LoadBalancer", "Service (selector → Endpoints)", "Ready Pod only", "Container"]}, {"t": "Safe rollout & rollback", "steps": ["kubectl apply new image", "Rolling update (maxSurge / maxUnavailable)", "Readiness probe gates traffic", "Healthy? yes → done", "No → kubectl rollout undo"]}], "cheats": [{"c": "kubectl get pods -o wide", "d": "Pods with node and IP"}, {"c": "kubectl describe pod <p>", "d": "Events: probe fails, OOMKilled, scheduling errors"}, {"c": "kubectl logs <p> --previous", "d": "Logs of the crashed container"}, {"c": "kubectl get endpoints <svc>", "d": "Which Pod IPs a Service really sends traffic to"}, {"c": "kubectl rollout undo deploy/<d>", "d": "Roll back to previous revision"}, {"c": "kubectl top pod", "d": "CPU/memory usage (metrics-server)"}, {"c": "kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp", "d": "Cluster events in time order"}, {"c": "kubectl exec -it <p> -- sh", "d": "Shell inside the container"}], "follow": [{"k": ["crashloop", "crash"], "q": "What is the difference between CrashLoopBackOff and ImagePullBackOff?", "a": "CrashLoopBackOff means the container starts but keeps exiting, so I check logs with --previous and the exit code. ImagePullBackOff means the image never downloaded, so I check the image name, tag, registry credentials in imagePullSecrets, and network access to the registry."}, {"k": ["oom", "memory", "limit"], "q": "Pod shows OOMKilled with exit code 137. What do you do?", "a": "It means the container crossed its memory limit and the kernel killed it. I check kubectl top and Grafana for the real usage pattern, then either raise the limit or fix a memory leak. I also set requests close to real usage so scheduling is accurate."}, {"k": ["probe", "liveness", "readiness"], "q": "Liveness vs readiness vs startup probe - when do you use each?", "a": "Readiness decides if a Pod receives traffic. Liveness decides if the container should be restarted. Startup probe protects slow-starting apps so liveness does not kill them too early. A common mistake is making liveness depend on a database, which causes restart storms."}, {"k": ["rollout", "deploy", "rollback", "update"], "q": "How does a rolling update work and how do you control it?", "a": "The Deployment creates a new ReplicaSet and shifts Pods gradually, controlled by maxSurge and maxUnavailable. Readiness probes make sure old Pods are removed only after new ones are Ready. If it goes wrong, kubectl rollout undo returns to the previous ReplicaSet."}, {"k": ["service", "endpoint", "selector", "label", "503"], "q": "Service exists but no traffic reaches the Pods. What do you check?", "a": "First kubectl get endpoints to see whether the Service has Pod IPs. If empty, the selector does not match Pod labels or Pods are not Ready. Then I check targetPort versus the container port, and NetworkPolicies."}, {"k": ["configmap", "secret", "config"], "q": "ConfigMap vs Secret - and are Secrets really secure?", "a": "Both inject config, Secrets are meant for sensitive data. By default Secrets are only base64 encoded, not encrypted. In production I enable encryption at rest with KMS, restrict RBAC, and prefer external stores like AWS Secrets Manager with External Secrets Operator."}, {"k": ["pending", "schedul", "node", "taint", "affinity"], "q": "Pod is stuck in Pending. Possible reasons?", "a": "Insufficient CPU or memory on nodes, taints without tolerations, node selectors or affinity that cannot be met, or an unbound PVC. kubectl describe pod shows the exact scheduler message under Events."}, {"k": ["hpa", "scale", "autoscal", "load"], "q": "How does HPA work and what can go wrong?", "a": "HPA reads metrics, usually CPU or memory from metrics-server, and adjusts replicas to hit a target utilization. It fails if resource requests are not set, metrics-server is down, or the node group has no capacity, so I pair it with Cluster Autoscaler or Karpenter."}, {"k": ["eks", "alb", "ingress", "elb", "loadbalancer"], "q": "On EKS, how does traffic get from the internet to a Pod?", "a": "Client to ALB or NLB created by the AWS Load Balancer Controller, then to the Service or directly to Pod IPs in IP target mode. Health check path, security groups and subnet tags are the usual causes when targets show unhealthy."}, {"k": ["pvc", "volume", "storage", "persistent"], "q": "A StatefulSet Pod cannot mount its volume. How do you debug?", "a": "kubectl describe pvc and pod for events. Typical causes are a wrong StorageClass, EBS volume in a different AZ than the node, or the volume still attached to another node. I check the CSI driver logs if the events are not clear."}], "edge": [{"q": "Your deployment is green, all Pods Ready, but 5% of requests still fail right after every deploy. Why?", "a": "Pods get SIGTERM and are removed from endpoints at the same moment, so in-flight requests or stale kube-proxy rules hit a dying Pod. I add a preStop sleep of 5-10 seconds, set terminationGracePeriodSeconds properly, handle SIGTERM in the app, and use a PodDisruptionBudget.", "fu": [{"q": "How would you prove it is a graceful-shutdown problem?", "a": "I correlate the error timestamps with Pod termination events and see 502/503 only during rollout, not in steady state."}]}, {"q": "A node goes NotReady at night. What do you do and what do you NOT do?", "a": "I check kubectl describe node for conditions like DiskPressure or MemoryPressure and look at kubelet logs. I cordon and drain it so workloads move, and if it is a managed node group I let the ASG replace it. I do not delete the node object blindly or restart kubelet without checking why.", "fu": [{"q": "What if Pods do not reschedule?", "a": "I check PodDisruptionBudgets blocking the drain, local storage, and whether other nodes have capacity."}]}, {"q": "Two teams share one cluster and one team consumes all resources. How do you stop it?", "a": "Namespaces per team with ResourceQuota and LimitRange, plus requests and limits mandatory. PriorityClasses for critical workloads and NetworkPolicies for isolation.", "fu": [{"q": "Would you use separate clusters?", "a": "For strong isolation or compliance yes, otherwise namespaces with quotas are cheaper and easier to run."}]}, {"q": "Image tag is \"latest\" and production runs different versions on different nodes. How did this happen?", "a": "imagePullPolicy IfNotPresent caches old latest on some nodes while new nodes pull the new one. I fix it by using immutable tags such as git SHA or digest and never latest in production.", "fu": []}, {"q": "kubectl apply succeeds but nothing changes in the cluster. What do you check?", "a": "Whether I am pointing at the right context and namespace, whether the manifest actually changed the Pod template, because a ConfigMap change alone does not restart Pods, and whether a GitOps tool like ArgoCD is reverting my change.", "fu": [{"q": "How do you avoid wrong-cluster mistakes?", "a": "Separate kubeconfigs, context shown in the shell prompt, and prod access only through CI/CD or GitOps."}]}], "qa": [{"id": 3, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "You deployed a new microservice in Kubernetes, but other services can't reach it even though the pods are Running. You can curl the pod IP directly but not via the service name. How do you diagnose the connectivity issue?", "a": "Check the Service exists and its selector matches pod labels (kubectl get svc, describe svc). Verify Endpoints are populated (kubectl get endpoints) — empty means selector mismatch. Test DNS from another pod (nslookup <svc>). Confirm targetPort/containerPort align. Check NetworkPolicies aren't blocking traffic and CoreDNS pods are healthy."}, {"id": 6, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "You manage a Kubernetes cluster and notice one node is showing 'NotReady' while its pods are being evicted to other nodes. The application is still serving traffic but at reduced capacity. Walk through how you'd investigate and recover the node.", "a": "Run 'kubectl describe node' to check conditions (MemoryPressure, DiskPressure, kubelet status). SSH to the node and check kubelet: 'systemctl status kubelet' and journalctl logs. Verify disk, network, and container runtime health. Common causes: kubelet crash, disk full, network partition. Restart kubelet if needed, free disk space, or cordon/drain and replace the node. Monitor node returns to Ready."}, {"id": 11, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're rolling out a new version of an app using a Kubernetes Deployment. Midway through the rollout, you realize the new image has a critical bug causing errors, but the old pods are already partially replaced. Users are seeing failures. What do you do to restore service quickly and safely?", "a": "Immediately run 'kubectl rollout undo deployment/<name>' to revert to the previous ReplicaSet. Verify with 'kubectl rollout status' and 'kubectl get pods'. Rollout history is preserved because Deployments keep old ReplicaSets. Then investigate the bad image, fix it, and use readiness probes plus a proper strategy (maxUnavailable/maxSurge) or canary to prevent full impact next time."}, {"id": 18, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your Kubernetes app connects to an external database using credentials stored in a Secret. After a password rotation, new pods work fine but existing pods still fail to authenticate. Why does this happen and how do you resolve it?", "a": "Secrets mounted as env vars are read at pod start, so running pods keep the old value even after the Secret updates. New pods pick up the new value. Restart the existing pods (e.g. 'kubectl rollout restart deployment') to reload credentials. If mounted as a volume, values update but the app may still cache them, so a restart is safest."}, {"id": 22, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "A teammate reports that a pod in your Kubernetes cluster is stuck in 'Pending' state and never gets scheduled. Other pods on the cluster are running fine. How do you find out why it won't schedule?", "a": "Run 'kubectl describe pod' and read the Events section — it usually states the reason: insufficient CPU/memory, no node matching nodeSelector/affinity, unsatisfied taints/tolerations, or unbound PVC. Check node capacity with 'kubectl describe nodes' and resource requests. If it's a PVC issue, verify the StorageClass and PV. Adjust requests, add tolerations, or fix the PVC to allow scheduling."}, {"id": 26, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're deploying an app to Kubernetes and set requests/limits on the container. Shortly after deploy, the pod gets OOMKilled repeatedly and restarts, but memory usage looked fine in your local tests. How do you approach diagnosing and fixing this?", "a": "Check 'kubectl describe pod' for OOMKilled reason and 'kubectl top pod' for actual usage. Compare the memory limit to real consumption under production load (which differs from local). Inspect app for memory leaks or JVM/runtime heap settings not respecting cgroup limits. Fix by raising the memory limit to match observed usage plus headroom, tuning runtime memory flags, and adding monitoring/alerts on memory before OOM."}, {"id": 31, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're deploying an app on Kubernetes and want zero-downtime updates, but during rolling updates some in-flight requests get dropped and users see brief errors. The Deployment strategy is RollingUpdate. How do you configure things to eliminate the dropped requests?", "a": "Add a readinessProbe so traffic only routes to ready pods. Set a preStop hook (e.g. sleep 10s) to let the pod finish in-flight requests and deregister from endpoints before terminating. Ensure terminationGracePeriodSeconds is high enough. Tune maxSurge/maxUnavailable for smoother rollout. This ensures new pods are ready before old ones drain, and connections finish gracefully."}, {"id": 36, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "You maintain a Node.js service in Kubernetes. After deploying, the pods run fine but the liveness probe keeps restarting them every 30 seconds even though the app works when you curl it manually from inside the pod. How do you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Check the probe config: path, port, initialDelaySeconds, timeoutSeconds. Often the app boots slowly so the probe fires before it's ready — increase initialDelaySeconds or add a startupProbe. Confirm the probe hits the right port/path (not a slow endpoint). Verify timeout isn't too short. Use 'kubectl describe pod' to see the failure reason and adjust thresholds accordingly."}, {"id": 41, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're managing an EKS cluster and enabled the Cluster Autoscaler. During a load test, pods stay 'Pending' with 'Insufficient cpu' events, but the Autoscaler never adds new nodes even though your node group has room to grow to a higher max. How do you investigate why scaling isn't happening?", "a": "Check Cluster Autoscaler logs for scale-up decisions and errors. Verify the ASG max size is above current desired. Confirm the node group has the required Auto Discovery tags (k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/enabled). Ensure pod resource requests can actually fit the node instance type. Check IAM permissions for the CA to modify the ASG, and verify no node affinity/taints block scheduling."}, {"id": 46, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're on the platform team and a developer's Kubernetes Deployment sits at 'ImagePullBackOff' after they pushed a new image to a private ECR repo. The image tag definitely exists in ECR. Other apps on the same cluster pull images fine. How do you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Run 'kubectl describe pod' to read the exact pull error. Likely an auth/credential issue specific to this deployment: missing or wrong imagePullSecrets, a node IAM role without ECR permissions for that repo, or a typo in the image URI/tag. Verify the registry URL, region, and digest. Fix by attaching the correct imagePullSecret or ECR pull permissions, then delete the pod to retry."}, {"id": 51, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "A developer's Kubernetes pod needs to write files to a persistent location, but every time the pod restarts, all data is lost. They're using an emptyDir volume. They ask you why data disappears and how to keep it. How do you explain and fix this?", "a": "emptyDir lives only as long as the pod on that node — it's deleted when the pod is removed. For durable data use a PersistentVolumeClaim backed by a StorageClass (e.g. EBS). Create a PVC, mount it in the pod, and data survives restarts/rescheduling. Choose the right access mode (RWO for single node). Confirm the PVC is Bound before relying on it."}, {"id": 59, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "A teammate created a new Kubernetes namespace for a project and deployed pods there, but they say the pods can't reach a database in the 'default' namespace using just the service name 'db'. From the app namespace, curl to 'db' fails but 'db.default' seems to work. Explain what's going on.", "a": "Short DNS names resolve within the pod's own namespace. From another namespace you must use the fully qualified form db.default.svc.cluster.local (or at least db.default). Services aren't global; cross-namespace access needs the namespace-qualified name. Fix by using db.default or the FQDN in the app config."}, {"id": 61, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "You manage a three-node Kubernetes cluster. After a routine upgrade, one worker node shows 'DiskPressure' condition and new pods won't schedule onto it, while existing pods stay put. The other two nodes are fine. How do you investigate and get the node accepting workloads again?", "a": "Check 'kubectl describe node' for the DiskPressure taint and eviction thresholds. SSH in and run 'df -h' plus 'du' on /var/lib/kubelet, /var/lib/docker, and container logs. Common causes: image bloat, orphaned volumes, or unrotated logs. Free space (prune unused images, clean logs), confirm kubelet clears the condition, then verify the taint is removed and pods schedule."}, {"id": 68, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're on call and get paged that a Kubernetes service is returning 503s. Describing the ingress shows the backend service has zero endpoints even though the deployment reports pods as Running. How do you diagnose and restore service?", "a": "Zero endpoints means no Ready pods match the service selector. Check readiness probes are passing (kubectl describe pod), and confirm the service selector labels match the pod labels. Recent label/selector change or a failing readiness probe is common. Fix the probe or align labels; endpoints repopulate and traffic flows again."}, {"id": 74, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your team runs a service on EKS and exposes it via a LoadBalancer Service. The AWS ELB was created but marked unhealthy, so traffic never reaches the pods, though the pods are Running and pass local curl checks. How do you investigate?", "a": "Check the ELB target group health checks — they often hit '/' on the node port; ensure the health check path/port matches an endpoint the app returns 200 on. Verify the Service's targetPort maps to the container port, security groups allow the health-check traffic, and readiness probes pass. Fix mismatched health-check path or SG rules."}, {"id": 76, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your team runs a Kubernetes CronJob that generates nightly reports, but you notice the same report is being generated twice some nights. Checking the cluster, you see two pods spawned for the same scheduled time. What's likely causing duplicate runs and how do you make the CronJob run exactly once per schedule?", "a": "Likely the previous job overran and the concurrencyPolicy default (Allow) let overlapping jobs run, or a missed schedule triggered catch-up runs. Set concurrencyPolicy: Forbid to skip if a prior run is active, tune startingDeadlineSeconds to avoid backfill bursts, and set successfulJobsHistoryLimit. Ensure the job itself is idempotent and completes before the next schedule."}, {"id": 82, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your team runs a stateless API on Kubernetes with a single replica. During node maintenance the pod was evicted and the API went down for two minutes. Product wants no downtime during routine node drains. What changes would you make?", "a": "Increase replicas to 2+ so drains don't take the app fully down. Add a PodDisruptionBudget (minAvailable) to limit voluntary disruptions. Use anti-affinity or topologySpreadConstraints to place replicas on different nodes. Ensure readiness probes so traffic only hits ready pods, and a Service/Ingress that load-balances across replicas."}, {"id": 86, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A teammate asks you to give a new developer read-only access to a specific Kubernetes namespace so they can view pods and logs but not change anything. How would you set this up?", "a": "Create a Role scoped to the namespace granting get/list/watch on pods and pods/log (and maybe services, deployments). Bind it to the user or group with a RoleBinding. Avoid ClusterRole/ClusterRoleBinding since that would grant access cluster-wide. Verify with 'kubectl auth can-i get pods --as user -n ns'. This follows least privilege."}, {"id": 91, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "General", "q": "You joined a team and notice their Kubernetes deployment sets no resource requests or limits on any container. One noisy pod recently starved others on the same node, causing evictions. A senior asks you to make workloads behave predictably. What would you configure and why?", "a": "Set CPU/memory requests so the scheduler places pods sensibly and reserves capacity, and set limits to cap runaway usage. Requests guarantee baseline resources; limits prevent one pod from starving neighbors. For memory, limit=request avoids OOM surprises; for CPU, use requests plus reasonable limits. Optionally add a LimitRange/ResourceQuota in the namespace to enforce defaults."}, {"id": 96, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After a recent Kubernetes deployment, a rolling update left half the pods on the old image and half on the new one, stuck for over 10 minutes. kubectl rollout status never completes. The new pods are in CrashLoopBackOff. Product is asking why the update is 'half done.' How do you diagnose and safely recover?", "a": "Rolling updates only proceed as old pods are replaced by healthy new ones; the new pods crashing blocks progress while maxUnavailable/maxSurge keep old ones serving. Check kubectl describe pod and logs for the crash cause (bad config, missing env/secret, migration). Fix the root cause and redeploy, or run kubectl rollout undo to revert to the last stable ReplicaSet immediately, then investigate offline."}, {"id": 101, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your team asks you to give a container running in Kubernetes access to an AWS S3 bucket. A senior warns you against baking AWS access keys into the pod's environment variables. What's the recommended way to give the pod AWS permissions on EKS?", "a": "Use IAM Roles for Service Accounts (IRSA). Create an IAM role with an S3 policy and a trust relationship to the cluster's OIDC provider, associate it with a Kubernetes ServiceAccount, and set that ServiceAccount on the pod. The pod then gets short-lived credentials via the web identity token—no static keys to leak or rotate."}, {"id": 106, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "You're a mid-level engineer running an EKS cluster. After scaling a node group down to save cost overnight, several pods stay in 'Pending' the next morning even though nodes came back up. The Cluster Autoscaler logs show 'no scale up' decisions. Product needs the app back. How do you diagnose why pods won't schedule and get them running?", "a": "Run 'kubectl describe pod' to read the scheduling failure reason (insufficient CPU/memory, node selector/affinity, taints, PVC zone mismatch). Check node capacity vs pod requests and any nodeSelector/affinity that no restored node satisfies. Verify Cluster Autoscaler can scale the node group (max size, ASG limits, IAM). Fix by adjusting requests, tolerations/affinity, or raising node group max, then confirm pods schedule."}, {"id": 112, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague pushed a change that added a new environment variable to a Kubernetes Deployment, but running pods still show the old value. They ran 'kubectl apply' and it said 'configured'. Why might the pods not reflect the change, and how do you confirm the rollout?", "a": "If the env var comes from a ConfigMap/Secret referenced by envFrom, editing that doesn't restart pods automatically. If it's inline in the Deployment spec, apply should trigger a rollout. Check 'kubectl rollout status deploy/x' and 'kubectl get pods' for new pod ages. For ConfigMap changes, trigger a restart with 'kubectl rollout restart deployment/x' so pods pick up new values."}, {"id": 116, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer says their Kubernetes pod is stuck in 'ImagePullBackOff' after you deployed a new version. The image tag was updated in the Deployment yaml and pushed to a private registry. Existing pods on the old tag still run fine. How do you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Run 'kubectl describe pod' to see the exact pull error. Common causes: wrong image tag/name, image not pushed yet, or missing/incorrect imagePullSecrets. Verify the image exists in the registry and the tag is correct. Ensure the Deployment references a valid imagePullSecret linked to the service account. Fix the tag or secret, then let the rollout retry."}, {"id": 124, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After a Kubernetes deployment, two services that need to talk to each other over the cluster network started failing intermittently, while pods within the same service communicate fine. Nothing changed in the app code — only a NetworkPolicy was added last week. How do you diagnose this?", "a": "NetworkPolicies are default-deny once applied to a namespace/pod. Adding one likely blocked cross-service traffic that was previously allowed. Run 'kubectl get networkpolicy' and inspect podSelectors and ingress/egress rules. Test connectivity with 'kubectl exec' + curl/nc between pods. Confirm the CNI enforces policies (Calico/Cilium). Fix by adding ingress rules allowing the required source pods/ports, then verify with a test connection."}, {"id": 126, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A teammate deleted a Kubernetes Deployment by mistake in production, and the app is now down. There are no backups of the YAML, but the cluster has been running for months. How would you recover the Deployment quickly without guessing the config?", "a": "Check if it's truly gone or just the Deployment object. Look at any ReplicaSets/pods still running, or use kubectl get events. Best source: your Git repo / GitOps (Argo, Flux) which holds the manifest — re-apply from there. If not in Git, check kubectl audit logs or a recent kubectl get deploy -o yaml output. Then re-apply and confirm rollout. Add GitOps/backups so state is version-controlled."}, {"id": 129, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After enabling autoscaling on an ECS/EKS workload, the app scales out under load but never scales back in during quiet periods, keeping costs high. Scale-out works fine. How would you diagnose why scale-in isn't happening?", "a": "Check the scaling policy's scale-in cooldown and metrics — CPU/memory may not drop below threshold if requests/limits are misconfigured or a metric floor stays high. For HPA, check kubectl describe hpa for current vs target and stabilization window. Verify there's no PodDisruptionBudget or minReplicas blocking it, and no lingering long-lived connections keeping utilization up. Tune target values and scale-in stabilization."}, {"id": 131, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague added a new volume mount to a Kubernetes pod so it can read config files, but the pod is now stuck in 'ContainerCreating' and never becomes Ready. The image pulls fine and other pods on the same node run normally. How do you diagnose why the pod won't start?", "a": "Run 'kubectl describe pod <name>' and read the Events section — mount failures show up there (FailedMount, timeout). Common causes: a referenced ConfigMap/Secret/PVC doesn't exist, wrong name, or a PVC still Pending because no matching PV/StorageClass. Check 'kubectl get pvc/configmap/secret' to confirm the source exists in the same namespace. Fix the missing/misnamed resource, then the mount succeeds and the container starts."}, {"id": 136, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer complains that their Kubernetes pod can reach the internet but cannot resolve the name of another service in the same cluster (e.g. 'my-svc.default.svc.cluster.local'). Other pods resolve it fine. How do you diagnose why this one pod can't resolve internal service names?", "a": "Check the pod's /etc/resolv.conf for the correct cluster DNS nameserver and search domains. Verify dnsPolicy isn't set to 'Default' (which uses node DNS, skipping cluster DNS). Exec into the pod and run nslookup against kube-dns. Check CoreDNS pods are healthy and the ClusterIP of the target Service exists. Fix by setting dnsPolicy: ClusterFirst."}, {"id": 146, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer reports that their Kubernetes pod stays in 'ImagePullBackOff' after deploying a new private image from your company's registry. The image tag is correct and other pods using public images run fine on the same cluster. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Run kubectl describe pod to read the exact pull error (usually 'unauthorized' or 'manifest unknown'). For a private registry, the node needs credentials via an imagePullSecret. Create one with kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred ... and reference it in the pod spec's imagePullSecrets (or attach to the ServiceAccount). Also verify the image path/tag actually exists in the registry."}, {"id": 151, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer asks you to add a new environment variable to a running Kubernetes Deployment without editing YAML files locally, so it takes effect on the next rollout. They also want to see the change reflected immediately. How would you do this?", "a": "Use kubectl set env deployment/myapp KEY=value, which patches the Deployment spec and triggers a rolling update automatically. Verify with kubectl describe deployment myapp or kubectl set env deployment/myapp --list. Confirm the new pods have it via kubectl exec and env | grep KEY. For persistent/GitOps setups, also update the manifest in the repo so the change isn't lost on the next apply."}, {"id": 156, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer wants to run a one-off maintenance task in your Kubernetes cluster that should execute once, run to completion, and not restart afterward. They tried using a Deployment but the pod keeps restarting after finishing. What Kubernetes resource should they use instead, and how would you set it up?", "a": "Use a Job (or CronJob for scheduled runs), not a Deployment. A Deployment always keeps pods running and restarts completed ones. A Job runs pods to completion and stops. Create it with kubectl create job, set restartPolicy to Never or OnFailure, and use backoffLimit to cap retries. Check status with kubectl get jobs and logs with kubectl logs job/<name>."}, {"id": 161, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer asks you to list all pods across every namespace in your Kubernetes cluster that are currently NOT in the 'Running' state, so they can quickly spot problems during a triage. They only know how to run kubectl get pods, which shows one namespace at a time. How would you get a cluster-wide view and filter out healthy pods?", "a": "Use kubectl get pods --all-namespaces (or -A) for a cluster-wide view. To filter unhealthy pods add a field selector: kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase!=Running. Note this misses CrashLoopBackOff (phase is Running), so also scan STATUS/RESTARTS columns, or pipe through grep -v Running. Combine with kubectl get pods -A -o wide to see nodes for pattern-spotting."}, {"id": 162, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team runs microservices on EKS. A new service can reach the internet and other services, but you want to lock it down so it can only receive traffic from the frontend service and only talk to the database service — nothing else. Currently all pods can talk to all pods. How would you enforce this restriction?", "a": "Use Kubernetes NetworkPolicies (ensure your CNI, e.g. Calico/Cilium, supports them). Create an ingress policy on the new service selecting its pods, allowing traffic only from the frontend's pod labels. Add an egress policy allowing traffic only to the DB pods/port plus DNS (kube-dns). Apply a default-deny policy in the namespace first so unlisted traffic is blocked. Test with connectivity checks after applying."}, {"id": 167, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your Kubernetes HorizontalPodAutoscaler isn't scaling your Deployment up under heavy load. kubectl get hpa shows the target metric as <unknown>. Pods are clearly maxing CPU. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "<unknown> means the HPA can't read metrics. Check that metrics-server is installed and running (kubectl top pods should work). Ensure the Deployment's containers define resources.requests.cpu, since CPU-based HPA scales relative to requests — without them the percentage can't be computed. Verify the HPA targets the correct Deployment and API version. Fix by deploying metrics-server and adding resource requests."}, {"id": 171, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer reports that their Kubernetes pod is stuck in 'CrashLoopBackOff' right after deployment. The image pulls fine and the container starts, but it exits within seconds and Kubernetes keeps restarting it. How would you diagnose the root cause?", "a": "Run kubectl describe pod to check events and exit code, then kubectl logs <pod> --previous to see output from the crashed container. Common causes: bad startup command, missing env vars/config, failing dependency connection, or a failing readiness/liveness probe killing it. Fix the underlying error (config, command, or probe timing) and redeploy."}, {"id": 176, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer reports that after deploying a new version, their Kubernetes Service still routes traffic to old, terminated pods, causing intermittent connection refused errors. The Deployment rolled out fine and new pods are Running. How would you diagnose why the Service isn't picking up only the healthy new pods?", "a": "Check the Service selector matches the pod labels (kubectl describe svc). Inspect endpoints with kubectl get endpoints <svc> to see which pods it targets. Terminated pods stuck in Terminating or mismatched labels cause stale endpoints. Verify readiness probes so only ready pods receive traffic, and confirm graceful termination (preStop/terminationGracePeriod) so old pods drain before removal."}, {"id": 181, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer says their pod can't reach a database running as a Kubernetes Service in another namespace. They're using just the service name db in their app config, and it works when both are in the same namespace but fails across namespaces. How would you diagnose and fix the connection?", "a": "Cross-namespace access needs the fully-qualified DNS name, not the short name. Use db.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local (or at least db.<namespace>). The short name only resolves within the same namespace via the search domain. Verify with kubectl exec and nslookup db.<namespace>. Also confirm the Service exists, has endpoints (kubectl get endpoints), and no NetworkPolicy blocks cross-namespace traffic. Update the app config with the FQD…"}, {"id": 186, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer reports that their pod was scheduled onto a node but is stuck in 'Pending' with an event message about 'insufficient memory'. Other smaller pods schedule fine on the same cluster. How would you diagnose and get this pod running?", "a": "Run kubectl describe pod and read the Events — it shows the scheduler couldn't find a node meeting resource requests. Compare the pod's requests.memory against node allocatable (kubectl describe node). Fixes: lower the requests if over-provisioned, add/scale nodes, or free capacity by evicting/rightsizing other pods. Check for taints or reserved capacity too."}, {"id": 191, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer reports their Kubernetes app reads config from a ConfigMap, but after they updated the ConfigMap the running pods still show the old values even though other new pods pick up the change. How would you get the running pods to use the updated config?", "a": "ConfigMaps mounted as env vars are only read at pod startup — they don't update live. Mounted volumes update but the app must re-read the file. Fix: trigger a rollout with kubectl rollout restart deployment/<name> so pods restart and pick up new values. For env-var configs, a restart is required. Consider tools like Reloader to automate restarts on ConfigMap change."}, {"id": 196, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer reports that their Kubernetes Deployment shows 3 desired replicas but only 1 pod is Running while the other 2 are stuck in 'Pending'. The cluster has other nodes with capacity, but a kubectl describe pod on a Pending pod shows a message about a node taint. Nothing was changed intentionally. How would you diagnose and get the pods scheduled?", "a": "Run kubectl describe pod and read the Events — the taint message means nodes have taints (e.g. NoSchedule) the pod doesn't tolerate. Check kubectl describe node for taints. Fix by either adding a matching tolerations block to the pod spec, or removing the taint via kubectl taint nodes <node> key-. Verify remaining nodes actually have capacity and correct nodeSelector/affinity too."}, {"id": 201, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer pushed a new Docker image tagged 'latest' to your registry, but when they redeploy the Kubernetes pod, it keeps running the old code. The Deployment YAML uses image 'myapp:latest' and imagePullPolicy isn't set. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "With tag 'latest' and default imagePullPolicy (IfNotPresent when tag isn't 'latest'—actually 'latest' defaults to Always, but if a specific tag or node already caches it, staleness occurs). Best fix: use immutable tags (git SHA/version) instead of mutable 'latest'. Set imagePullPolicy: Always as a stopgap. Trigger a rollout with kubectl rollout restart. Immutable tags prevent this permanently."}, {"id": 206, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A teammate's kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml succeeds, but when they check kubectl get pods they see zero pods for that deployment even though kubectl get deployment shows it exists with 0/0 ready. No pods are Pending or in any error state — none exist at all. How would you diagnose why no pods were created?", "a": "Run kubectl describe deployment <name> and check the ReplicaSet events. Likely replicas: 0 in the YAML, a paused rollout, or a selector/label mismatch so the ReplicaSet can't own pods. Also check kubectl get rs and its events for quota or admission-webhook rejections. Fix by setting replicas correctly, aligning spec.selector.matchLabels with template.metadata.labels, and re-applying."}, {"id": 211, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer reports that a Kubernetes pod is stuck in 'ImagePullBackOff' after deploying. The image name and tag are correct, and the same image runs fine on their laptop. The image lives in a private registry. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Run kubectl describe pod to read the exact pull error (auth denied vs not found). ImagePullBackOff with a private registry usually means the cluster lacks credentials. Create a docker-registry secret: kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred --docker-server=... --docker-username=... --docker-password=..., then reference it under imagePullSecrets in the pod/deployment spec. Verify the node can reach the registry network too."}, {"id": 212, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "During a deploy you need to safely roll back a Kubernetes Deployment to the previous version because the new pods are serving errors. The team lead wants it done fast without editing YAML. How would you do it and confirm it worked?", "a": "Use kubectl rollout undo deployment/<name> to revert to the previous revision (or --to-revision=N after checking kubectl rollout history). Watch progress with kubectl rollout status deployment/<name> and confirm with kubectl get pods that new pods are Running and old-bad ones are gone. This works because Deployments retain ReplicaSet revision history."}, {"id": 217, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team's EKS cluster costs are climbing. You notice many nodes run at ~20% CPU/memory utilization, yet the cluster autoscaler isn't scaling down. Pods are spread thinly across nodes. How would you diagnose and reduce the wasted capacity?", "a": "Nodes likely can't be drained because pods lack disruption tolerance or block eviction — check for pods without controllers, PodDisruptionBudgets that are too strict, local storage/emptyDir pods, or pods without proper resource requests causing poor bin-packing. Fix by setting sensible requests, adding PDBs that allow eviction, using descheduler for consolidation, and enabling autoscaler scale-down (or Karpenter consolidation) to bin-pack work…"}, {"id": 221, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your team asks you to give a new contractor read-only access to view logs and pods in a single Kubernetes namespace called 'app-dev', but they must not be able to modify anything or see other namespaces. How would you set this up?", "a": "Create a Role (namespaced, not ClusterRole) in 'app-dev' granting get/list/watch on pods and pods/log. Bind it to the contractor's user or group with a RoleBinding in that namespace. RoleBindings scope access to only that namespace, so they can't touch others or write anything. Verify with kubectl auth can-i --as=user get pods -n app-dev and confirm they can't delete or access other namespaces."}, {"id": 226, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "After deploying to a Kubernetes cluster, users report the app is up but you can't reach it via the LoadBalancer Service's external IP — the connection just hangs. The pods are Running and healthy, and kubectl port-forward to a pod works fine. How would you diagnose why the LoadBalancer isn't routing traffic?", "a": "Run kubectl get svc to confirm an external IP was assigned (not <pending>). Check the Service selector matches pod labels and that kubectl get endpoints <svc> lists pod IPs — empty means selector mismatch. Verify targetPort matches the container's listening port. Then check cloud security groups/firewall rules allow the Service's port. Fix the selector/port mismatch or open the firewall."}, {"id": 232, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your Kubernetes cluster's nodes intermittently go 'NotReady' for a few minutes then recover. Pods on those nodes get evicted and rescheduled, causing brief outages. Node CPU/memory look normal in metrics. How would you diagnose and stabilize this?", "a": "Check kubelet health and node conditions via kubectl describe node (look for DiskPressure, PIDPressure, network issues). Inspect kubelet and container runtime logs (journalctl) around the NotReady windows. Common causes: kubelet losing contact with API server (network/DNS flaps), disk I/O pressure, or kubelet resource starvation. Reserve resources with kube/system-reserved, fix networking, tune node-monitor-grace-period, and add PodDisruptio…"}, {"id": 237, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your Kubernetes HorizontalPodAutoscaler shows 'unknown' for the current CPU metric and never scales your Deployment, even under load. The pods are Running and serving traffic. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "HPA needs metrics-server and resource requests defined. Check kubectl top pods — if it fails, metrics-server is missing/misconfigured (fix TLS/kubelet flags). Then verify the Deployment's containers set resources.requests.cpu; without requests, HPA can't compute utilization. kubectl describe hpa shows the error. Add requests, ensure metrics-server healthy, then re-check."}, {"id": 241, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "After enabling a new liveness probe on a Kubernetes Deployment, pods start crash-looping — they get killed and restarted every couple of minutes even though the app appears to work when you exec in and curl the endpoint manually. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Run kubectl describe pod and check the events for 'Liveness probe failed' details (status code, timeout). Common causes: probe path/port wrong, initialDelaySeconds too short so the app isn't ready yet, or timeoutSeconds too low for a slow endpoint. Compare your manual curl (from inside) with the probe config. Fix by correcting path/port, increasing initialDelaySeconds/timeoutSeconds/failureThreshold, and using a lightweight health endpoint."}, {"id": 246, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer reports that their application container keeps restarting with exit code 137 shortly after startup, even though it runs fine on their laptop. The Kubernetes Deployment sets a memory limit of 128Mi. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Exit code 137 = SIGKILL from OOMKilled. Check kubectl describe pod for 'OOMKilled' and kubectl get pod -o yaml for lastState. The 128Mi limit is too low for the app's actual usage. Measure real usage via kubectl top pod or metrics, then raise the memory limit/request to a safe value (and tune the app's heap/memory settings). Set requests and limits appropriately so the scheduler reserves enough memory."}, {"id": 252, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your Kubernetes cluster's Ingress controller returns 404 for a newly added service, while existing services work fine. The Ingress resource, Service, and pods all look correct and pods are Running. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Check the Ingress's host/path rules and that the backend serviceName/port exactly match the Service. Verify the Service's selector matches pod labels and has endpoints (kubectl get endpoints). Confirm the Ingress uses the correct ingressClassName. Check controller logs and that the Ingress is in the same namespace as the Service. Validate path type (Prefix vs Exact)."}, {"id": 256, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer reports that their CronJob in Kubernetes was supposed to run hourly to clean up temp data, but checking the cluster you find dozens of completed and failed Jobs piling up, and some runs seem to overlap. The cleanup task is slow and sometimes takes longer than an hour. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Check kubectl get jobs and kubectl get cronjob -o yaml. Overlap happens because concurrencyPolicy defaults to Allow. Set concurrencyPolicy: Forbid (or Replace) so slow runs don't overlap. Set startingDeadlineSeconds to handle missed schedules. Pileup is because completed Jobs aren't cleaned—set successfulJobsHistoryLimit and failedJobsHistoryLimit (e.g. 3/1) so old Jobs are garbage-collected automatically."}, {"id": 258, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Users report your app returns intermittent 503 errors. A senior asks you to check whether your containerized app is hitting its configured resource limits and getting throttled in Kubernetes. How would you investigate?", "a": "Run kubectl top pods to see live CPU/memory usage versus requests/limits. Check kubectl describe pod for restarts, OOMKilled events, and throttling. For CPU throttling inspect container cgroup metrics via Prometheus (container_cpu_cfs_throttled_seconds_total) or metrics-server. If hitting memory limit it gets OOMKilled (exit 137); if CPU-limited it's throttled. Fix by raising limits/requests or optimizing the app."}, {"id": 263, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A teammate asks you to confirm whether a recently deployed pod is actually receiving traffic from its Service, since users report inconsistent behavior. The Service and pods are Running. How would you verify the Service is routing to the correct pods?", "a": "Check kubectl get endpoints <svc> — it should list the pod IPs. If empty, the Service selector labels don't match the pod labels. Compare kubectl get svc <svc> -o yaml selectors with pod labels. Also verify targetPort matches the container port. Test with kubectl run tmp --rm -it --image=busybox -- wget -qO- <svc>:<port> from inside the cluster."}, {"id": 266, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer reports their Kubernetes pod is stuck in 'Pending' state after deployment. No pods are running for the new deployment, and describing the pod shows an event about scheduling. How would you diagnose why the pod won't schedule?", "a": "Run kubectl describe pod <name> and read the Events. Common causes: insufficient CPU/memory on nodes (check kubectl top nodes), node selectors/affinity not matching any node, taints without matching tolerations, or no available PVC for a required volume. Check kubectl get nodes for Ready status and resource requests in the spec. Fix by adjusting requests, adding capacity, or correcting affinity/tolerations."}, {"id": 272, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A mid-level engineer notices that an EKS cluster is reporting 'Node pressure' and pods are being evicted. The node capacity hasn't changed, but a recent update added a sidecar container to every pod. How do you investigate if this is the cause?", "a": "Use 'kubectl top nodes' and 'kubectl top pods' to identify resource consumption. Inspect the deployment manifests to check the 'resources.requests' and 'limits' for the new sidecar. If the sum of requests across all containers in a pod exceeds the available node capacity, the scheduler will struggle, and if actual usage exceeds limits, Kubelet will evict pods. Adjust resource requests to accurately reflect actual usage."}, {"id": 278, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "You discover that a Kubernetes Deployment's rolling update strategy is causing downtime because the app takes 30 seconds to initialize before it can serve traffic. How do you fix this using Kubernetes native features?", "a": "I would implement a 'readinessProbe' in the Deployment manifest. This ensures that the Service load balancer does not send traffic to the pod until it reports 'Ready'. I would also tune the 'initialDelaySeconds' or 'periodSeconds' parameters in the probe to match the application's actual startup time, preventing the traffic shift until the app is fully initialized and warmed up."}, {"id": 283, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "You are a mid-level engineer. A Kubernetes deployment is failing, and the pods are in 'CrashLoopBackOff'. The logs disappear too quickly to read. How do you investigate the issue without changing the application code?", "a": "Use 'kubectl describe pod <name>' to check the events and exit code. Use 'kubectl logs <name> --previous' to view the output from the last failed attempt. If the pod crashes instantly, update the deployment's 'command' in the spec to a sleep command like 'sleep 3600', then exec into the container to manually run the entrypoint script and observe the error."}, {"id": 287, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A database migration job in your Kubernetes cluster is failing because it exceeds the allowed memory. You cannot change the code. How do you address this without causing downtime for the primary application?", "a": "Verify the current memory limits using 'kubectl describe pod'. Use 'kubectl edit' or apply an updated manifest to increase the 'resources.limits.memory' for the job pod. Since it is a migration job (likely a Job or InitContainer), increasing resources won't impact the main application pods. Ensure you monitor the node capacity to prevent OOM kills on other pods."}, {"id": 294, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A Kubernetes Service is failing to route traffic to your pods, despite the pods being 'Running'. How do you verify if the endpoints are correctly registered and mapped to the service?", "a": "Run 'kubectl get endpoints <service-name>' to verify that the service has identified the pod IPs. If the list is empty, check the Service selector labels against the pod labels using 'kubectl get pods --show-labels'. If the endpoints are correct, check the port definitions in the Service manifest to ensure 'targetPort' matches the application container's listening port."}, {"id": 299, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A Prometheus alert indicates your Kubernetes nodes are reporting 'DiskPressure'. You need to find which pods are consuming the most local ephemeral storage to remediate this without simply deleting pods.", "a": "Use 'kubectl get pods --all-namespaces' to identify pods, then 'kubectl describe pod <name>' to check if logs or emptyDir volumes are filling up. You can also use 'kubectl exec' to run 'df -h' inside suspicious pods. If logs are the culprit, implement log rotation or adjust the logging driver to prevent filling the node's disk."}, {"id": 304, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A K8s rolling update is failing because pods are failing health checks. You notice the pods are stuck in 'ContainerCreating'. How do you isolate the root cause?", "a": "Use 'kubectl describe pod <pod-name>' to check the 'Events' section for ImagePullBackOff or mounting errors. If the image is fine, check the nodes for available resources. If the pods are failing post-start, check the 'Liveness' probe definition to see if it is too aggressive for the application's startup time. Use 'kubectl logs' with '--previous' to capture logs from the container before it crashed."}, {"id": 310, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A Kubernetes CronJob is failing to complete within the expected window. How do you inspect the logs of a failed job run that has already been cleaned up by the cluster?", "a": "Check if the Job's 'successfulJobsHistoryLimit' or 'failedJobsHistoryLimit' prevented retention. If the pod is gone, review the event logs using 'kubectl get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp' to find the reason for failure. If logs are still needed, ensure that logs are being forwarded to a centralized aggregator like Fluentd, CloudWatch, or ELK, as those are the only sources for logs once the pod object is garbage collected."}, {"id": 313, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A Kubernetes Pod is stuck in 'ImagePullBackOff'. You have verified the image name is correct. How do you investigate if the cluster has the necessary pull credentials?", "a": "Use 'kubectl describe pod <name>' to verify the exact error message. If it indicates an authentication failure, check if the ServiceAccount has an 'imagePullSecrets' entry configured. You can then run 'kubectl get secret <secret_name> -o yaml' to verify the secret content is valid and present in the correct namespace."}, {"id": 320, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A Kubernetes Pod is frequently restarting with an 'OOMKilled' error. You have a memory limit set. How do you verify if the application is actually exceeding the limit versus leaking memory?", "a": "Inspect the pod status with 'kubectl describe pod <name>' to see the 'Last State' exit reason. Use 'kubectl top pod <name>' to monitor real-time memory usage. Check the application logs for 'Out of Memory' stack traces. If the limit is hit, use 'cAdvisor' or 'Prometheus' metrics to view the memory usage trend over time. If the trend is a constant slope upwards, it is a memory leak; if it spikes sharply, investigate recent request volumes or sp…"}, {"id": 322, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A Prometheus alert indicates a Kubernetes node has reached its max capacity for persistent volumes. How do you identify which specific Pod is bound to which PV before attempting to resize or detach storage?", "a": "Use 'kubectl get pvc -A' to list all claims and their statuses. Use 'kubectl describe pvc <name>' to find the 'Used by' section, which identifies the specific Pod utilizing the volume. Correlate this with 'kubectl get pv' to confirm the actual physical storage underlying the claim before performing any destructive volume changes."}, {"id": 326, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Notice a Kubernetes deployment scaling up, but the new pods stay in 'Pending'. Describe how you would determine if this is a resource constraint issue versus a scheduling requirement issue.", "a": "First, run 'kubectl describe pod <pod-name>'. If the events show 'FailedScheduling' with 'Insufficient cpu/memory', it is a resource constraint. If it shows 'NodeAffinity' or 'Tolerations' failures, it is a scheduling requirement. Check node resources using 'kubectl top nodes' and inspect the pod's node selector or affinity rules against available nodes."}, {"id": 332, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A Kubernetes Pod is failing its readiness probe. How do you identify if the failure is due to application code latency or an underlying node resource issue?", "a": "Use 'kubectl describe pod' to view events and probe failures. Check 'kubectl top node' to assess overall node pressure. If node metrics are healthy, use 'kubectl logs' and 'kubectl exec' to test connectivity from within the pod. Check the application metrics for latency spikes to determine if the process is unresponsive under load."}, {"id": 337, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A mid-level engineer observes that a Kubernetes node's memory usage is high, but the total sum of requests from pods is low. How do you find which process, possibly outside Kubernetes, is consuming the memory?", "a": "Use 'kubectl debug node/<node-name>' to access the host shell. Then run 'top' or 'htop' sorted by memory usage. Check for non-containerized system processes or 'zombie' processes that may have leaked outside the container runtime control groups."}, {"id": 341, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A junior engineer notices that a new Kubernetes service is deployed but its ClusterIP is not reachable from another pod. What command-line steps should you take to verify if the service is correctly associated with an endpoint without assuming it's a network policy issue?", "a": "First, run 'kubectl describe svc <service-name>' to check the 'Endpoints' field. If it is <none>, the selector is misaligned with pod labels. Then, use 'kubectl get ep <service-name>' to confirm IPs are listed. If endpoints exist, use 'kubectl exec' to run 'curl' or 'nc' from a source pod to verify connectivity to the ClusterIP directly. If the endpoints are empty, fix the label match before checking firewall rules."}, {"id": 346, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "After a deploy, your Kubernetes service returns 503 errors intermittently. kubectl get pods shows all pods Running and Ready, but some requests still fail. A senior mentions checking the Service's endpoints. How would you diagnose whether traffic is reaching all pods correctly?", "a": "Run kubectl get endpoints <svc> and compare the IPs to kubectl get pods -o wide. Missing endpoints mean a pod isn't Ready or the Service selector doesn't match pod labels. Check readiness probe status and label selectors. If a pod flaps Ready/NotReady it's dropped from endpoints mid-request. Fix probe thresholds/timeouts and ensure selectors match so all healthy pods receive traffic."}, {"id": 351, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team's Kubernetes HorizontalPodAutoscaler isn't scaling up under heavy load — kubectl get hpa shows the target metric as '<unknown>' instead of a percentage. Pods are clearly maxed out. How would you diagnose why the HPA can't read metrics?", "a": "'<unknown>' means the HPA can't fetch metrics. Check that metrics-server is installed and running (kubectl top pods should work). Verify the pods define resource requests — CPU utilization HPAs need requests to compute a percentage. Check metrics-server logs, its --kubelet-insecure-tls/address flags, and API availability (kubectl get apiservice v1beta1.metrics.k8s.io). Fix missing requests or restore metrics-server, then the HPA populate…"}, {"id": 359, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your EKS cluster's pods intermittently fail to reach the AWS API and other pods report 'connection refused' during high pod churn. You suspect you've run out of IP addresses. Using the AWS VPC CNI, how would you diagnose and address IP exhaustion?", "a": "The VPC CNI assigns pod IPs from the subnet; nodes have a max ENI/IP limit per instance type. Check subnet free IPs, node's allocatable pods, and CNI logs for 'failed to assign IP'. Fix: use larger subnets/secondary CIDRs, enable prefix delegation to pack more IPs per ENI, choose instance types allowing more ENIs, or right-size node counts. Monitor available IPs via CloudWatch."}, {"id": 365, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After a Kubernetes node dies unexpectedly, its pods took over 5 minutes to reschedule onto healthy nodes, causing an outage longer than expected. Product wants faster recovery. How would you reduce the failover time for pods on a dead node?", "a": "The delay is the node-monitor-grace-period plus the default 5-min pod-eviction toleration. Kubernetes adds tolerations for node.kubernetes.io/not-ready and unreachable with tolerationSeconds=300. Lower tolerationSeconds on critical Deployments, tune kubelet/controller grace periods, and ensure enough spare capacity/PodDisruptionBudgets so rescheduled pods can place quickly."}, {"id": 366, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer says their newly deployed pod keeps getting OOMKilled and restarting, even though the same app runs fine on their laptop with plenty of RAM available. kubectl describe pod shows 'OOMKilled' with exit code 137. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Check the container's resource limits with kubectl describe pod — a low memory limit causes the kernel to kill the process even if the node has free RAM. Compare actual usage (kubectl top pod) against the limit. Fix by raising memory limits/requests to realistic values based on observed usage, or optimize the app's memory footprint. Set requests near steady-state and limits with headroom."}, {"id": 374, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "During a production incident your Kubernetes app is returning errors and you suspect a recent config change. You need to quickly see what changed and roll back a Deployment to the previous working version without a redeploy from CI. How would you do this?", "a": "Use kubectl rollout history deployment/<name> to see revisions and --revision=N for details. Roll back with kubectl rollout undo deployment/<name> (or --to-revision=N). Confirm with kubectl rollout status. Note rollout history only tracks pod template changes; ConfigMap/Secret changes aren't captured, so check those separately."}, {"id": 377, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your Kubernetes Ingress with an nginx controller returns 404 for a newly added path, while existing paths on the same host work fine. The backend Service and pods for the new path are healthy and reachable via port-forward. How would you diagnose why the Ingress rule isn't routing?", "a": "Inspect the Ingress with kubectl describe ingress and confirm the new path/host rule and pathType (Prefix vs Exact) are correct. Check the Service name/port referenced actually match. Review nginx controller logs and the generated nginx.conf for the route. Verify the Ingress class annotation and that the controller picked up the update. Test with correct Host header via curl."}, {"id": 379, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "During a deploy your rolling update stalls: kubectl rollout status hangs, and kubectl get pods shows new pods stuck in Pending while old ones stay Running. The cluster seemed fine before. How would you diagnose why the new pods can't schedule?", "a": "Run kubectl describe pod on a Pending pod and read the Events — usually it shows insufficient CPU/memory, node selectors/affinity/taints not satisfied, or unbound PVCs. Check node capacity with kubectl top nodes / describe nodes. If resource requests are too high or cluster is full, adjust requests, scale the node group, or fix scheduling constraints so the ReplicaSet can place pods."}, {"id": 381, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer created a new Kubernetes Secret with a database password and mounted it as an environment variable, but the pod shows the value as garbled text instead of the actual password. They created the secret using kubectl create secret generic db-secret --from-literal, but then edited the secret YAML by hand and pasted the plain password into the data: field. Why is the value garbled and how do you fix it?", "a": "Under data:, Secret values must be base64-encoded; pasting a plain password there makes Kubernetes decode invalid base64 into garbage. Either put the plain value under stringData: (Kubernetes encodes it), or base64-encode it yourself (echo -n 'pass' | base64) before placing it under data:. Recreate/apply the secret and restart the pod so it picks up the corrected value."}, {"id": 389, "cat": "Kubernetes", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your Kubernetes cluster runs fine, but after a node's kubelet certificate expired, that node went NotReady and its pods became unavailable. Product asks how to prevent silent expiry outages. How would you detect and prevent kubelet/cluster certificate expiry proactively?", "a": "Enable kubelet certificate rotation (--rotate-certificates + serverTLSBootstrap) so certs auto-renew via CSRs the control plane approves. Monitor expiry: kubeadm certs check-expiration for control-plane certs, and export cert expiry as Prometheus metrics (e.g., via node exporter/kube-state or a cert checker) with alerts firing weeks ahead. Automate renewal in maintenance windows to avoid manual expiry."}]}, {"name": "Docker", "slug": "docker", "icon": "🐳", "color": "#2496ed", "blurb": "Images, networking, Compose, Dockerfile best practices, container debugging.", "diagrams": [{"t": "Container exits immediately", "steps": ["docker ps -a (exit code)", "docker logs <c>", "CMD/ENTRYPOINT runs foreground?", "docker run -it image sh", "Fix command / config / env"]}, {"t": "Image build and layers", "steps": ["FROM base", "COPY package files", "RUN install deps (cached)", "COPY source", "CMD start"]}, {"t": "Multi-stage build", "steps": ["Stage 1: build (compilers, dev deps)", "Copy only artifact", "Stage 2: slim runtime image", "Smaller, safer image"]}], "cheats": [{"c": "docker ps -a", "d": "All containers with exit codes"}, {"c": "docker logs -f <c>", "d": "Follow logs"}, {"c": "docker exec -it <c> sh", "d": "Shell in running container"}, {"c": "docker inspect <c>", "d": "Full config, IP, mounts"}, {"c": "docker system df / prune", "d": "Disk usage and cleanup"}, {"c": "docker build --no-cache", "d": "Force fresh build"}, {"c": "docker stats", "d": "Live resource usage"}, {"c": "docker compose up -d --build", "d": "Rebuild and start stack"}], "follow": [{"k": ["exit", "exiting", "cmd", "entrypoint"], "q": "Difference between CMD and ENTRYPOINT?", "a": "ENTRYPOINT is the fixed executable, CMD supplies default arguments that can be overridden at docker run. I often use ENTRYPOINT for the binary and CMD for default flags. Both should use exec form so signals reach the app."}, {"k": ["size", "image", "large", "layer", "build", "slow"], "q": "How do you reduce Docker image size?", "a": "Multi-stage builds, slim or distroless base images, combining RUN steps and cleaning caches, and a good .dockerignore. Smaller images also mean faster pulls and fewer vulnerabilities."}, {"k": ["cache", "layer", "slow", "build"], "q": "Why does Docker rebuild everything after a small code change?", "a": "Because a changed layer invalidates all layers after it. I copy dependency files and install first, then copy source, so the dependency layer stays cached."}, {"k": ["network", "compose", "connect", "depends"], "q": "How do containers talk to each other?", "a": "On a user-defined bridge network, containers resolve each other by service name through Docker DNS. In Compose that happens automatically, so I use the service name as hostname and not localhost."}, {"k": ["volume", "data", "persist", "mount"], "q": "Volume vs bind mount?", "a": "Volumes are managed by Docker and best for persistent data like databases. Bind mounts map a host path, good for local development. In production I prefer volumes or external storage."}, {"k": ["root", "security", "secret", "vulnerab"], "q": "How do you make containers secure?", "a": "Run as a non-root user, use minimal base images, scan with Trivy in CI, never bake secrets in images, make the filesystem read-only where possible, and drop unneeded capabilities."}, {"k": ["disk", "space", "prune", "clean"], "q": "Docker host is full. What do you clean safely?", "a": "docker system df first, then docker system prune for stopped containers, dangling images and unused networks, and image prune -a for old images. I avoid pruning volumes blindly since that is data."}, {"k": ["memory", "cpu", "limit", "oom"], "q": "Container gets killed with exit code 137. Meaning?", "a": "It was SIGKILLed, usually by the OOM killer because of a memory limit. I check docker inspect for OOMKilled true and raise the limit or fix the leak."}], "edge": [{"q": "Your app works on your laptop in Docker but fails in the pipeline. What differs?", "a": "Architecture, for example Apple M1 arm64 vs amd64 build agents, environment variables, build context and .dockerignore, cached layers, base image versions that moved, and network access during build. I pin base image versions and build with buildx for the right platform.", "fu": []}, {"q": "Docker container cannot resolve DNS names but the host can. Debug?", "a": "I check /etc/resolv.conf inside the container, custom daemon DNS config, corporate VPN or firewall blocking UDP 53, and try --dns 8.8.8.8 to confirm. On user-defined networks I check the embedded DNS 127.0.0.11.", "fu": []}, {"q": "A container keeps restarting under restart: always and hides the real error. How do you find it?", "a": "docker logs --tail and docker inspect for the exit code and restart count. I temporarily run it without the restart policy or override the entrypoint with sh to debug.", "fu": []}, {"q": "A secret ended up in an image layer. Is deleting it in a later layer enough?", "a": "No. Earlier layers still contain it and anyone with the image can extract it. I rotate the secret, rebuild from clean, and use build secrets with BuildKit or runtime injection.", "fu": []}], "qa": [{"id": 15, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "General", "q": "During a deploy, your monitoring shows a spike in HTTP 502 errors between your load balancer and backend containers. The containers appear healthy in 'docker ps'. How do you investigate the cause of the 502s?", "a": "502 means the proxy/LB couldn't get a valid response from the backend. Check that the app is actually listening on the expected port inside the container ('docker exec ... netstat/ss'). Verify the LB health check path and port match the app. Review container logs for startup delays or crashes, confirm the app finished booting before receiving traffic, and check for port mapping or network mismatches."}, {"id": 17, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're asked to expose an internal Node.js app running in a Docker container on your laptop to a teammate for testing. You run the container with 'docker run myapp' but the teammate can't reach it, and even you can't hit it on localhost:3000. What's likely wrong and how do you fix it?", "a": "The container port isn't published to the host. 'docker run' without -p doesn't map ports. Run 'docker run -p 3000:3000 myapp' to bind the container port to the host. Verify the app listens on 0.0.0.0, not 127.0.0.1 inside the container. For teammate access, share your host IP and ensure firewall allows the port."}, {"id": 30, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "General", "q": "After a Docker Compose deploy, your app container can't connect to the database container using the hostname 'db', throwing a 'could not resolve host' error, even though both containers are up. How do you troubleshoot?", "a": "Verify both services are on the same Docker network ('docker network inspect'). By default Compose creates a shared network where service names resolve via DNS, so check the service is actually named 'db' and both are in the same compose file/project. Confirm the DB is listening and the app waits for it to be ready. Test with 'docker exec app ping db'. Fix naming, network config, or add depends_on/healthchecks."}, {"id": 43, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "General", "q": "You run a Dockerized app on a single EC2 host. Every few days the host runs out of disk and containers fail to start. df shows /var/lib/docker consuming most of the space, but you only run a couple of containers. What's happening and how do you fix it?", "a": "Docker accumulates dangling images, stopped containers, unused volumes, and build cache. Run 'docker system df' to see usage. Clean with 'docker system prune -a --volumes' (carefully). For long-term fix, schedule periodic pruning, configure log rotation via the json-file log driver (max-size/max-file), and monitor disk with alerts."}, {"id": 57, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "General", "q": "A developer complains that their Docker image is 1.8GB and takes forever to push and pull in CI, slowing deploys. It's a simple Python Flask app. They ask you how to make the image smaller without breaking the app. What do you suggest?", "a": "Use a slim/alpine base image, adopt multi-stage builds to keep only runtime artifacts, install only needed deps with --no-cache-dir, combine RUN layers, and add a .dockerignore to exclude build/test files. Remove build tools from the final stage. This typically cuts size dramatically and speeds push/pull."}, {"id": 64, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "General", "q": "A developer says their containerized app can't connect to an external API, getting connection timeouts, but the same request works from the Docker host. Both use the same DNS. How do you troubleshoot the container's networking?", "a": "Exec into the container and test: nslookup/dig the API host, curl the IP directly, and check /etc/resolv.conf. Verify the container's network mode and that outbound traffic isn't blocked by a firewall or missing NAT. Compare DNS resolution inside vs host. Common causes: custom DNS in the daemon, restrictive network policy, or the container on an isolated bridge lacking egress."}, {"id": 66, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your team's Docker Compose stack builds fine locally, but a colleague on macOS says the same 'docker compose up' fails with a 'no matching manifest for linux/arm64' error when pulling one of your base images. Your CI on Linux x86 works. How do you explain and fix this?", "a": "The base image lacks an arm64 variant for Apple Silicon. Options: use a multi-arch image that supports arm64, or build multi-arch with docker buildx and push both platforms, or force platform with 'platform: linux/amd64' in compose (runs via emulation, slower). Prefer multi-arch base images so all devs and CI pull the right architecture automatically."}, {"id": 72, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "General", "q": "A developer's containerized microservice needs to talk to another service in the same Docker Compose file, but connections fail on startup. Both services are defined, yet the app crashes because the dependency isn't ready yet. How would you make startup reliable?", "a": "depends_on only waits for the container to start, not for the service to be ready. Add a healthcheck to the dependency and use 'depends_on: condition: service_healthy'. Better still, add retry/backoff logic in the app so it reconnects if the dependency is temporarily unavailable, since startup order shouldn't be relied on in production."}, {"id": 80, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "General", "q": "A developer's Docker build was working yesterday but now fails on the CI runner with a 'no space left on device' error during the build step, though the app code hasn't changed. How do you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "The runner's disk is full, often from accumulated Docker build cache, dangling images, stopped containers, and volumes. Check with df -h and docker system df. Reclaim space with docker system prune -af and remove unused volumes. For a permanent fix, schedule regular pruning on the runner, use a fresh ephemeral runner per job, or add a build cleanup step. Ensure enough disk headroom for large image layers."}, {"id": 87, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "General", "q": "A developer running a container locally complains that changes to their source code don't appear in the running container unless they rebuild the image every time, slowing development. How would you improve their workflow?", "a": "Mount the source code as a bind volume (docker run -v $(pwd):/app or a volumes entry in Compose) so host file changes reflect immediately without rebuilds. Combine with a hot-reload tool (nodemon, flask --reload) inside the container. Keep dependency installs in the image layer so only code is mounted. Rebuild only when dependencies change."}, {"id": 99, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You're a junior engineer setting up a new microservice and need to store a database password so the app can read it at runtime. A teammate suggests hardcoding it in the Docker image to keep things simple. Why is that a bad idea and how would you handle secrets properly?", "a": "Baking secrets into an image means anyone who pulls the image (or inspects layers) can extract it, and rotating requires a rebuild. Instead inject secrets at runtime: use Kubernetes Secrets (mounted or env), AWS Secrets Manager/SSM Parameter Store, or Docker/Compose env from a secrets store. Keep secrets out of Git, restrict access with least-privilege IAM/RBAC, and enable rotation."}, {"id": 104, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Need to run a one-off data migration script inside a running Docker container on a production host, but you don't want to modify the image or restart the container. How would you execute the script safely?", "a": "Use docker exec to run a command in the running container, e.g. docker exec -it <container> python migrate.py. Copy the script in first with docker cp if it's not already inside. Run it in a maintenance window, ensure you have a DB backup, and remember exec changes aren't persisted in the image—so bake permanent changes into the Dockerfile later."}, {"id": 109, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're on call and a Docker container on a production host keeps restarting every few minutes. 'docker ps' shows it cycling, and the service is intermittently unavailable. You need to find why it's dying without guessing. How do you investigate?", "a": "Check 'docker inspect <container>' for the exit code and restart policy, and read 'docker logs --tail 100 <container>' for the crash cause. Exit 137 suggests OOMKilled (check memory limits and host memory), 139 a segfault, non-zero app errors point to config/dependency issues. Verify liveness/health checks aren't killing it. Fix root cause—raise memory limit, correct config, or fix the failing dependency—then confirm it stabilizes."}, {"id": 114, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A teammate asks you to run a container that must keep restarting if it crashes, but currently it just exits and stays stopped after a failure. They're running it with 'docker run' manually. How would you make Docker automatically restart it?", "a": "Add a restart policy: 'docker run --restart unless-stopped myimage' (or on-failure with a max retry count). This tells the Docker daemon to relaunch the container if it exits or if the host reboots. For production, though, prefer an orchestrator or systemd unit to manage lifecycle and health rather than relying solely on the Docker restart policy."}, {"id": 125, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your CI pipeline's Docker build succeeds, but the resulting image is over 2 GB when the app is a small Go binary. A senior asks you to shrink it significantly. How would you approach reducing the image size?", "a": "Use a multi-stage build: compile in a full builder image, then copy only the binary into a minimal final stage (scratch, distroless, or alpine). Avoid installing build tools in the final image. Combine RUN layers and clean package caches. Use .dockerignore to exclude unneeded files. For Go, a static binary in FROM scratch can drop the image to a few MB."}, {"id": 133, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer says their newly built Docker image runs fine locally but exits immediately with code 0 when deployed to the server. No errors appear in logs. How would you figure out why the container won't stay running?", "a": "Exit code 0 means the main process finished successfully — the container isn't crashing, it just has nothing long-running to do. Check the ENTRYPOINT/CMD: a script that runs and exits, or a service started in background so PID 1 returns immediately. Run 'docker logs' and 'docker inspect' to see the command. Fix by making the foreground process the long-running one (e.g. run the server in foreground, don't background it), so PID 1 stays alive."}, {"id": 139, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague reports that a Docker container can't connect to a Postgres database running in another container on the same host. Both containers are up, and the DB accepts connections locally. The app uses 'localhost:5432' as the connection string. Why does it fail and how do you fix it?", "a": "Inside a container 'localhost' refers to that container itself, not the DB container. Put both containers on a user-defined Docker network and connect using the DB container's service/name (e.g. 'db:5432'). With docker-compose this works automatically via service names. Verify with 'docker network inspect' and test connectivity from inside the app container."}, {"id": 143, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer wants their app's Docker container to automatically restart if it crashes or if the Docker host reboots, but right now they run docker run myapp and it stays down after a crash. How would you configure this?", "a": "Add a restart policy: docker run --restart unless-stopped myapp (or always). on-failure restarts only on non-zero exit, unless-stopped also survives host reboot (with Docker enabled at boot). Verify with docker inspect under RestartPolicy. For production, better to use an orchestrator or systemd unit managing the container."}, {"id": 150, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague reports that after a new container was deployed on a Docker host, other services on the box slowed down because that one container is consuming all the CPU. They ran docker run myapp with no limits. How would you prevent one container from starving the host?", "a": "Constrain the container's resources at run time. Use docker run --cpus=\"1.5\" --memory=\"512m\" myapp to cap CPU and memory. --cpus limits CPU cores it can use and --memory caps RAM (with OOM protection for the host). In Compose set deploy.resources.limits or cpus/mem_limit. This ensures a noisy container can't starve other workloads on the host."}, {"id": 153, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer reports that their Docker image is over 1.5 GB, making pulls slow in the pipeline. It's a simple Python app. How would you reduce the image size?", "a": "Use a slim/alpine base image (e.g. python:3.12-slim) and a multi-stage build so build tools aren't in the final image. Combine RUN steps and clean apt/pip caches. Add a .dockerignore to exclude venv, tests, and .git. Pin and install only production dependencies. Verify with docker images and docker history to see which layers are largest."}, {"id": 159, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your CI/CD pipeline builds the same Docker image for both the test stage and the production push, but developers complain builds are slow because unrelated code changes trigger full rebuilds of dependency layers. How would you optimize the Dockerfile for better build caching?", "a": "Order Dockerfile instructions from least to most frequently changing. Copy dependency manifests (package.json, requirements.txt) and install deps before copying application source, so dependency layers stay cached when only app code changes. Use multi-stage builds and enable BuildKit caching (--cache-from/registry cache) in CI so layers are reused across runs."}, {"id": 178, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer's Docker container runs fine with docker run but immediately exits when started via docker-compose. docker ps -a shows exit code 0. No error logs appear. How would you diagnose why the container exits under compose but not with docker run?", "a": "Exit code 0 means the main process finished normally — likely the compose config overrides the command/entrypoint or lacks the tty/interactive flag the app needs. Compare docker run args to the compose command, entrypoint, and tty/stdin_open settings. Ensure the container runs a long-lived foreground process; run docker-compose logs and inspect the effective command with docker inspect."}, {"id": 185, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer built a multi-stage Dockerfile, but the final image still includes build tools and source code they intended to leave behind, making it large and exposing source. They copied files into the final stage but something's off. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "In multi-stage builds, only what you explicitly COPY --from=builder into the final stage should be present. If the image is bloated, they likely built off the builder stage (missing final FROM), used a heavy base image in the final stage, or copied the whole workdir instead of just the built artifact. Fix: start the final stage from a slim/distroless base, copy only the compiled binary/needed files, and ensure the last FROM is the runtim…"}, {"id": 193, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "After a deploy your Dockerized app can't connect to a Redis container on the same host; it worked before you moved both into a docker-compose setup. The app uses localhost:6379 as the Redis address. Why does it fail and how would you fix it?", "a": "In compose each container has its own network namespace, so localhost refers to the app container itself, not Redis. Fix: reference Redis by its compose service name (e.g. redis:6379), since compose provides DNS resolution between services on the shared network. Ensure both services are on the same network and the Redis port is exposed to other containers."}, {"id": 205, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer says their two Docker containers on a custom bridge network can't reach each other by container name, even though both are on the same network. Pinging by container name fails with 'unknown host'. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Verify both containers are actually on the same user-defined network with 'docker network inspect <net>' (default 'bridge' has no automatic DNS; user-defined networks do). Ensure you used --network or a shared network in compose, and reference containers by their service/container name, not localhost. Recreate containers attached to the same user-defined bridge so Docker's embedded DNS resolves names."}, {"id": 210, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "During an incident an on-call asks you to quickly find which container on a busy Docker host is generating the most log output and filling the disk, without stopping any containers. How would you approach this?", "a": "Check per-container log sizes under /var/lib/docker/containers/*/*-json.log with du -sh to find the biggest. Map the container ID to a name via docker ps. Tail the noisy container with docker logs --tail 50 -f <id> to see what's spamming. Short-term: truncate the log file or configure log rotation (max-size, max-file) in the daemon or compose. Address the root cause of excessive logging in the app."}, {"id": 216, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A teammate reports that after a recent deploy your Nginx-based Docker container returns a 502 Bad Gateway for all requests, even though the upstream app container is running and you can curl the app directly from your laptop. Both containers run on the same host under docker-compose. How would you diagnose and fix the 502?", "a": "Check whether Nginx can reach the app by container name over the compose network, not localhost. Exec into the Nginx container and curl http://app:port. A 502 usually means Nginx can't reach the upstream — verify the upstream host/port in nginx.conf matches the app's service name and internal port (not the published one), confirm both are on the same compose network, and check depends_on/startup order. Fix the upstream address and reload N…"}, {"id": 228, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer says their Docker build fails on the CI runner with 'no space left on device', though the same build works on their laptop. The runner has been building images for weeks. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Run docker system df to see space used by images, containers, volumes, and build cache — dangling layers and old images accumulate over time. Free space with docker system prune -af --volumes (careful with volumes) or docker builder prune. Check host disk with df -h. Long-term, add a scheduled prune to the runner or use a cleanup step in the pipeline and monitor disk usage."}, {"id": 235, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer says their Docker container works locally but on the production host it starts as root, and the security team flagged that the app process runs as root inside the container. The Dockerfile has no USER instruction. How would you fix this so the app runs as a non-root user?", "a": "By default containers run as root when no USER is set. Add a non-root user in the Dockerfile: create it (e.g. RUN adduser -D appuser), set ownership of needed files with chown, then add USER appuser before the CMD. Ensure the app doesn't need privileged ports (<1024) or adjust to a higher port. Rebuild, and verify with docker exec <c> whoami. Optionally enforce via securityContext runAsNonRoot in Kubernetes."}, {"id": 250, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your Docker image build is extremely slow and the final image is over 1.5GB for a small Python app. Every code change forces a full reinstall of dependencies. How would you reduce build time and image size?", "a": "Order Dockerfile steps so dependencies install before copying app code, so dependency layers stay cached when only code changes. Use a slim base image (e.g., python:3.x-slim) and a multi-stage build to exclude build tools from the final image. Add a .dockerignore to skip unnecessary files. Combine RUN steps and clean package caches. This leverages layer caching and shrinks the final image."}, {"id": 261, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your team wants to run the same container image across dev, staging, and prod, but developers keep hardcoding environment-specific config (DB URLs, feature flags) into the image, forcing a rebuild per environment. How would you fix this so one image works everywhere?", "a": "Externalize config from the image. Inject settings at runtime via environment variables, ConfigMaps/Secrets in Kubernetes, or mounted config files. The image should stay immutable; only the config layer changes per environment. This follows the 12-factor 'config in environment' principle, enabling build-once-deploy-anywhere and faster, consistent releases."}, {"id": 269, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your Docker Compose setup for local development has a database container and an app container, but the app intermittently fails on startup with 'connection refused' to the DB. The DB container is defined and starts, but the app sometimes races ahead. How would you fix this reliably?", "a": "depends_on only waits for container start, not DB readiness. Add a healthcheck to the DB service and use depends_on with condition: service_healthy. Alternatively add retry/backoff logic or a wait-for-it/wait-for-port script in the app's entrypoint. The app should tolerate the DB being temporarily unavailable rather than assuming instant availability."}, {"id": 273, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "As a junior engineer, you notice a Docker image being built in your pipeline takes 15 minutes because 'pip install' runs every time, even for minor CSS changes. How do you optimize this?", "a": "Implement Docker multi-stage builds and leverage layer caching. Copy only the requirements.txt file first and run 'pip install' before copying the rest of your application code. This ensures the dependency installation layer is only rebuilt when the requirements.txt file changes, significantly speeding up subsequent builds for code-only updates."}, {"id": 279, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A container in your local Docker environment fails to resolve external hostnames, but the host machine has no issues with DNS. What steps would you take to troubleshoot the container's networking?", "a": "I would first check the container's '/etc/resolv.conf' to see if it is pointing to a valid DNS resolver. I would then verify if the Docker daemon is correctly inheriting the host's DNS settings or if a custom '--dns' flag is required. I would test connectivity from within the container using 'nslookup' or 'dig' to identify if the issue is DNS resolution or overall network connectivity to the upstream gateway."}, {"id": 293, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A Docker container is crashing on startup. You suspect it's failing to connect to a mandatory environment variable-based secret, but the container exits so fast you can't inspect it. How do you troubleshoot the startup command without modifying the Dockerfile?", "a": "Override the entrypoint during runtime using 'docker run --entrypoint /bin/sh -it <image_name>'. This keeps the container alive in an interactive shell, allowing you to manually inspect environment variables with 'env', test connectivity, and run the start command manually to observe the exact error message."}, {"id": 298, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your CI pipeline uses a Multi-Stage Dockerfile to build an application. You notice the final image is still very large, containing build tools like gcc and maven. How do you modify your Dockerfile to exclude these from the final production image?", "a": "Use a multi-stage build. In the first stage, use a base image with necessary build tools to compile the artifact. In the final stage, use a 'FROM' command with a smaller, runtime-only image (like alpine or distroless), and use the 'COPY --from=<stage_name>' command to copy only the compiled binary or JAR file from the builder stage into the final image."}, {"id": 301, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A containerized application is throwing 'Connection Refused' when trying to reach an external API. The host machine can ping the API fine. How do you troubleshoot the container networking?", "a": "First, inspect the container's /etc/resolv.conf to check DNS configuration. Then, use 'docker run --rm busybox nslookup <hostname>' inside the network namespace to see if the container can resolve the domain. If it can resolve but not connect, check if the Docker network bridge has restricted outbound access via iptables or if the host's firewall (ufw/firewalld) is blocking the container's subnet traffic."}, {"id": 306, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Notice a Docker container is failing to mount a host volume, resulting in an 'access denied' error. The host directory exists and has global read/write permissions. How do you investigate this?", "a": "Check for SELinux or AppArmor constraints. These security modules often restrict container access to host files even if standard Linux permissions allow it. Run 'dmesg | grep -i denied' or check system audit logs to confirm if an AVC (Access Vector Cache) denial is occurring, then adjust the context with 'z' or 'Z' flags in the docker run volume mount if applicable."}, {"id": 311, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A Docker container using a bind mount to a host directory is failing with 'Permission Denied'. How do you verify the mismatch between the container's user ID and the host file ownership?", "a": "First, use 'docker exec -it <container_id> id' to check the effective UID inside the container. Then, run 'ls -n <host_path>' on the host to see the numeric UID/GID of the files. If they don't match, the container process lacks permissions. You can resolve this by adjusting file ownership on the host to match the container's user, or by running the container with the '--user' flag set to the host's UID."}, {"id": 318, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A Jenkins CI pipeline fails because a Docker image build hangs. You suspect a stale process is locking the Docker socket. How do you safely find and clean up only the stuck build process?", "a": "Run 'docker ps' to see if any containers are stuck in 'Created' or 'Running' state for an abnormal duration. Use 'ps aux | grep docker' to identify the specific process ID (PID) of the build or container runtime task. Avoid 'docker system prune' as it affects all containers. Instead, use 'docker stop <container_id>' on the specific ID. If unresponsive, check the Docker daemon logs via 'journalctl -u docker' for hardware or resource saturation …"}, {"id": 323, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "You suspect a Docker container is not respecting its CPU limits. The container is running a heavy calculation. How do you verify the actual CPU shares the container is receiving compared to the allocated quota?", "a": "Use 'docker stats' to monitor real-time resource consumption. To verify the settings, inspect the container configuration using 'docker inspect <container_id>'. Look under 'HostConfig.CpuShares' or 'HostConfig.NanoCpus'. Compare these values against the host's performance metrics using 'top' or 'htop' to see if the process is hitting the kernel-enforced throttling limits."}, {"id": 333, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A local Docker container using an entrypoint script fails immediately after startup. How do you inspect the exit code and logs without the container disappearing?", "a": "Run the container with '--rm=false' (or don't use --rm). Once it exits, use 'docker ps -a' to see the exit status code. Run 'docker inspect <container_id>' to see the state, and use 'docker logs <container_id>' to retrieve the stdout/stderr output generated by the entrypoint script during the crash."}, {"id": 345, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A junior engineer notices that a Docker container running a web server is reporting 'Connection Refused' when accessed from the host machine, even though the process is running. How do you verify the container's port binding configuration?", "a": "Use 'docker ps' to check the 'PORTS' column to ensure the host port is correctly mapped to the container port (e.g., 8080:80). If the mapping looks correct, use 'docker inspect <container-id>' to check the 'Config.ExposedPorts'. Finally, use 'netstat -tulpn' or 'ss -tulpn' on the host to confirm the Docker daemon is actually listening on the mapped host port. If not, the application inside the container might be bound to 127.0.0.1 instead of 0…"}, {"id": 356, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your Docker container runs a Python web app, but when you try to view logs with 'docker logs <container>' after the app writes to a log file inside the container, you see nothing — the logs command returns empty even though the app is clearly processing requests. Why, and how should the app be configured so logs show up?", "a": "The app writes to a file inside the container, but 'docker logs' only captures stdout/stderr. Fix: configure the app to log to stdout/stderr (console handler) instead of a file, following the 12-factor logging approach. Then Docker's logging driver captures output and 'docker logs' works. For files, you'd otherwise need to exec in and cat the file or mount a volume."}, {"id": 361, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer wants to run their app container on a specific port so it's reachable from the host at http://localhost:9000/, but the app inside the container listens on port 8080. They ran 'docker run myapp' and can't reach it from the host at all. How would you make the app reachable at localhost:9000?", "a": "The container port isn't published to the host. Use 'docker run -p 9000:8080 myapp' — the left side (9000) is the host port, the right side (8080) is the container port the app listens on. Verify the app binds to 0.0.0.0 not 127.0.0.1 inside the container, then confirm with 'docker ps' showing the port mapping and curl localhost:9000."}, {"id": 370, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer wants to share persistent data between two containers running on the same Docker host so both can read and write the same files. Right now each container has its own copy and changes aren't seen by the other. How would you set this up?", "a": "Use a shared Docker volume mounted into both containers. Create it with 'docker volume create shared-data', then run both containers with '-v shared-data:/path/in/container'. Both will read/write the same underlying storage. For files on the host, use a bind mount '-v /host/path:/container/path'. In Compose, define the volume once and reference it in both services under volumes."}, {"id": 372, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After migrating an app to Docker, containers on a busy host randomly fail to start with 'no space left on device', yet df -h on the root filesystem shows plenty of free space. How would you diagnose and resolve this?", "a": "Check docker system df and df -i — the issue is often inode exhaustion or Docker's overlay/volumes dir filling a separate mount. Dangling images, stopped containers, unused volumes and build cache accumulate. Run docker system prune -a --volumes (carefully), clean old images, and set up log rotation / a cron cleanup to prevent recurrence."}, {"id": 388, "cat": "Docker", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague reports that after running 'docker build', their image contains an old version of a dependency even though they updated requirements.txt. They rebuild but Docker seems to skip re-installing dependencies. Why is this happening and how would you fix it?", "a": "Docker is reusing a cached layer. If requirements.txt is COPYed after other files or the install step's inputs didn't change hash, the RUN pip install layer is served from cache. Fix by COPYing requirements.txt before the app code and running install on it, so changes to it bust the cache. To force a clean build use --no-cache. Proper layer ordering keeps caching correct."}]}, {"name": "CI/CD", "slug": "ci-cd", "icon": "🔁", "color": "#22c55e", "blurb": "Jenkins, GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, caching, deployment strategies, pipeline failures.", "diagrams": [{"t": "Typical CI/CD pipeline", "steps": ["Git push / PR", "Build + unit tests", "Lint + security scan", "Build and push image", "Deploy to staging", "Approval / smoke tests", "Deploy to prod"]}, {"t": "Blue-Green vs Canary", "steps": ["New version deployed beside old", "Blue-Green: switch 100% at once", "Canary: send 5% → 25% → 100%", "Watch error rate and latency", "Rollback = switch traffic back"]}, {"t": "Pipeline failure triage", "steps": ["Read failing stage log", "Env or code issue?", "Reproduce locally / rerun", "Check agent disk, creds, cache", "Fix and add guard in pipeline"]}], "cheats": [{"c": "git log --oneline --graph", "d": "Understand branch history"}, {"c": "git revert <sha>", "d": "Safe undo on shared branches"}, {"c": "git bisect", "d": "Find the commit that broke it"}, {"c": "actions/cache with hashFiles()", "d": "Dependency caching"}, {"c": "docker buildx --cache-from", "d": "Layer caching in CI"}, {"c": "Jenkinsfile: post { always { cleanWs() } }", "d": "Workspace cleanup"}, {"c": "if: github.ref == refs/heads/main", "d": "Conditional deploy"}, {"c": "needs: [job]", "d": "Job dependencies in Actions"}], "follow": [{"k": ["cache", "slow", "speed", "minutes"], "q": "Pipeline takes 25 minutes. Where do you start optimising?", "a": "Measure per-stage time first. Then dependency and Docker layer caching, run independent jobs in parallel, path filters so only affected parts run, and faster or larger runners where it pays off."}, {"k": ["secret", "credential", "token", "password"], "q": "How do you handle secrets in pipelines?", "a": "Store in the CI secret store or an external vault, inject at runtime as masked variables, and prefer OIDC federation to cloud roles instead of long-lived keys. Never print them or commit them, and scan repos for leaks."}, {"k": ["rollback", "deploy", "release", "canary", "blue"], "q": "Blue-green vs canary vs rolling?", "a": "Rolling replaces instances gradually and is the simplest. Blue-green gives instant switch and rollback but needs double capacity. Canary exposes a small percentage first and is best for risky changes with good metrics."}, {"k": ["flaky", "fails", "intermittent"], "q": "A test fails randomly. What do you do?", "a": "I rerun to confirm flakiness, look for shared state, timing, external dependencies, and test order issues. I quarantine it with a ticket rather than ignoring it, and fix the root cause so developers keep trusting the pipeline."}, {"k": ["merge", "git", "branch", "conflict", "commit", "revert"], "q": "git revert vs git reset?", "a": "Revert creates a new commit that undoes a change, safe on shared branches. Reset moves the branch pointer and rewrites history, so only for local or unpushed work."}, {"k": ["jenkins", "agent", "node", "disk"], "q": "Jenkins agent offline or builds stuck in queue. Steps?", "a": "Check the agent connection and logs, disk space and Java process, executor count, and labels in the Jenkinsfile that must match the agent. For stability I use ephemeral agents in Kubernetes or Docker."}, {"k": ["approval", "prod", "gate", "test"], "q": "How do you stop bad code reaching production?", "a": "Branch protection with required reviews, automated tests and security scans as required checks, environment approvals for prod, and automated rollback on failed health checks."}], "edge": [{"q": "A deployment to prod succeeded in the pipeline but users see the old version. Why?", "a": "Cache layers like CDN or browser cache, image tag not changed so Kubernetes did not roll out, a rollout still in progress, or the deploy went to a different environment or cluster. I verify the running image digest.", "fu": []}, {"q": "Someone force-pushed to main and wiped commits. How do you recover?", "a": "Use git reflog on any clone that had the commits, or the CI runner and GitHub PR refs, then push the restored commit. Then enable branch protection to forbid force pushes.", "fu": []}, {"q": "The same pipeline passes on one branch and fails on another with identical code. Why?", "a": "Different variables or secrets scoped per branch or environment, cache poisoning, a different base commit, or conditional logic in the pipeline. I diff the environment and clear the cache.", "fu": []}, {"q": "Your pipeline deploys to prod on every merge and a bad release goes out Friday evening. How do you improve the process?", "a": "Add automated smoke tests and canary with auto-rollback, deployment windows or approvals for risky changes, feature flags, and clear rollback runbooks so recovery takes minutes.", "fu": []}], "qa": [{"id": 1, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your team pushes code multiple times a day, but the CI pipeline takes 25 minutes because every run reinstalls dependencies and rebuilds the Docker image from scratch. Developers are frustrated by slow feedback. How would you speed up the pipeline?", "a": "Introduce caching: cache dependency directories (npm/pip/maven) keyed on lockfile hash, and use Docker layer caching (BuildKit, cache-from) so unchanged layers aren't rebuilt. Order Dockerfile so dependencies install before copying source. Split pipeline into parallel stages (lint/test/build). Use a persistent or warmer build agent, and only run full builds on relevant path changes."}, {"id": 8, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You're setting up a CI pipeline for a small team. A junior developer's merge request passes tests locally but fails in CI with 'command not found' errors. The code hasn't changed. What could be causing the difference and how do you fix it?", "a": "The CI environment differs from local — missing tools, different PATH, or dependencies not installed. Check the CI runner image and pipeline steps to ensure all required packages/tools are installed. Pin tool versions and use a consistent base image (Docker) matching local. Add explicit install steps and print versions for debugging. Standardizing environments prevents 'works on my machine' issues."}, {"id": 13, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "General", "q": "A developer's feature branch build in your CI pipeline suddenly fails at the test stage with intermittent, different failures each run, while the same tests pass locally every time. What likely causes these flaky failures and how do you approach fixing them?", "a": "Flaky tests usually stem from shared state, race conditions, timing/async waits, test ordering, or external dependencies (network, DB). Reproduce by running tests repeatedly and in random order. Isolate the flaky test, add proper waits/mocks, avoid shared global state, and ensure clean fixtures per run. Quarantine the flaky test to unblock the team while fixing the root cause."}, {"id": 21, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "General", "q": "You joined a team using GitHub Actions. A workflow that builds and pushes a Docker image to ECR fails at the push step with an authentication error, though the build succeeds. The AWS credentials are stored as repo secrets. How do you troubleshoot?", "a": "Check that the AWS secrets are actually available to the job (secrets aren't passed to workflows triggered from forks/PRs). Verify the configure-aws-credentials step ran and the IAM user/role has ecr:GetAuthorizationToken and push permissions. Confirm you ran 'aws ecr get-login-password | docker login' before pushing, and that the ECR repo exists in the correct region/account. Check token expiry and correct registry URL."}, {"id": 28, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your CI/CD pipeline deploys to staging automatically, but the team wants production deploys to require a manual approval step after staging passes. How would you design this in your pipeline?", "a": "Split the pipeline into stages: build/test, deploy-staging (automatic), then deploy-production gated by a manual approval. Use the tool's approval/gate feature (e.g. GitHub Actions environments with required reviewers, GitLab manual jobs, or Jenkins input step). Restrict who can approve, keep the same artifact promoted through stages, and log who approved for auditability."}, {"id": 34, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're setting up your first CI pipeline for a Python project. The build passes but you realize secrets like the database password are being printed in the pipeline logs when tests run. How do you fix this and prevent leaks?", "a": "Store secrets in the CI's secret store (masked variables/repo secrets), not in code or plain env files. Ensure the CI masks them in logs. Remove any echo/print of env vars and disable debug logging that dumps environment. Rotate the exposed password immediately. Use least-privilege credentials scoped to the pipeline only."}, {"id": 38, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your CI pipeline builds a Docker image and tags every build as 'latest', pushing to your registry. A rollback is needed after a bad deploy, but you realize 'latest' was overwritten and you can't identify or pull the previous good image. How would you fix your tagging strategy?", "a": "Stop relying on 'latest' for deploys. Tag images with immutable identifiers like the Git commit SHA or a semantic version (e.g. app:1.4.2 or app:sha-abc123). Keep 'latest' as a convenience pointer only. This lets you pin deployments and roll back to any specific previous tag. Enable registry image retention/immutability policies."}, {"id": 42, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A teammate deleted a file from a Git repo and committed, but the file contained a large binary that's still bloating the repo. Cloning is now painfully slow for everyone. As the junior on the team, how would you reduce the repo size safely?", "a": "Simply deleting a file only removes it from the latest commit; it stays in history. Use git filter-repo (or BFG Repo-Cleaner) to purge the blob from all commits, then force-push. Coordinate with the team since history rewriting breaks existing clones. Going forward, add large binaries to Git LFS and update .gitignore."}, {"id": 48, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your CI pipeline runs unit tests then integration tests that need a database. The integration stage fails randomly because the DB container isn't ready when tests start, even though it was started in a prior step. How do you fix this?", "a": "The DB process being 'started' doesn't mean it's accepting connections. Add a readiness wait: poll the port/health with a retry loop (e.g., wait-for-it, pg_isready, or a healthcheck) before running tests. Use service containers with healthchecks so CI waits for 'healthy'. Avoid fixed sleeps; wait on actual readiness to eliminate the race."}, {"id": 53, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your CI pipeline builds and deploys on every push to main, but a bad commit went straight to production because nothing validated the build artifact. The team wants a safety gate that still keeps deploys fast. How would you design the pipeline?", "a": "Add stages: build → automated tests/linting → build immutable artifact → deploy to staging → smoke/health checks → deploy to prod. Only promote the same tested artifact forward. Use fail-fast on tests and optionally a canary or automated rollback on health-check failure. Keep prod deploy gated on passing checks so speed is retained but bad builds are blocked."}, {"id": 60, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your team uses GitHub Actions to deploy to multiple environments. The workflow duplicates the same 40 lines of build steps across dev, staging, and prod jobs, and a recent bug fix had to be copy-pasted three times, causing one env to be missed. How would you restructure this?", "a": "Extract shared logic into a reusable workflow (workflow_call) or a composite action, then call it per environment with inputs/matrix. Use environment-specific variables/secrets via GitHub Environments. This DRYs the pipeline so fixes apply everywhere at once and reduces drift between environments."}, {"id": 63, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your team deploys with a blue-green strategy on AWS using two target groups behind an ALB. After switching traffic to green, users report errors, and you need to roll back fast. What's the quickest safe rollback and what should you verify afterward?", "a": "Switch the ALB listener rule back to the blue target group to instantly restore the known-good version, since blue is still running. Verify health checks pass on blue, confirm error rates drop in monitoring, and drain green connections. Then investigate green's failures in a non-prod path before retrying. Blue-green's key benefit is this instant rollback with no redeploy."}, {"id": 69, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A teammate created a Jenkins pipeline that clones a large monorepo on every run, and builds now take 20+ minutes just fetching Git history. Actual compile time is under 3 minutes. As a junior, how would you speed up the checkout?", "a": "Use a shallow clone (--depth 1) and single-branch fetch so you don't pull full history. Enable Git reference/mirror caching or persistent workspace caching on the agent. For monorepos, use sparse-checkout to fetch only needed paths. This cuts network and disk I/O dramatically without changing build logic."}, {"id": 75, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your GitHub Actions CI builds fine, but deploys to AWS started failing after a teammate rotated the IAM access keys stored as secrets. You want to stop storing long-lived keys entirely. How would you redesign authentication?", "a": "Replace static keys with OIDC: configure GitHub as an OIDC identity provider in AWS IAM, create a role with a trust policy scoped to your repo/branch, and use aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials with role-to-assume. This gives short-lived, auto-rotated credentials, removes secrets from the repo, and lets you tightly scope permissions per workflow."}, {"id": 78, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your CI pipeline runs a suite of tests that pass locally and on most runs, but roughly one in five pipeline runs fails on a different test each time with no code change. Developers are re-running until green. How would you investigate and stabilize these flaky tests?", "a": "Identify flaky tests by tracking which fail intermittently across runs. Common causes: shared state between tests, timing/race conditions, reliance on external services, or test ordering. Isolate by running tests in random order and in parallel. Fix by adding proper waits/retries for async ops, mocking external calls, and cleaning state between tests. Quarantine flaky tests so they don't block deploys while being fixed."}, {"id": 83, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're setting up a new GitHub Actions CI job for a Node app. Every run reinstalls all npm dependencies from scratch, adding several minutes. A senior asks you to cut build time. What's your approach?", "a": "Add dependency caching using actions/cache keyed on the OS and a hash of package-lock.json, caching ~/.npm or node_modules. Use 'npm ci' for reproducible installs. On cache hit installs are near-instant; on lockfile change the key changes and it refreshes. Also consider setup-node's built-in cache option which handles this automatically."}, {"id": 90, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "General", "q": "A developer's CI job needs to run on every pull request to run tests, but you notice it also triggers full deploys on draft/PR builds, occasionally pushing untested code. How would you restructure the pipeline triggers so tests run on PRs but deploys only happen on merges to main?", "a": "Separate concerns with trigger filters: run test jobs on pull_request events (any branch), and gate deploy jobs to only run on push to main (or after merge). In GitHub Actions use 'on: pull_request' for tests and 'on: push: branches: [main]' for deploy, or add conditional 'if: github.ref == refs/heads/main'. This ensures deploys only fire post-merge."}, {"id": 94, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You're a junior engineer asked to give a new teammate the ability to run a build job in Jenkins but not configure or delete other jobs. Currently everyone shares an admin login. How would you set this up properly?", "a": "Enable Jenkins security with individual accounts (or SSO/LDAP) instead of a shared admin. Use Matrix-based or Role-based Authorization Strategy to grant the teammate only Job/Build and Job/Read on the relevant jobs, not Administer or Configure/Delete. Verify by logging in as them. This follows least-privilege and gives an audit trail per user."}, {"id": 110, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your team wants merges to the main branch to automatically deploy, but only after a senior manually approves the production step. Right now the pipeline deploys straight through with no gate. How would you add a manual approval before prod using your CI/CD tool?", "a": "Split the pipeline into build/test, deploy-to-staging, and deploy-to-prod stages. Put a manual approval gate before the prod stage—in GitHub Actions use an Environment with required reviewers, in GitLab a 'when: manual' job, in Jenkins an 'input' step. The pipeline pauses until an authorized reviewer approves, so automated tests still run fast but production requires human sign-off."}, {"id": 117, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your GitHub Actions workflow needs to deploy to AWS, but it currently runs sequential jobs that each spin up and repeat setup steps, making the pipeline slow. You want build artifacts produced once and reused by later deploy jobs. How would you structure this?", "a": "Split into a build job that produces artifacts and deploy jobs that depend on it via 'needs'. Use actions/upload-artifact in build and actions/download-artifact in deploy jobs so you build once. Use caching for dependencies. Parallelize independent jobs and gate deploy on build success. This avoids redundant work and speeds the pipeline."}, {"id": 127, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After promoting a new build through your CI/CD pipeline, staging works but production fails because an environment-specific config value is wrong. Configs are currently copy-pasted per environment in the pipeline. How would you restructure this to prevent such mismatches?", "a": "Externalize config from the pipeline: keep a single build artifact and inject env-specific values at deploy time (env vars, config maps, or a secrets/parameter store like SSM/Vault). Use per-environment value files validated in CI. Add a promotion step that reuses the exact same artifact across envs so only config differs. Validate configs with schema checks before deploy to catch bad values early."}, {"id": 137, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your team wants every push to a feature branch to run linting and unit tests automatically, but a teammate's commits keep skipping the pipeline entirely. The workflow file exists in main but not always in feature branches. Why might CI not trigger and how do you ensure it runs on all branches?", "a": "GitHub Actions runs the workflow file present in the branch being pushed, so if the feature branch was created before the workflow existed (or was deleted there), CI won't run. Ensure the workflow file is merged/present in the branch, set proper 'on: push' branch filters (or '**'), and rebase/merge main so all branches include the workflow."}, {"id": 145, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your team's GitHub Actions workflow needs to deploy only when code is pushed to main, but right now it runs on every push to every branch, wasting runners and sometimes deploying feature code. How would you restrict deployment to main only?", "a": "Scope the trigger with branch filters: on: push: branches: [main]. For a shared workflow, gate the deploy job with a condition like if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'. Keep lint/test steps running on all branches but restrict the deploy job. Optionally use environments with required reviewers for extra protection on production deploys."}, {"id": 149, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your CI/CD pipeline deploys a new app version, but you want to release it to only 10% of production traffic first and gradually increase if error rates stay low. Currently deploys switch 100% of traffic at once. How would you implement this?", "a": "Implement a canary deployment. Route a small percentage of traffic to the new version using a service mesh (Istio), an ingress with weighted routing, or two target groups behind an ALB with weighted listener rules. Monitor error rate/latency metrics for the canary; if healthy, gradually shift weights up, otherwise roll back. Tools like Argo Rollouts or Flagger can automate this with metric-based analysis."}, {"id": 164, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your production deploy uses a blue-green strategy on AWS. After switching the ALB to the green environment, users report intermittent errors and stale sessions, even though green passed health checks. Rolling back to blue fixes it. How would you diagnose and prevent this class of issue?", "a": "Intermittent errors after cutover often stem from in-flight requests, sticky sessions tied to blue, or shared state (cache/DB) mismatched with green's schema. Diagnose via ALB access logs, target group metrics, and app logs correlating errors to the cutover time. Prevent by draining connections (deregistration delay), disabling/warming sticky sessions, ensuring backward-compatible schema/session store (shared Redis), and doing a canary before …"}, {"id": 169, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your GitHub Actions CI pipeline passes locally-run tests but intermittently fails in CI with flaky test errors and different results between runs. The tests hit a real shared database that multiple pipeline runs use simultaneously. How would you make the pipeline reliable?", "a": "The shared DB causes race conditions between concurrent runs. Isolate each run's state: spin up an ephemeral DB per job using a service container (e.g. Postgres service in the workflow), or use isolated schemas/namespaces per run. Ensure tests set up and tear down their own fixtures and don't rely on shared mutable data. This makes runs deterministic and parallel-safe."}, {"id": 173, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your GitHub Actions workflow fails at the build step with 'permission denied' when running a script, but only in CI — it works on your machine. The repo was cloned fresh by the runner. How would you fix this?", "a": "The script likely lost its executable bit in Git. Check with git ls-files --stage script.sh; if the mode is 100644 it's not executable. Fix by running git update-index --chmod=+x script.sh and committing, or invoke it with bash script.sh in the workflow. This ensures the executable permission is tracked in the repo for all runners."}, {"id": 195, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your CI/CD pipeline needs to deploy to a Kubernetes cluster, but the deploy job intermittently fails with 'unable to connect to the server: dial tcp: i/o timeout' while other jobs succeed. The kubeconfig and credentials are correct. How would you diagnose this?", "a": "Intermittent timeouts suggest network reachability rather than auth. Check if the cluster API endpoint is private and the runner is outside the allowed CIDR/VPC, or hitting an IP allowlist. Verify DNS resolution of the API server, any load balancer health, and whether self-hosted runners lose network. Fix by ensuring runners have stable, whitelisted access (VPC peering, authorized networks) and add retries for transient blips."}, {"id": 199, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team's CI/CD pipeline runs unit tests, builds a Docker image, and deploys — all in one long sequential job that takes 40 minutes. Developers wait a long time for feedback even on small changes. How would you restructure the pipeline to give faster feedback and reduce wasted work?", "a": "Split into stages/parallel jobs: run lint and unit tests first and in parallel so failures surface fast, then build only if tests pass, then deploy. Cache dependencies and Docker layers between runs. Run fast checks on every push and heavier integration/deploy steps only on main. Use fail-fast and only rebuild what changed to cut feedback time."}, {"id": 213, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team wants zero-downtime database schema migrations in your CI/CD pipeline, but currently deploys briefly break because new app code expects a column that the migration adds at the same time. How would you restructure the process?", "a": "Use the expand-and-contract pattern. First deploy a backward-compatible migration (add nullable column) that old and new code both tolerate. Then deploy app code that writes/reads the new column. Once all instances run new code, run a contract migration to remove old columns/backfill. Decouple migration steps from app deploy in the pipeline so schema changes are always compatible with both versions running simultaneously."}, {"id": 219, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your GitLab CI pipeline deploys to production on every merge to main, but the team now wants a manual approval gate before prod and automatic deploys to staging. They also want the same build artifact promoted, not rebuilt for prod. How would you restructure the pipeline?", "a": "Build the artifact/image once in an early stage and store it (registry with an immutable tag or SHA). Auto-deploy that same artifact to staging. Add a separate prod deploy job set to when: manual (or a protected environment with required approvers) that only promotes the already-built image—no rebuild. Use environment protection and only allow authorized users to trigger the prod job, ensuring build-once-deploy-many consistency."}, {"id": 222, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team stores application secrets (DB passwords, API keys) as plaintext environment variables in the GitHub Actions workflow YAML. Security flagged this. You need to inject secrets into deploys securely without exposing them in logs or the repo. How would you redesign this?", "a": "Move secrets out of YAML into GitHub Actions encrypted secrets (or an external vault like AWS Secrets Manager/HashiCorp Vault) referenced via ${{ secrets.NAME }}. GitHub masks these in logs. For AWS, use OIDC to assume a role and pull secrets at runtime instead of storing long-lived keys. Rotate the already-exposed secrets, restrict secret access by environment, and avoid echoing them in scripts."}, {"id": 231, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your team's Jenkins pipeline has started failing on the 'checkout' stage with an error about the Git credentials being invalid, but the same repo clones fine from your laptop using your personal token. The pipeline hasn't changed in weeks. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Check if the Jenkins credential (PAT/deploy key) expired or was rotated — most managed tokens have expiry. Look at the exact Git error in console output. Verify the credential ID referenced in the pipeline still exists and maps to a valid secret. Generate a new token/deploy key with correct repo scope, update the Jenkins credential, and re-run. Prefer machine/deploy keys or a bot account with long-lived, scoped access over personal tokens."}, {"id": 236, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer reports that after a git push, the CI job clones the repo but fails at a build step with 'permission denied' when running a shell script (./build.sh). The script exists in the repo and runs fine on their laptop. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "The script likely lost its executable bit. Check git ls-files -s build.sh; mode 100644 means not executable. Fix by chmod +x build.sh && git update-index --chmod=+x build.sh then commit, or invoke it as bash build.sh in the pipeline. Ensure core.filemode isn't hiding the change. Verify on a fresh clone."}, {"id": 242, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your GitHub Actions CI reruns identical dependency installation and builds on every push, making pipelines slow and expensive. The same dependencies rarely change. How would you speed up the pipeline?", "a": "Add dependency caching with actions/cache (or setup-* built-in caching), keyed on a lockfile hash (e.g. package-lock.json) so cache invalidates only when deps change. Use Docker layer caching (buildx + registry/gha cache) for image builds. Split into parallel jobs and only run affected stages via path filters. Cache build outputs where safe. Result: cache hits skip reinstalls, cutting runtime significantly."}, {"id": 254, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After merging a change, your GitHub Actions workflow that deploys to production ran automatically on a feature branch push and nearly deployed untested code. You need to ensure prod deploys only happen on main merges and require approval. How would you restructure this?", "a": "Scope the deploy trigger to on: push: branches: [main] or to release tags, not all branches. Split build/test from deploy into separate jobs with needs. Use a GitHub Environment named 'production' with required reviewers for a manual approval gate. Restrict who can approve. Separate staging (auto) from prod (gated) jobs."}, {"id": 264, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your CI/CD pipeline builds and tests fine, but deployments to production occasionally succeed in the pipeline while the app is actually broken for users. There's no verification after deploy. How would you add confidence that a deploy is truly healthy before declaring success?", "a": "Add post-deploy smoke tests and health checks against the live environment (hit key endpoints, check readiness). Use a progressive rollout (canary/rolling) with automated metric checks on error rate and latency, and auto-rollback on failure. Gate the pipeline's 'success' on these verifications rather than just the deploy command exit code."}, {"id": 268, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A teammate asks you to roll back a merged change in your Git repo that introduced a bug in production, but other important commits were made after it on main. You must not lose the newer work. How would you safely undo just the bad change?", "a": "Use git revert <bad-commit-sha> which creates a new commit that undoes those changes while preserving history and later commits. For a merge commit, use git revert -m 1 <sha>. Avoid git reset --hard on a shared branch since it rewrites history and loses later work. Push the revert, verify in a pipeline, then deploy."}, {"id": 285, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "You are a mid-level engineer. Your CI/CD pipeline uses a 'docker build' step that is consistently failing on an 'npm install' step only in the CI environment, but not on developer machines. How do you troubleshoot this difference?", "a": "Check the CI runner's resource limits; it may be hitting memory caps. Verify the 'npm' cache settings and ensure the CI environment has appropriate egress access to the registry (as it may be restricted vs local machines). Inspect the Dockerfile for hidden dependencies like 'git' or build-essential packages that exist on dev laptops but are missing from the CI runner's base image."}, {"id": 338, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A GitHub Actions runner is failing because it cannot pull a private Docker image. How do you debug the authentication flow without exposing credentials?", "a": "First, verify the secret scope in the repository settings. Then, add a temporary 'docker login' step in the pipeline using 'echo $SECRET | docker login ... --password-stdin' to test authentication. Check the runner logs to confirm if the error is an 'access denied' (permissions) or 'connection timeout' (network)."}, {"id": 350, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After merging a change, your CI/CD pipeline built and pushed a Docker image tagged 'latest', but the Kubernetes cluster didn't pull the new image on rollout—pods still run old code despite a successful deploy. Why, and how would you fix this reliably?", "a": "With the mutable 'latest' tag and imagePullPolicy behavior, Kubernetes may reuse the cached image since the tag string is unchanged. Best fix: use immutable, unique tags per build (e.g., git SHA) so each deploy references a new image, forcing a pull and clean rollout. As a stopgap, set imagePullPolicy: Always, but unique tags also improve rollback and traceability."}, {"id": 357, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team wants zero-downtime database schema migrations during CI/CD deploys. Currently the migration runs as part of app startup, and during a rolling update old and new pods run simultaneously against the same DB, causing errors when the new schema breaks old code. How would you structure migrations to avoid this?", "a": "Use backward-compatible, expand/contract migrations. Phase 1: additive changes (new columns/tables) that old code ignores. Deploy new code that uses both. Phase 2: after all pods run new code, run contract migration to remove old columns. Run migrations as a separate step/Job, not on app startup, so ordering is controlled and both app versions stay compatible during rollout."}, {"id": 362, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team wants CI builds to run faster. The pipeline reinstalls all npm dependencies from scratch on every run, adding 4-5 minutes each time, even when package.json hasn't changed. How would you speed this up in your CI/CD tool?", "a": "Add dependency caching keyed on the lockfile hash (e.g. package-lock.json). In GitHub Actions use actions/cache or setup-node's built-in cache with a key like hash of the lockfile. On cache hit, deps restore instantly; on miss, they reinstall and repopulate the cache. This avoids redundant downloads while auto-invalidating when dependencies actually change."}, {"id": 371, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your team's Jenkins pipeline deploys artifacts to a server over SSH, but the deploy stage fails with 'Host key verification failed' whenever it runs on a freshly provisioned build agent, though it works on the old agent. How would you diagnose and fix this so deploys work on any agent?", "a": "The new agent has no entry for the target host in its known_hosts, so SSH refuses. Fix by adding the host key ahead of time: run ssh-keyscan target-host >> ~/.ssh/known_hosts in a setup step, or use a managed known_hosts credential. Avoid disabling StrictHostKeyChecking blindly in prod as it opens MITM risk; prefer pre-seeding verified keys."}, {"id": 376, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer complains that after they push code, the GitHub Actions workflow runs but always uses a stale version of a helper script stored in the repo. They just committed a fix to that script, yet the pipeline still runs the old logic. The workflow uses actions/checkout. What would you check to ensure the pipeline runs the latest committed code?", "a": "Confirm the workflow triggered on the correct commit (check the run's commit SHA vs their push). Ensure actions/checkout isn't pinned to a ref/branch or using a cached path, and that fetch-depth is adequate. Check for caching steps restoring old files, and that the script isn't being overwritten by a cache or artifact. Verify they pushed to the branch the workflow listens on."}, {"id": 384, "cat": "CI/CD", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your CI/CD pipeline builds a Docker image, runs tests inside it, and pushes to ECR, but the push step intermittently fails with 'no basic auth credentials'. The AWS credentials in the pipeline are valid and other AWS calls succeed. What causes this and how do you fix it reliably?", "a": "Having valid AWS credentials isn't enough—Docker needs an ECR login token, which expires after 12 hours. Intermittent failures usually mean a stale/missing docker login. Fix by authenticating right before push: aws ecr get-login-password | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin <acct>.dkr.ecr.<region>.amazonaws.com. Ensure the IAM role has ECR push permissions and login runs in the same job as the push."}]}, {"name": "Terraform", "slug": "terraform", "icon": "🏗️", "color": "#7b42bc", "blurb": "State, modules, environments, drift, imports, safe applies.", "diagrams": [{"t": "Terraform workflow", "steps": ["Write .tf code", "terraform init", "terraform plan (review)", "terraform apply", "State updated (remote + lock)"]}, {"t": "Safe remote state", "steps": ["Pipeline A / Pipeline B", "S3 backend (encrypted, versioned)", "DynamoDB lock table", "One applies, other waits", "Separate state key per env"]}, {"t": "Plan wants to destroy a resource", "steps": ["Read plan: which attribute forces replace?", "Immutable field or renamed resource?", "Use moved block / state mv / lifecycle", "Backup state", "Re-plan until no destroy"]}], "cheats": [{"c": "terraform plan -out=tfplan", "d": "Save the exact plan to apply"}, {"c": "terraform state list / show", "d": "Inspect state"}, {"c": "terraform state mv", "d": "Rename without destroy"}, {"c": "terraform import", "d": "Bring existing resource into state"}, {"c": "terraform force-unlock <id>", "d": "Release a stuck lock (carefully)"}, {"c": "terraform fmt / validate", "d": "Format and syntax check"}, {"c": "terraform apply -target=...", "d": "Limited apply (use rarely)"}, {"c": "terraform workspace list", "d": "Workspaces"}], "follow": [{"k": ["state", "backend", "lock", "dynamodb", "s3"], "q": "Why remote state and what is state locking?", "a": "Remote state gives the team one source of truth and encryption. Locking, for example with DynamoDB or S3 native locking, prevents two applies from corrupting the state at the same time."}, {"k": ["destroy", "recreate", "replace", "forces"], "q": "How do you stop Terraform from destroying a critical resource?", "a": "prevent_destroy in lifecycle, careful plan review in CI, and restricted prod apply permissions. For a rename I use moved blocks or terraform state mv so it is not recreated."}, {"k": ["drift", "manual", "console", "changed"], "q": "Someone changed a resource manually in the console. How do you handle drift?", "a": "terraform plan shows the drift. I decide whether to revert it with apply or update the code to match. I also restrict console write access and run scheduled drift detection."}, {"k": ["module", "reuse", "environment", "workspace", "tfvars"], "q": "Workspaces or separate directories for environments?", "a": "Workspaces share code and are good for small cases, but are easy to confuse. For prod I prefer separate directories or backends per environment with shared modules and tfvars, so a wrong apply cannot touch another environment."}, {"k": ["import", "existing", "manual"], "q": "How do you bring existing infrastructure under Terraform?", "a": "Write the resource block, run terraform import or an import block, then run plan until it shows no changes. I do this one resource at a time to avoid surprises."}, {"k": ["secret", "password", "sensitive", "tfstate"], "q": "Secrets in Terraform state: risk and fix?", "a": "State can contain secrets in plain text, so the backend must be encrypted with tight access. I mark variables sensitive, pull secrets from Secrets Manager or Vault, and avoid hardcoding them in code."}, {"k": ["count", "for_each", "loop"], "q": "count vs for_each?", "a": "count uses index, so removing a middle item shifts and recreates later resources. for_each uses stable keys, so it is safer for collections that change."}, {"k": ["lock", "stuck", "error"], "q": "Apply failed with state lock error. What do you do?", "a": "First check nobody is running an apply, the pipeline included. If the lock is stale, I use terraform force-unlock with the lock ID. Never unlock while a run is active."}], "edge": [{"q": "terraform plan shows changes on resources nobody touched. Why?", "a": "Provider version upgrade changing defaults, drift from manual or other tooling changes, computed attributes, or missing lifecycle ignore_changes for things like autoscaling desired count or tags added by AWS. I pin provider versions.", "fu": []}, {"q": "Your state file got deleted or corrupted. What now?", "a": "Restore from the S3 object version, which is why versioning is on. If lost, I rebuild state with terraform import for each resource, which is slow, so backups and versioning are mandatory.", "fu": []}, {"q": "Two modules need each other output, creating a circular dependency. How do you solve it?", "a": "Restructure so shared items such as a security group live in a third module or a lower layer, and pass IDs as variables. Layered stacks such as network, then data, then app avoid cycles.", "fu": []}, {"q": "Apply partially failed and half the infra exists. What do you do?", "a": "Do not panic or destroy. State records what was created, so I fix the cause, such as quota or permission, and rerun apply, which continues. If something is tainted I review before replacing it.", "fu": []}], "qa": [{"id": 7, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "General", "q": "A colleague pushed a change to a shared Terraform state, and now your local 'terraform plan' shows drift and unexpected changes to resources you didn't touch. The team uses a single state file with no locking. How do you handle this safely?", "a": "First run 'terraform plan' to understand the drift without applying. Coordinate with the team to freeze changes. Enable remote state with locking (S3 + DynamoDB) to prevent concurrent writes. Use 'terraform refresh'/'terraform state' commands to reconcile. Review who changed what via version control and state history. Only apply after confirming intended changes to avoid clobbering a teammate's work."}, {"id": 16, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "General", "q": "You manage a Terraform module for provisioning VPCs. A teammate ran 'terraform apply' and now the plan wants to change subnet CIDRs that no one edited. You suspect someone modified resources manually in the AWS console. How do you reconcile the actual infrastructure with your Terraform code without downtime?", "a": "Run 'terraform plan' to see the drift, then use 'terraform refresh'/plan to understand current state. For manual changes you want to keep, update the .tf code to match reality. Use 'terraform import' if resources exist outside state. Avoid blind apply—review each change, especially destructive ones. Communicate to enforce no manual changes going forward and consider drift detection."}, {"id": 33, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your company uses Terraform to manage AWS. A new engineer wants to test changes but is worried about accidentally affecting production infrastructure since everyone shares the same AWS account and state. How would you structure Terraform to safely separate environments?", "a": "Use separate state files per environment via workspaces or, better, separate backend configs/directories (dev, staging, prod). Store state remotely in S3 with DynamoDB locking. Parameterize with tfvars per environment. Consider separate AWS accounts for real isolation. Enforce plan review and least-privilege IAM. This prevents a dev apply from touching prod resources."}, {"id": 44, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your Terraform config provisions an S3 bucket and an IAM policy referencing it. On a fresh apply it fails with a dependency error because the policy is created before the bucket exists, but sometimes it works. How do you make the ordering reliable?", "a": "The intermittent behavior comes from Terraform's parallel resource creation without an explicit dependency. Reference the bucket's attribute (e.g., aws_s3_bucket.this.arn) directly in the policy so Terraform infers the dependency graph, or add an explicit 'depends_on'. Attribute references are preferred as they build a correct DAG automatically."}, {"id": 49, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "General", "q": "You manage Terraform for infra. After importing an existing manually-created load balancer into state, terraform plan still wants to make many changes to it even though nothing should change. How do you reconcile this?", "a": "Import only brings the resource into state, not its full configuration. The plan diff means your HCL doesn't match reality. Run 'terraform plan' to see each attribute drift, then update your resource block to match the actual settings (from console/CLI describe) until plan shows no changes. Iterate attribute by attribute. Consider 'terraform plan -refresh-only' to inspect current state."}, {"id": 54, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're deploying infrastructure with Terraform across a team, but two engineers ran apply at the same time and corrupted the state file, causing conflicting resources. How would you prevent this from happening again?", "a": "Move state to a remote backend like S3 with DynamoDB state locking (or Terraform Cloud). Locking prevents concurrent applies. Enable versioning on the S3 bucket so you can recover prior state. Standardize on the remote backend for all team members and never keep state local for shared infra."}, {"id": 71, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "General", "q": "You maintain a Terraform config that provisions EC2 instances. A colleague adds a new instance and runs 'terraform apply', but it fails saying the resource already exists in AWS. The instance was actually created manually last week to match a spec. How do you bring it under Terraform management without recreating it?", "a": "Use 'terraform import aws_instance.name i-xxxx' to map the existing instance into state. Then run 'terraform plan' and adjust the HCL config until the plan shows no changes, so Terraform's code matches reality. Never delete/recreate. Commit the config and communicate to the team so no more manual instances are made."}, {"id": 84, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your team manages AWS with Terraform. A new security requirement says all EC2 instances must have a specific tag. Rather than checking manually after each apply, you want to enforce this so non-compliant configs fail before deploy. How would you implement this?", "a": "Add a policy-as-code check in the pipeline: run 'terraform plan -out' then evaluate with a tool like OPA/Conftest or tfsec/Checkov against the plan JSON to reject resources missing the tag. Optionally use Terraform default_tags at the provider level to auto-apply org tags, and add validation. Fail the pipeline on violations before apply."}, {"id": 88, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your team runs a Terraform-managed VPC and needs to add a new private subnet for a database tier. A colleague worries that applying could disrupt existing running instances. How do you safely add the subnet and confirm no existing resources are affected?", "a": "Add the new subnet resource, then run 'terraform plan' and carefully review the output—it should show only the new subnet being created (+ add) with no changes/destroys to existing resources. If plan shows unexpected modifications, investigate before applying. Use targeted apply if needed. Version control the change and get it peer-reviewed via PR."}, {"id": 95, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your team runs a Terraform-managed set of resources, and after a colleague manually changed a security group rule in the AWS console, the next terraform plan shows it wants to revert the change. The colleague insists their change is needed. How do you resolve this drift correctly?", "a": "This is configuration drift. Don't just apply and overwrite. Decide the source of truth: if the manual change is valid, update the Terraform code (the security_group_rule) to match so plan is clean; then apply. If it was unauthorized, apply to restore desired state. Going forward, restrict console write access and use terraform plan in CI to detect drift early."}, {"id": 100, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "General", "q": "You maintain a Terraform module used by several teams. After you released a new module version, one team's terraform apply unexpectedly wants to destroy and recreate their database because you renamed a resource block internally. They're panicking about data loss. How do you avoid the destroy and safely ship the rename?", "a": "Renaming a resource block changes its address, so Terraform sees the old one as removed and plans a destroy/create. Prevent this with moved {} blocks in the module (or terraform state mv) to map the old address to the new one so Terraform treats it as a rename, not a replacement. Also add prevent_destroy on stateful resources as a safety net and communicate the upgrade path clearly."}, {"id": 105, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your team provisions an EC2 instance with Terraform and uses a user_data script to install and configure the app on boot. A teammate updated the user_data script and ran apply, expecting the change to take effect, but the running instance still uses the old setup. Why, and how do you apply it?", "a": "user_data only runs at first boot; changing it doesn't re-run on existing instances. By default Terraform may not even replace the instance unless user_data_replace_on_change is set. To apply, either set user_data_replace_on_change=true (or taint the instance) so Terraform recreates it, or SSH in and run the new steps manually. Recreation may cause downtime, so plan accordingly."}, {"id": 115, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After applying a Terraform change to add a new tag to all EC2 instances, the plan shows it also wants to modify unrelated attributes like AMI and instance_type that no one changed. You're worried apply will replace instances. How do you investigate and apply only the tag safely?", "a": "Read the plan carefully to see if changes are in-place (~) or replacements (-/+). AMI/type drift often means the config drifted from real state or a data source resolved a new AMI. Run 'terraform plan' with -refresh, pin the AMI to a fixed ID or use lifecycle ignore_changes for AMI, and use -target on the tag change if urgent. Reconcile the config so plan is clean before broad apply."}, {"id": 122, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A Terraform apply failed halfway through creating a set of resources, leaving state that partially matches reality. Now every plan errors out or shows confusing diffs, and a teammate wants to just delete the state file and start over. Why is that dangerous, and how do you recover cleanly?", "a": "Deleting state orphans real resources — Terraform loses track and may try to recreate duplicates. Instead: run terraform plan to see the delta, use terraform state list to inspect what's tracked. Import any real-but-untracked resources with terraform import. Remove stale entries with terraform state rm if the resource was destroyed. Then re-run apply to converge. Always back up the state file first."}, {"id": 142, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your CI pipeline runs Terraform for multiple teams, but two pipelines occasionally apply at the same time and corrupt each other's changes because they share one state file with no locking. How would you make concurrent runs safe and isolate teams?", "a": "Use a remote backend that supports state locking, e.g. S3 backend with a DynamoDB lock table. Locking blocks concurrent applies so runs queue safely. Isolate teams/environments with separate state files via distinct backend keys or Terraform workspaces, so each pipeline manages its own state. Add plan-then-apply gating in CI to reduce conflicts."}, {"id": 155, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "After applying Terraform for a new EC2 instance, the apply succeeds but you realize you need to change the instance type. When you update the type and run apply, Terraform wants to destroy and recreate the instance, which would lose the data. How would you handle this?", "a": "Changing instance type is normally an in-place update, but destroy/recreate suggests a forces-new-resource attribute changed (like AMI). Run terraform plan and read the '# forces replacement' notes to see what triggers it. If only type changed, it updates in place after a stop/start. To protect data, ensure the root volume isn't deleted on termination, or move data to a separate EBS volume/EFS. Use lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true } on c…"}, {"id": 166, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer reports that after running terraform apply, their change didn't take effect because someone else's changes overwrote the state. They both keep local terraform.tfstate files on their laptops and share it via Slack. Why is this fragile, and how would you fix state management for a team?", "a": "Local state on laptops has no locking, no single source of truth, and gets out of sync — leading to overwrites and drift. Move state to a shared remote backend like S3 with DynamoDB for locking (or Terraform Cloud). This gives one authoritative state, prevents concurrent applies, and enables versioning/rollback. Remove local tfstate from Git and never share it manually."}, {"id": 174, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "You're a mid-level engineer managing infrastructure with Terraform. A teammate accidentally deleted an S3 bucket resource block, and the next apply plans to destroy the live bucket that holds production data. You catch it in the plan before applying. How would you keep the resource but stop Terraform from destroying it?", "a": "Don't apply the destructive plan. To protect it, add lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true } on the resource so Terraform errors instead of deleting. If the block was removed, restore it (from Git) so state and config match. Alternatively terraform state rm to stop managing it. Also enable S3 versioning and consider deletion protection for critical data."}, {"id": 179, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your CI/CD pipeline deploys a Terraform change to an AWS environment, but the apply hangs for 20+ minutes on creating an RDS instance and eventually times out. Re-running sometimes succeeds. Other resources apply quickly. How would you diagnose and make this reliable?", "a": "RDS creation legitimately takes 10-20+ min, so a short pipeline/Terraform timeout is the likely culprit. Set an appropriate timeouts block on the resource and increase the CI job timeout. Check whether a dependency (subnet group, parameter group) or capacity/quota issue delays provisioning. Review CloudTrail/RDS events for the actual bottleneck rather than assuming Terraform is stuck."}, {"id": 187, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your Terraform-managed AWS security group has drifted — someone added an inbound rule manually via the console during an incident. The next terraform plan wants to remove it, but the team wants to keep the change now that it's needed. How would you reconcile this safely?", "a": "Don't just apply and wipe it. Confirm the manual rule is legitimate, then codify it: add the corresponding ingress block to the Terraform config so plan shows no changes. Run terraform plan to verify zero diff, document the change, and consider drift detection (scheduled plans) and stricter console access to prevent unmanaged edits going forward."}, {"id": 192, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team's Terraform code provisions infra across dev, staging, and prod, but everyone edits the same directory and hardcodes values per environment, causing frequent mistakes when applying to the wrong environment. How would you restructure this for safe multi-environment management?", "a": "Separate state per environment using workspaces or, better, per-env directories/backends with distinct state files. Extract shared logic into reusable modules and pass env-specific values via tfvars files. Use separate backend configs (e.g. different S3 keys) so a wrong apply can't touch another env. Enforce plan review in CI and require explicit -var-file selection to prevent misapplies."}, {"id": 209, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your Terraform module creates an IAM role and an EC2 instance that uses it, but terraform apply intermittently fails with 'iam instance profile not found' when creating the instance. Re-running fixes it. How would you make this reliable?", "a": "It's a race/eventual-consistency issue: IAM instance profile propagation lags behind creation. Ensure Terraform knows the dependency by referencing the profile's attribute in the instance (implicit depends), or add depends_on = [aws_iam_instance_profile.this]. For propagation delay, add a time_sleep resource or retry. Best practice: reference aws_iam_instance_profile.name directly so the dependency graph enforces correct ordering."}, {"id": 224, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After a Terraform apply, a critical EC2 instance was replaced (destroyed and recreated) even though you only intended to change a tag. This caused unexpected downtime. How would you investigate why it replaced instead of updated, and prevent it in future?", "a": "Run terraform plan and read the plan carefully — replacements show '-/+' and list the attribute forcing replacement (e.g. changing a subnet, AMI, or user_data). Some attributes are 'ForceNew'. To prevent surprise downtime, always review plans in CI, use lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true } on critical resources, and create_before_destroy where recreation is unavoidable. Avoid touching force-new attributes casually."}, {"id": 233, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A teammate's Terraform apply fails with 'Error acquiring the state lock' and won't proceed. They say their previous apply was interrupted (laptop crashed). No one else is running Terraform. How would you resolve this safely?", "a": "The interrupted run left a stale lock in the backend (e.g. DynamoDB for S3 backend). First confirm nobody is actually applying. Read the lock ID/info from the error message, then run terraform force-unlock <LOCK_ID> to release it. Verify state integrity with terraform plan afterward. Avoid manually editing the lock table unless force-unlock fails. Going forward, ensure applies run in CI so interrupted local runs don't leave locks."}, {"id": 239, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team's Terraform state is stored in an S3 backend, but two engineers ran apply from different branches with different variable values, and now the deployed infra doesn't match anyone's code. Future plans show confusing diffs. How would you recover and prevent recurrence?", "a": "Run terraform plan to see drift, reconcile by picking the intended config and applying from a single source of truth (main branch). Enable state locking via DynamoDB to prevent concurrent applies. Enforce applies only through CI on merged main, not local runs. Use terraform state inspection and consider workspaces/separate state per env. Document a single apply path."}, {"id": 257, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your Terraform code manages an S3 bucket that another team manually configured last month with lifecycle rules and bucket policies. You need to bring it under Terraform management without destroying and recreating it or losing its existing config. How would you approach this?", "a": "Write the resource block matching the existing bucket, then run terraform import aws_s3_bucket.name bucket-name to add it to state without recreating it. Run terraform plan and reconcile the diffs—add lifecycle rules, policy, versioning etc. to code until plan shows no changes. Use terraform state show to see imported attributes. Commit once plan is clean so Terraform matches reality."}, {"id": 274, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A mid-level engineer realizes that a Terraform-managed production database cluster is performing poorly. The team suspects a manual index was added via the console, but applying the current Terraform code would overwrite it. How do you safely synchronize?", "a": "Do not edit state files manually. First, use 'terraform plan' to see the drift. Identify the missing index/configuration and update the Terraform code to match the desired state of the database including that index. Run a plan again to ensure the desired configuration is now correctly represented in your code and that the plan shows no further unexpected changes before applying."}, {"id": 281, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "You are a mid-level engineer. You notice a Terraform plan shows that an existing AWS RDS instance will be replaced because of a minor parameter group change. You know replacing the database will cause data loss. How do you prevent this while keeping the infrastructure as code?", "a": "Use 'terraform state rm' to remove the resource from the state file, then update the code to match the existing RDS configuration exactly. Run 'terraform import' to bring the existing database back under management without triggering a recreation. Finally, verify the plan shows 'no changes' before applying."}, {"id": 288, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A Terraform deployment is blocked because an AWS resource has a 'drifted' state. A teammate manually added a tag to a Security Group. How do you resolve this without causing a destructive replacement?", "a": "Run 'terraform plan' to see the exact delta. If the drift is just tags or non-destructive properties, update your Terraform configuration to match the manual changes exactly. This brings the state in line without destruction. If the change was truly manual and unauthorized, ensure you have team alignment before 'terraform import' or removing the drift to avoid future conflicts."}, {"id": 295, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You are a junior engineer. You need to test if a Terraform script will successfully provision an AWS EC2 instance without actually modifying the production environment. What specific command do you use, and what should you look for in the output?", "a": "Use 'terraform plan'. Review the output for the 'Plan: X to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy' summary. Carefully inspect the resource attributes in the output to ensure that tags, AMI IDs, and instance sizes match your requirements. If the plan shows '1 to destroy' or '1 to replace' unexpectedly, stop and investigate the cause before applying."}, {"id": 305, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A Terraform plan indicates a change to an AWS Security Group, but you suspect this will break existing production connectivity. How do you safely validate this?", "a": "Never apply blindly. Use 'terraform plan -out=tfplan' and 'terraform show -json tfplan' to inspect the specific attributes changing. Compare the current state with the AWS Console or use 'aws ec2 describe-security-group-rules' to identify dependencies. Check the application logs for current traffic patterns. If unsure, update the Terraform code to import the current state or add a lifecycle 'prevent_destroy' block."}, {"id": 307, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A CI/CD pipeline using Terraform fails to apply changes because a resource in AWS is locked by an unknown process. How do you safely identify the source of the lock without risking data corruption?", "a": "Terraform state locks are managed in the backend (e.g., S3/DynamoDB). Check the DynamoDB table associated with the state file to identify the 'LockID' and the 'Info' field containing the process ID and hostname. Verify if a colleague has a running job, or if it is a stale lock from a previous failed run. Never delete the entry manually without ensuring no Terraform process is actively writing to the state."}, {"id": 315, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A CI/CD pipeline fails to run a Terraform plan because the state file is locked. What should you do before attempting to force-unlock the state?", "a": "Do not use 'terraform force-unlock' immediately. First, coordinate with the team to ensure no other engineer is actively running a plan or apply. Check the CI/CD dashboard for abandoned or hung pipeline jobs that might still hold the lock. Only after confirming the lock is stale and no process is actually modifying infrastructure, use the command to release the lock to prevent corruption."}, {"id": 319, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your Terraform plan shows that an existing AWS IAM role will be recreated, which would detach it from all EC2 instances. How do you verify the configuration difference and prevent the recreation?", "a": "Review the 'terraform plan' output carefully to see the 'forces replacement' indication. Check if the resource name was changed, as Terraform treats name changes as replacements. Use 'terraform show' to inspect the state versus your current code. If the change is necessary, use the 'lifecycle' block with 'prevent_destroy = true' in your code, or move the resource to a separate state file/module to prevent accidental modifications during standa…"}, {"id": 327, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A Terraform apply fails because a resource (e.g., an S3 bucket) is marked as already existing in the state, but it was manually deleted in AWS. How do you safely synchronize the state without manual editing?", "a": "Do not edit the state file directly. First, run 'terraform plan' to confirm the drift. Use 'terraform import <resource_type>.<resource_name> <resource_id>' to re-map the existing AWS resource into the state, or if the resource is truly gone, remove the resource block from the Terraform code and run 'terraform apply' to reconcile the state. Always run 'terraform plan' again to verify the state matches the reality."}, {"id": 334, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A Terraform plan reports a change that will destroy and recreate an RDS database. How do you identify which specific attribute change is triggering this destructive action?", "a": "Run 'terraform plan' and inspect the output. Terraform explicitly labels destructive changes with a '-' and will note '# forces replacement'. Review the plan output to identify the attribute being modified. Compare the current Terraform code against the AWS Console settings to ensure the change is intentional before proceeding."}, {"id": 342, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A mid-level engineer observes that a Terraform plan shows a database instance will be 'replaced' instead of 'updated' due to an attribute change. How do you identify which specific attribute is causing the forced replacement?", "a": "Run 'terraform plan -out=tfplan' followed by 'terraform show -json tfplan | jq' to inspect the plan structure. Look for the 'resource_changes' block and check for a field named 'requires_replace'. This explicitly lists the attributes triggering the destruction. Reviewing this before applying prevents accidental data loss; if the attribute is critical, consider using 'lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }' in the resource block."}, {"id": 348, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A teammate's Terraform apply keeps failing with 'Error acquiring the state lock' even though no one else is running Terraform. They ran it earlier and it crashed midway. How do you diagnose and safely resolve the stuck lock?", "a": "The earlier crashed run left a stale lock in the DynamoDB/backend lock table. First confirm no active apply is running elsewhere. Then use terraform force-unlock <LOCK_ID> with the ID shown in the error. Never force-unlock while a real apply is in progress, as it can corrupt state. After unlocking, run terraform plan to verify state integrity before applying again."}, {"id": 354, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your Terraform code needs to create S3 buckets for three environments (dev, staging, prod) with identical config but different names. Right now you've copy-pasted the resource block three times. How would you make this DRY?", "a": "Use a single resource with for_each over a set/map of environment names, e.g. for_each = toset([\"dev\",\"staging\",\"prod\"]) and reference each.key in the bucket name. This creates all buckets from one block, keyed by environment, so changes apply consistently. For per-env differences, use a map of objects. Avoid count here since for_each gives stable, name-based addressing."}, {"id": 364, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team wants to safely test infrastructure changes before applying to production. Currently everyone runs 'terraform apply' directly against prod state, and a bad change once caused an outage. How would you introduce a safer workflow?", "a": "Separate state per environment (workspaces or separate backends/dirs). Enforce a plan-review workflow: run 'terraform plan' in CI on PRs and require approval before apply. Use remote state with locking (e.g. S3 + DynamoDB), restrict prod apply to a pipeline with manual gating, and consider policy checks (OPA/Sentinel) to block risky changes automatically."}, {"id": 367, "cat": "Terraform", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team's Terraform-managed AWS resources were manually changed in the console during an incident (a security group rule was added by hand). Now the next plan wants to remove that rule, but the team needs to keep it. How would you reconcile the drift without losing the manual change?", "a": "This is config drift. Run terraform plan to see the diff. To keep the manual change, codify it: add the security group rule to your Terraform config so it matches reality, then plan/apply shows no change. Alternatively use terraform import if it's a new resource. Never blindly apply, which would revert the manual fix. Long-term, block console changes and enforce IaC."}]}, {"name": "AWS", "slug": "aws", "icon": "☁️", "color": "#ff9900", "blurb": "EC2, VPC, IAM, S3, RDS, ALB. Networking and permissions are the favourite scenarios.", "diagrams": [{"t": "VPC layout: public vs private", "steps": ["Internet", "Internet Gateway", "Public subnet (ALB, NAT, Bastion)", "Private subnet (App EC2 / EKS)", "Private DB subnet (RDS)"]}, {"t": "EC2 unreachable: layer by layer", "steps": ["Instance running + status checks", "Security Group port/source", "NACL in + out", "Route table (IGW / NAT)", "App listening? (ss -tlnp)", "ALB target health"]}, {"t": "IAM access denied analysis", "steps": ["Identify caller (sts get-caller-identity)", "Identity policy allows?", "Resource policy / bucket policy deny?", "SCP / permission boundary?", "Policy Simulator / CloudTrail"]}], "cheats": [{"c": "aws sts get-caller-identity", "d": "Who am I right now"}, {"c": "aws ec2 describe-instances", "d": "Instance details"}, {"c": "aws s3 ls / sync", "d": "S3 operations"}, {"c": "aws logs tail <group> --follow", "d": "Live CloudWatch logs"}, {"c": "aws ssm start-session --target i-xxx", "d": "Shell without SSH or open port 22"}, {"c": "aws iam simulate-principal-policy", "d": "Test IAM permissions"}, {"c": "aws rds describe-db-instances", "d": "RDS endpoints and status"}, {"c": "aws elbv2 describe-target-health", "d": "ALB target health reason"}], "follow": [{"k": ["security group", "nacl", "timeout", "connect", "5432", "port"], "q": "Security Group vs NACL - what is the difference?", "a": "Security Groups are stateful, attach to ENIs and only allow rules. NACLs are stateless, attach to subnets, support allow and deny, and need both inbound and outbound rules including ephemeral ports. Timeouts usually mean one of these is blocking."}, {"k": ["private", "nat", "internet", "subnet"], "q": "How does a private subnet instance reach the internet?", "a": "Through a NAT Gateway placed in a public subnet. The private route table sends 0.0.0.0/0 to the NAT, which uses an Elastic IP and the IGW. For AWS services I prefer VPC endpoints to save NAT cost."}, {"k": ["iam", "role", "policy", "denied", "403", "access"], "q": "IAM user vs role - when do you use each?", "a": "Roles for anything running in AWS such as EC2, Lambda, EKS pods via IRSA, and for CI with OIDC, because credentials are temporary. IAM users with long-lived keys only when unavoidable, and I rotate and avoid them."}, {"k": ["s3", "bucket", "403", "encrypt", "public"], "q": "How do you secure an S3 bucket?", "a": "Block Public Access, least-privilege bucket policy, encryption with SSE-S3 or KMS, versioning, access logging, and access only through IAM roles or VPC endpoints."}, {"k": ["alb", "target", "health", "unhealthy", "elb", "502", "503"], "q": "ALB shows targets unhealthy though the app runs. Why?", "a": "Health check path, port or expected status code is wrong, the instance security group does not allow the ALB security group, or the app is slow and times out. I read the failure reason in the target group health details."}, {"k": ["rds", "database", "db", "multi-az", "replica"], "q": "Multi-AZ vs Read Replica in RDS?", "a": "Multi-AZ is for high availability with synchronous standby and automatic failover, not for reads. Read replicas are asynchronous and used to scale reads, and can be promoted manually."}, {"k": ["cost", "bill", "expensive"], "q": "AWS bill suddenly doubled. How do you investigate?", "a": "Cost Explorer grouped by service, then by usage type and tags to spot the jump. Usual suspects are NAT data transfer, unattached EBS or idle EC2, large S3 requests, and CloudWatch logs. Then I set Budgets alerts."}, {"k": ["autoscaling", "asg", "scale", "ec2"], "q": "How does an Auto Scaling Group decide to scale and replace instances?", "a": "Scaling policies on CloudWatch metrics such as CPU or request count per target, and health checks replace unhealthy instances. I use ELB health checks so unhealthy app instances are replaced, not only dead ones."}, {"k": ["ssh", "bastion", "ssm", "access"], "q": "How do you access private instances without opening SSH?", "a": "AWS Systems Manager Session Manager. No inbound port, IAM controlled, and fully logged in CloudTrail and optionally S3 or CloudWatch."}], "edge": [{"q": "App in a private subnet suddenly cannot call an external API; it worked yesterday. What do you check?", "a": "NAT Gateway state and its route in the private route table, NAT in the right AZ, security group outbound rules, NACLs, and whether the external service changed IP or blocked our NAT Elastic IP. Then DNS resolution.", "fu": [{"q": "NAT gateway failed in one AZ, what is the design fix?", "a": "One NAT per AZ with AZ-specific route tables, so one AZ failing does not take down others."}]}, {"q": "An access key was committed to a public GitHub repo. What are your steps?", "a": "Immediately deactivate the key, rotate it, and check CloudTrail for any use from unknown IPs. Remove the secret from the repo history, review what permissions it had, and then add secret scanning with pre-commit or GitHub secret scanning. Preferably move to roles.", "fu": [{"q": "Why is deleting the commit not enough?", "a": "Because it stays in history, forks and caches, and bots scrape within minutes, so the key must be treated as compromised."}]}, {"q": "Deployment fails with AccessDenied but the role policy looks correct. What else can block it?", "a": "Resource policy with explicit deny, SCP at organisation level, permission boundary, KMS key policy for encrypted resources, or a condition like source IP or MFA. Explicit deny always wins.", "fu": []}, {"q": "Your RDS runs out of storage at midnight. What do you do right away and long term?", "a": "Immediately increase allocated storage, which is online, and check what grew, for example logs or a runaway table. Long term enable storage autoscaling, set CloudWatch alarms at 80%, and archive old data.", "fu": []}, {"q": "You need zero-downtime deploy for an EC2 app behind an ALB. How?", "a": "Rolling or blue-green with an ASG: new instances register to the target group, pass health checks, and old ones drain with connection draining before termination. CodeDeploy or instance refresh can automate this.", "fu": [{"q": "How do you roll back?", "a": "Shift traffic back to the old target group or old launch template version, which is why I keep the previous version alive until validation passes."}]}], "qa": [{"id": 5, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A junior teammate accidentally pushed AWS access keys into a public GitHub repo. You're alerted before any misuse is confirmed. What immediate actions do you take?", "a": "Immediately deactivate/delete the exposed keys in IAM to stop misuse. Rotate to new keys and update any systems using them. Review CloudTrail for unauthorized activity during exposure. Remove the keys from git history (not just latest commit) and force-push, or rotate regardless since history persists. Enable secret scanning and adopt IAM roles/secrets manager to prevent recurrence."}, {"id": 12, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your team runs a static website on an S3 bucket served through CloudFront. After a recent content update, users still see the old version even hours later, while you see the new version. What's happening and how do you fix it?", "a": "CloudFront caches objects at edge locations based on TTL, so old content is still served until cache expires. Create a CloudFront invalidation (e.g. '/*' or specific paths) to purge cached objects. For the long term, use versioned filenames or set proper Cache-Control headers so updated assets get fresh URLs. Your browser may also be caching, but edge cache is the main cause."}, {"id": 20, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your team runs an app behind an AWS Application Load Balancer with an Auto Scaling Group. During a traffic spike, users get timeouts even though CloudWatch shows the ASG scaled out and new instances are running. How do you find why the new capacity isn't helping?", "a": "Check the ALB target group health—new instances may be failing health checks (wrong port/path, app not ready) and not receiving traffic. Verify security groups allow ALB-to-instance traffic. Confirm instances finished bootstrapping. Look at target registration and the health check grace period. Also check if a downstream dependency (DB, cache) is the real bottleneck."}, {"id": 24, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "General", "q": "You provisioned an EC2 instance in a new subnet via Terraform. The instance is running and has a public IP, but you can't SSH into it from your machine. Security group allows port 22. How do you diagnose the connectivity problem?", "a": "Check the subnet is public: it needs a route table with a route to an Internet Gateway. Verify the instance actually got a public IP and you're using it. Confirm the security group allows 22 from your source IP (not just default) and the NACL allows inbound 22 and ephemeral outbound. Ensure correct key pair and SSH user. Use 'nc -zv' to test reachability and check the instance's system log/status checks."}, {"id": 27, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "General", "q": "A developer asks you to grant their application running on EC2 access to an S3 bucket. They're currently hardcoding AWS access keys in the app config. What's the recommended approach and how would you implement it?", "a": "Avoid hardcoded keys. Attach an IAM role (instance profile) to the EC2 instance with a least-privilege policy allowing only the needed S3 actions on that bucket. The SDK automatically retrieves temporary credentials from instance metadata. Remove the static keys from config and rotate/revoke them. This eliminates long-lived secrets and auto-rotates credentials."}, {"id": 39, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your team runs an app in AWS behind an ALB. After enabling HTTPS with an ACM certificate, users on some pages get mixed-content warnings and some assets fail to load, though the site loads over HTTPS. How do you investigate and resolve this?", "a": "Mixed content means the page loads over HTTPS but references assets via http:// URLs. Check browser console for blocked resources. Fix hardcoded http links to https or protocol-relative/relative URLs. Ensure the app knows it's behind TLS via the X-Forwarded-Proto header so it generates https links. Configure the framework to trust the proxy and redirect http to https at the ALB."}, {"id": 47, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "General", "q": "A finance team's nightly ETL job runs on an EC2 Spot instance to save cost, but jobs keep failing halfway because the instance gets reclaimed. They want cost savings but reliable completion. How would you redesign this?", "a": "Spot interruptions are expected, so make the job idempotent and checkpointable so it can resume. Handle the 2-minute interruption notice to save state gracefully. Use a mix: Spot with an On-Demand fallback (e.g., ASG with mixed instances or capacity-optimized allocation), or run on Fargate Spot with retries. Persist intermediate results to S3 so restarts don't lose progress."}, {"id": 52, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're asked to set up an S3 bucket that hosts application backups, but a security review flags that the bucket is publicly accessible and unencrypted. You need to lock it down without breaking the app that writes to it. How would you approach this?", "a": "Enable Block Public Access at bucket and account level, remove any public bucket policy/ACL. Turn on default SSE (SSE-S3 or SSE-KMS) encryption. Grant the app write access via an IAM role/policy scoped to the bucket ARN, not public access. Enable versioning for backup safety and test the app can still put/get objects."}, {"id": 56, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "General", "q": "You manage a fleet of EC2 web servers behind an ALB. Marketing is running a campaign that will 10x traffic at a scheduled time tonight. Your ASG scales on CPU, but scaling takes ~5 minutes and you're worried early users will hit unhealthy capacity. How would you prepare so the app handles the spike from the first minute?", "a": "Use scheduled scaling to pre-warm the ASG before the known event, setting desired/min capacity ahead of time. Combine with target-tracking policies for organic bursts. Pre-warm/verify ALB and confirm health checks and warm-up time. Load test beforehand. Consider connection draining and predictive scaling if traffic patterns repeat."}, {"id": 67, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "General", "q": "A developer reports their AWS Lambda function times out intermittently when calling an RDS database inside a VPC. It works sometimes, fails other times, and the errors spike under load. How do you investigate?", "a": "Check if the Lambda is exhausting DB connections (no pooling / new connection per invocation) or ENI limits in the subnet. Verify subnet has enough free IPs and NAT for outbound. Look at RDS max_connections and CloudWatch DatabaseConnections. Fix with RDS Proxy or connection reuse, right-size concurrency, and ensure security groups/subnets are correct."}, {"id": 79, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "General", "q": "You manage an AWS environment and get an alert that CloudWatch billing has spiked significantly this month, but no one deployed major new infrastructure. You need to find the source of the unexpected cost quickly. How do you investigate?", "a": "Open AWS Cost Explorer and group costs by service, then by usage type and region to find the spike. Check for surprises like NAT Gateway data transfer, unattached EBS volumes/EIPs, forgotten instances, or high S3/data egress. Use Cost Anomaly Detection and resource tags to attribute spend. Once identified, remediate the resource and set a budget alert to catch future spikes early."}, {"id": 89, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're on call and get paged that an application on an EC2 instance is unreachable. You can't SSH in either, though the instance shows 'running' in the console. How do you diagnose and recover access?", "a": "Check the AWS console: instance status checks (system vs instance), CloudWatch metrics for CPU/disk, and the EC2 serial console or 'Get system log' to see boot/OS errors. Verify security groups and NACLs allow SSH and app ports. If the OS is hung (disk full, kernel panic), reboot; if that fails, stop/start to move to new hardware. Consider SSM Session Manager as an alternate access path."}, {"id": 97, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your team's AWS bill shows a large NAT Gateway data-processing charge. Instances in private subnets pull container images and OS updates through the NAT Gateway constantly. A senior asks you to reduce this cost without removing internet access those instances need. What options would you explore?", "a": "NAT Gateways charge per GB processed, so heavy egress to AWS services is costly. Add VPC Gateway/Interface Endpoints (S3, ECR, etc.) so that traffic stays on the AWS network and bypasses NAT. Cache/mirror frequently pulled images (e.g., ECR pull-through cache). Confirm which destinations drive traffic via VPC Flow Logs, then route AWS-service traffic through endpoints while keeping NAT only for genuine internet needs."}, {"id": 108, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your ALB health checks are marking healthy EC2 targets as unhealthy right after deploys, causing brief 502s for users. The app takes about 90 seconds to warm up on boot, but instances register into the target group immediately. How would you stop the premature failures?", "a": "Increase the ALB health check settings so warm-up is tolerated: raise HealthyThreshold and Interval, and use a lightweight health path. Configure a deregistration/registration delay and enable target group health check 'slow start' or set the ASG health check grace period (~120s) so instances aren't judged before ready. Ideally add a readiness endpoint that only returns 200 once the app is fully warm."}, {"id": 111, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team's ECS/EC2 service scaled up during a traffic spike, but new tasks failed to pull the container image with a 'CannotPullContainerError' referencing your private ECR repo. Existing running tasks are fine. Product wants capacity restored. How do you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Check the task execution role has ecr:GetAuthorizationToken and ecr:BatchGetImage/GetDownloadUrlForLayer permissions, plus CloudWatch logs perms. Confirm private subnets can reach ECR (NAT Gateway, or ECR/S3 VPC endpoints). Verify the image tag exists in ECR and the region matches. Fix the IAM policy or endpoint/routing, then force a new deployment to reschedule tasks."}, {"id": 119, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "You're a mid-level engineer running a Terraform-managed S3 bucket and CloudFront distribution. After adding a new bucket policy, users start getting 403 errors on some objects served through CloudFront. The bucket itself is private. How do you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Check that CloudFront's Origin Access Identity/Control is granted read access in the bucket policy, and that your new policy didn't override or deny it. Verify object-level permissions and that objects aren't blocked by a Deny statement. Review CloudFront cache behaviors and origin settings. Restore/merge the OAC permissions into the policy and invalidate the cache to retest."}, {"id": 130, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your team's S3-hosted static website suddenly serves stale content — users see the old version even after you uploaded new files and confirmed they're in the bucket. How would you figure out why the update isn't visible?", "a": "Almost certainly caching. If served via CloudFront, the edge cache still holds old objects — create an invalidation for the changed paths (or /*). Also check browser cache and object Cache-Control/max-age headers. Confirm you uploaded to the correct bucket/prefix and that CloudFront origin points there. Going forward, use versioned filenames or shorter TTLs to avoid stale content."}, {"id": 132, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After enabling a blue/green deployment on your app behind an ALB, you notice occasional 504 Gateway Timeout errors during the traffic cutover, even though both target groups show healthy targets. Users hit errors for a few seconds. How would you diagnose and reduce these timeouts during cutover?", "a": "504s at cutover usually mean in-flight requests hit instances being drained or the ALB idle/response timeout is shorter than backend response time. Check deregistration delay (connection draining) on the target group — set it long enough for requests to finish. Ensure the app handles SIGTERM gracefully and finishes active requests before exit. Also verify ALB idle timeout vs backend keepalive. Increase draining, add graceful shutdown, and conf…"}, {"id": 134, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After a Terraform apply that added a new IAM role and attached policies, your CI pipeline's assume-role step intermittently fails with an AccessDenied error, but retrying a minute later succeeds. The policy is correct. How do you explain and handle this?", "a": "This is IAM eventual consistency — newly created roles/policies take a short time to propagate globally, so an immediate assume-role can fail before the change is visible. Don't 'fix' the policy; it's timing. Add a short wait or retry with backoff after role creation, use Terraform depends_on to order creation, or have the pipeline retry the assume-role. For automation, build in retry logic rather than assuming a permanent permissions bug."}, {"id": 138, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After moving an application from a single large EC2 instance to an Auto Scaling Group behind an ALB, users report they keep getting logged out randomly. Login works but sessions don't persist across requests. How do you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Sessions are likely stored in local instance memory, so requests routed to a different instance lose the session. Confirm by checking session storage config. Fix by externalizing sessions to a shared store like Redis/ElastiCache or DynamoDB, or as a stopgap enable ALB sticky sessions (session affinity). Externalized sessions are the scalable, resilient solution."}, {"id": 147, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team runs a fleet of EC2 instances behind an ALB. After a spike, CloudWatch shows the Auto Scaling Group launched new instances, but the ALB never routes traffic to them and they sit idle while existing instances are overloaded. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Check that the ASG is attached to the ALB's target group and that new instances register automatically. Then check target health — new instances likely fail health checks because the app hasn't finished booting. Tune the health check path/thresholds and set an ASG health check grace period so instances aren't marked unhealthy prematurely. Confirm security groups allow ALB-to-instance health check traffic."}, {"id": 152, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team's AWS bill jumped unexpectedly last month. Finance wants to know which service and which team caused it, but resources aren't consistently tagged. How would you investigate the spike and prevent recurrence?", "a": "Use Cost Explorer to break down spend by service, region, and time to find the spike source; enable AWS Cost Anomaly Detection for alerts. Check CloudTrail for who launched the resources. Going forward, enforce a tagging policy (team, env, owner) via AWS Organizations tag policies and SCPs, activate cost allocation tags, and set up Budgets with alerts. Consider Trusted Advisor/Compute Optimizer to spot idle or oversized resources."}, {"id": 157, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team's AWS ECS/EC2 app fetches secrets like DB passwords from environment variables hardcoded in the task definition. Security wants secrets removed from plaintext config but the app still needs them at runtime. How would you manage these secrets securely?", "a": "Store secrets in AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store (SecureString). Reference them in the ECS task definition's secrets block by ARN so ECS injects them at runtime without plaintext in config. Grant the task execution role least-privilege access to those secrets via IAM, and enable rotation. This keeps secrets out of git and task JSON."}, {"id": 168, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague can SSH to a new EC2 instance from the office but not from home. The security group allows SSH (port 22) from 0.0.0.0/0 and the instance has a public IP. From home they get 'connection timed out'. How would you diagnose this?", "a": "Timeout (not refused) points to network-layer blocking. Check the subnet's Network ACL — NACLs are stateless, so both inbound port 22 and outbound ephemeral ports (1024-65535) must be allowed. Also verify their home network/ISP isn't blocking outbound 22. Confirm the route table has an internet gateway route. Test with nc -zv <ip> 22 to isolate. Fix the NACL or use Session Manager to avoid open SSH."}, {"id": 172, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team runs an app on AWS behind an ALB. During a traffic spike, some users get 504 Gateway Timeout errors while backend instances show healthy CPU and memory. How would you investigate the source of the 504s?", "a": "504 means the ALB didn't get a response within its idle timeout. Check ALB access logs and CloudWatch TargetResponseTime. Likely causes: slow backend requests exceeding the ALB idle timeout, backend thread/connection pool exhaustion, or slow downstream dependencies (DB/external API). Fix by tuning timeouts, scaling backend concurrency, adding caching, or optimizing slow queries."}, {"id": 177, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team runs an app on AWS. A CloudWatch alarm fires that an SQS queue's message age is growing rapidly, but the consumer EC2 instances show low CPU and no errors in logs. Messages are piling up and processing is falling behind. How would you diagnose and fix this backlog?", "a": "Check consumer polling: are enough workers polling? Verify visibility timeout isn't causing reprocessing. Look at ApproximateNumberOfMessages and receive/delete rates. Low CPU with backlog suggests too few consumers or throttled downstream calls. Scale consumers (Auto Scaling on queue depth), increase batch size/concurrency, and check for a poison-pill message stuck in a loop; add a dead-letter queue."}, {"id": 182, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team runs a Node.js app on AWS Auto Scaling behind an ALB. Deploys are done by baking a new AMI, but each deploy takes 25+ minutes because you rebuild the full AMI from scratch and instances take long to boot and pass health checks. Product wants faster, safer rollouts. How would you improve deploy speed and safety?", "a": "Speed up AMI bakes by starting from a pre-baked base image with the OS and dependencies (golden AMI + Packer layering), so deploys only add app code. Use immutable deploys via ASG instance refresh or a rolling launch-template update with health check grace periods tuned realistically. Consider CodeDeploy for in-place/blue-green with automatic rollback on alarms. Pre-warm instances and cache dependencies to cut boot time. Add canary/health gati…"}, {"id": 189, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team's staging and production share one AWS account, and a misconfigured CI job accidentally deployed a test workload that terminated production instances. Leadership wants to prevent cross-environment blast radius. How would you redesign this?", "a": "Separate environments into distinct AWS accounts under an Organization so IAM boundaries prevent cross-env actions. Use per-environment IAM roles with least privilege, scoped CI credentials (OIDC), and SCPs to block destructive actions. Add resource tagging, separate Terraform state/workspaces per env, and require approvals for prod deploys. This isolates blast radius structurally, not just by convention."}, {"id": 197, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team runs a service on AWS behind CloudFront + S3 for static assets. After a deploy, users complain they still see the old version of the JavaScript files even after refreshing, while a few users see the new one. How would you diagnose and fix this so all users get the new assets reliably?", "a": "This is CDN/browser caching. CloudFront serves cached objects until TTL expires. Diagnose via response headers (X-Cache, Age, ETag). Fix immediately with a CloudFront invalidation on the changed paths. Long-term, use content-hashed/fingerprinted filenames (e.g. app.a1b2c3.js) with long cache TTLs so new deploys reference new URLs, and set short/no-cache on index.html."}, {"id": 202, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your AWS Lambda function behind API Gateway intermittently returns errors during traffic spikes, with logs showing 'Rate Exceeded' and throttling. The function itself runs fine at low volume. How would you diagnose and resolve the throttling?", "a": "Check Lambda concurrency: default account limit is 1000 shared across functions; throttles when reserved/unreserved concurrency is exhausted. In CloudWatch, view Throttles and ConcurrentExecutions metrics. Fix by requesting a concurrency limit increase, setting reserved concurrency for the critical function, and enabling provisioned concurrency for predictable spikes. Add SQS buffering or exponential backoff retries for graceful degradation."}, {"id": 207, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your CI pipeline pushes Docker images to AWS ECR, but the push step recently started failing with 'no basic auth credentials'. Nothing changed in the pipeline config, and it worked yesterday. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "ECR auth tokens expire after 12 hours. The pipeline likely caches a stale docker login or the login step runs before push in a separate job/runner. Re-authenticate right before push using aws ecr get-login-password | docker login. Verify the IAM role/instance profile still has ecr:GetAuthorizationToken and push permissions. Ensure the login and push happen in the same job/session so credentials aren't lost."}, {"id": 215, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your AWS application in a private subnet needs outbound internet access to call a third-party API, but requests time out. Instances in the public subnet reach the API fine. How would you diagnose and fix the outbound connectivity for the private subnet?", "a": "Private subnets have no direct internet route. Check the route table: it needs a 0.0.0.0/0 route to a NAT Gateway (in a public subnet) — not an Internet Gateway. Verify the NAT Gateway exists, is in a public subnet with an IGW route, and the private subnet's route table points to it. Also confirm security groups/NACLs allow outbound HTTPS. Fix by adding/repairing the NAT route."}, {"id": 223, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague reports that after resizing an EBS volume in the AWS console from 20GB to 50GB, the Linux instance's filesystem still shows only 20GB available. The volume shows 50GB in AWS. How would you make the extra space usable?", "a": "AWS only grew the block device, not the partition/filesystem. Check with lsblk and df -h. Grow the partition with growpart /dev/xvda 1 (if partitioned), then extend the filesystem: resize2fs /dev/xvda1 for ext4 or xfs_growfs / for XFS. Re-run df -h to confirm the 50GB is now available. No reboot needed."}, {"id": 227, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team wants blue-green deployments on AWS behind an ALB, but currently a rolling deploy occasionally serves errors during cutover and rollback takes minutes. You need instant switch and instant rollback. How would you design this?", "a": "Run two identical target groups (blue and green) behind one ALB listener. Deploy the new version to the idle group, run health checks and smoke tests against it via a test listener/host. Cut over by switching the listener's default action / weighted target groups to green. Rollback is instant — point the listener back to blue. Keep the old group warm until confidence is high, then scale it down."}, {"id": 230, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You need to give a script running on an EC2 instance permission to read from a specific S3 bucket, but a senior tells you not to put AWS access keys on the instance. How would you grant access securely?", "a": "Create an IAM role with a least-privilege policy allowing s3:GetObject (and ListBucket) on just that bucket's ARN. Attach the role to the EC2 instance via an instance profile. The AWS SDK/CLI on the instance automatically retrieves temporary credentials from the instance metadata service — no static keys needed. Verify with aws s3 ls s3://bucket from the instance."}, {"id": 238, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Users report intermittent 'connection reset' errors from an app behind an AWS Application Load Balancer. The targets show healthy and app logs show no errors. How would you investigate?", "a": "Likely an idle/keep-alive timeout mismatch: the target closes connections before the ALB's idle timeout (default 60s). Set the app/server keep-alive timeout higher than the ALB idle timeout. Check ALB access logs and CloudWatch (TargetResetCount, HTTPCode_ELB_5XX). Also review target group health check settings and deregistration delay. Align timeouts to fix resets."}, {"id": 247, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team's AWS Auto Scaling Group scales out new EC2 instances during traffic spikes, but the new instances immediately fail health checks and get terminated, so capacity never actually increases. The AMI and launch template haven't changed in weeks. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Check whether the ELB/target-group health check grace period is too short for the app to boot, causing premature termination. Review instance user-data/bootstrap logs via EC2 console or CloudWatch to confirm the app starts. Verify the health check path, port, and security group rules allow the LB to reach the instance. Likely a dependency (new package version, external config) changed at boot time. Increase health check grace period and pin bo…"}, {"id": 251, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A teammate says their AWS EC2 instance in a public subnet has a public IP and an internet gateway attached to the VPC, but they still can't SSH in from their laptop — the connection just times out. The instance is Running and status checks pass. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Check the Security Group has an inbound rule allowing TCP 22 from your IP. Verify the subnet's route table has 0.0.0.0/0 pointing to the IGW. Confirm the Network ACL allows inbound 22 and ephemeral outbound ports. Ensure the instance actually has a public IP and you're using the right key/user. Test with the EC2 console's connect or VPC Reachability Analyzer."}, {"id": 262, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After migrating services to a new VPC, two microservices in different subnets can't communicate over a specific port, though both are healthy and security groups 'look open'. Cross-subnet traffic times out while same-subnet traffic works. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Check security group rules allow the port inbound from the source SG/CIDR, and verify NACLs on both subnets allow traffic both directions (NACLs are stateless, need inbound AND outbound rules). Confirm route tables route between subnets. Use VPC Reachability Analyzer or Flow Logs to pinpoint where packets drop. Likely a missing NACL ephemeral-port return rule or SG reference."}, {"id": 267, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your AWS Lambda function behind API Gateway occasionally returns high latency spikes for the first request after idle periods, while subsequent requests are fast. Users complain about slow page loads intermittently. How would you diagnose and reduce this?", "a": "This is cold start latency. On idle, Lambda spins up new execution environments, adding init time (worse with large packages/VPC ENI attach). Diagnose via CloudWatch 'Init Duration' metrics and X-Ray traces. Reduce by enabling Provisioned Concurrency, shrinking deployment package, lazy-loading dependencies, increasing memory (more CPU), and avoiding unnecessary VPC attachment if not needed."}, {"id": 271, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You are a junior engineer managing a private-subnet EC2 instance that needs to pull security updates from an external repository. You have verified the Security Group allows outbound traffic, but 'yum update' still times out. How do you troubleshoot the network path?", "a": "Check the VPC route table associated with the private subnet. Unlike public subnets that route 0.0.0.0/0 to an Internet Gateway, private subnets must route to a NAT Gateway. Verify the NAT Gateway is in a public subnet and its status is 'Available'. Confirm the route table entry exists. If these are correct, ensure the NACLs are not blocking traffic."}, {"id": 276, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "You notice that an AWS Application Load Balancer is periodically timing out for traffic destined for a private subnet. The EC2 instances are healthy, and the Security Group allows traffic. How do you verify if the route table is the bottleneck?", "a": "I would check the Route Table associated with the private subnet to ensure it has a route to a NAT Gateway for outbound traffic and that the NACLs are not blocking return traffic. If the path to the NAT Gateway is missing or misconfigured, traffic cannot exit the private network to respond, resulting in timeouts. I would also verify the Subnet Association of the route table in the AWS Console or CLI."}, {"id": 282, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You are a junior engineer. Your team uses a private subnet for an EC2 instance that needs to fetch images from a public container registry. The instance has no public IP. What AWS networking component must you configure to enable this?", "a": "You must deploy a NAT Gateway in a public subnet. Configure the private subnet's route table to point 0.0.0.0/0 traffic to that NAT Gateway. Ensure the public subnet's route table routes 0.0.0.0/0 to an Internet Gateway. This allows the private instance to initiate outbound requests while remaining unreachable from the public internet."}, {"id": 286, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "An AWS Lambda function has stopped triggering on an S3 event. You suspect the S3 bucket's permissions or the EventBridge/Lambda configuration are incorrect. How do you verify the event flow?", "a": "First, check the CloudWatch logs for the Lambda to see if it triggered at all. Next, inspect the S3 bucket's 'Properties' tab for existing event notifications. Ensure the Lambda function's resource-based policy allows 's3.amazonaws.com' to invoke it. If using EventBridge, check the rule's target configuration to verify it matches the source S3 event pattern."}, {"id": 292, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your CI pipeline uses an AWS S3 bucket to store build artifacts. Suddenly, pipelines are failing with '403 Forbidden' errors. How do you verify if the issue is a restrictive IAM policy, a bucket policy, or a Block Public Access setting?", "a": "First, check the IAM user/role permissions for 's3:PutObject'. Second, review the S3 bucket policy for any 'Deny' statements or missing 'Allow' blocks for your CI role. Third, check if 'Block Public Access' is enabled (though this only affects public requests). Use the AWS Policy Simulator to test the specific action against the resource to isolate which layer is blocking the request."}, {"id": 296, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You need to deploy an application to an AWS EC2 instance in a private subnet. The instance needs to reach the internet to download dependencies, but it lacks a public IP. Which networking component must be deployed in a public subnet to facilitate this traffic, and what must the private subnet's route table point to?", "a": "A NAT Gateway must be deployed in a public subnet with an associated Elastic IP. The private subnet's route table must have a default route (0.0.0.0/0) pointing to the NAT Gateway's ID. This allows outbound internet traffic while preventing direct inbound connections from the internet."}, {"id": 300, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You notice an S3 bucket with 'Block Public Access' enabled, but the bucket policy itself contains a wildcard 'Allow' for 's3:GetObject' from 'Principal: *'. Does this bucket allow public access?", "a": "No. AWS 'Block Public Access' settings act as a security guardrail at the account or bucket level that overrides any individual resource policies. Even if a bucket policy explicitly allows public access, the 'Block Public Access' setting will effectively deny that access, serving as a safety layer to prevent accidental exposure."}, {"id": 302, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "An AWS ALB target group is reporting 'Unhealthy' for instances in a private subnet. The Security Group allows all traffic. How do you verify the routing path?", "a": "First, confirm the route table associated with the private subnet includes a route to the NAT Gateway (if traffic is routed externally) or is within the VPC peering/Transit Gateway range. Check VPC Flow Logs for 'REJECT' entries to see if the traffic is being dropped. Use 'tcpdump' or 'ncat' on the instance to see if the health check packets are reaching the server interface."}, {"id": 308, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "An AWS EC2 instance in a private subnet cannot reach a custom internal service hosted in a different VPC via VPC Peering. The routing table is configured. What is the next logical step?", "a": "Verify the Security Groups of both the source instance and the target service. Ensure the target service's Security Group explicitly allows inbound traffic on the required port from the CIDR block of the source VPC. Also, check for NACL rules that might be blocking the return traffic, as NACLs are stateless and require explicit rules for both directions."}, {"id": 312, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "You are a mid-level engineer managing an AWS ECS service where tasks in a private subnet are timing out while trying to reach an external API. How do you distinguish whether the NAT Gateway or the NACL is the bottleneck?", "a": "Check the VPC Flow Logs for the network interface of the task. If you see 'REJECT' actions, the NACL is likely blocking the traffic (remember NACLs are stateless). If traffic is 'ACCEPT' but no response is received, check the NAT Gateway metrics (e.g., ErrorPortAllocation) in CloudWatch to ensure it hasn't exhausted its connection capacity or reached a throughput limit."}, {"id": 316, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "An AWS Lambda function fails to connect to an RDS database in a private subnet. The function timeout is hitting. How do you verify if the Lambda's Security Group and the RDS instance's Security Group allow the traffic?", "a": "First, check the Lambda's egress rules and the RDS instance's ingress rules. Use the 'Reachability Analyzer' in the VPC console to trace the path. Ensure the RDS security group specifically allows traffic from the Lambda's security group ID. Confirm the Lambda is configured with the correct subnets that have a route to the RDS subnet, and verify that the RDS instance is not restricted by a NACL blocking the return ephemeral port range."}, {"id": 324, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You need to verify if an AWS Security Group rule change will block existing application traffic. How do you safely audit the current traffic flow before applying the update?", "a": "Enable VPC Flow Logs for the network interfaces attached to the resources in question. Analyze the logs in CloudWatch or S3 using Athena to see established connections (ACCEPT logs) occurring on specific ports. Before applying the Terraform change, compare the current 'ACCEPT' traffic patterns against the proposed rule modification to ensure no active protocols are removed."}, {"id": 328, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "An AWS Lambda function in a VPC cannot reach a public API. You've verified the security group is correct. What components should you inspect to ensure traffic routes correctly through a NAT Gateway?", "a": "Check the Lambda's VPC subnet route table. Ensure there is a route for '0.0.0.0/0' pointing to a NAT Gateway ID. Confirm the NAT Gateway is in a public subnet and that the public subnet's route table has a route for '0.0.0.0/0' pointing to an Internet Gateway. Finally, check that the NACLs on both subnets allow egress and ingress traffic for the API's port and ephemeral range."}, {"id": 331, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "An AWS EC2 instance in a public subnet has an IGW attached, but you cannot reach the internet. How do you verify if the route table is correctly configured for public traffic?", "a": "Check the VPC route table associated with the public subnet. Ensure there is a route for '0.0.0.0/0' targeting the Internet Gateway (igw-xxxxxxxx). Use 'aws ec2 describe-route-tables' or the console to verify the destination and target mapping, ensuring it is not pointing to a NAT Gateway or interface."}, {"id": 336, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A junior engineer notices an AWS EC2 instance in a private subnet cannot resolve internal DNS names from another VPC peering connection. What is the most likely VPC configuration missing?", "a": "Check the VPC settings to ensure 'DNS resolution support' and 'DNS hostnames' are enabled for both peered VPCs. Without these enabled, private IP-to-hostname resolution across the peering link will fail even if routing is correct."}, {"id": 344, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A mid-level engineer finds that an AWS EC2 instance in a private subnet is experiencing intermittent packet drops to an S3 bucket via a VPC Endpoint. How do you distinguish between a potential routing issue and a policy-based restriction?", "a": "First, check the VPC Endpoint (Gateway) route table entries in the subnet's route table. If traffic hits the endpoint, verify the VPC Endpoint Policy using 'aws ec2 describe-vpc-endpoints'. If the policy denies specific actions, logs will show 'Access Denied'. Use 'vpc-reachability-analyzer' in the AWS console to trace the path and confirm if the NACL or the Endpoint Policy is dropping the traffic packets."}, {"id": 347, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team runs an RDS instance behind private subnets. An EC2 app in the same VPC can't connect, timing out on port 5432, while a bastion in a public subnet connects fine. Both are in the same VPC. How would you diagnose and fix the connectivity issue?", "a": "Timeouts point to network/security layers, not auth. Check the RDS security group allows inbound 5432 from the app's SG or subnet CIDR. Verify the app subnet's route table and NACLs (NACLs are stateful-unaware and need both inbound/outbound rules). Confirm the app is using the RDS endpoint, not IP. The bastion likely has an allowed SG rule the app lacks; add the app's SG to RDS ingress."}, {"id": 360, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "After setting up a new AWS EC2 instance, you can SSH in fine, but the instance cannot reach the internet to run 'apt update' — it times out. The instance is in a public subnet and has a public IP. What would you check to get outbound internet working?", "a": "Check the route table for the subnet — it needs a route 0.0.0.0/0 pointing to an Internet Gateway. Confirm the IGW is attached to the VPC. Verify security group allows outbound (usually default allows all) and NACLs aren't blocking return traffic. Also confirm the instance actually has a public IP assigned. Missing IGW route is the most common cause."}, {"id": 373, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer asks you to give a teammate read-only access to a single S3 bucket, but the teammate currently has no AWS access at all. How would you grant just that access following least privilege?", "a": "Create an IAM user or role for the teammate and attach a scoped policy allowing only s3:GetObject and s3:ListBucket on that specific bucket ARN and its objects (arn:aws:s3:::bucket and arn:aws:s3:::bucket/*). Avoid broad managed policies like AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess since it grants all buckets. Prefer a role with temporary credentials over long-lived keys."}, {"id": 378, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague set up an AWS Application Load Balancer, but the target EC2 instances show as 'unhealthy' in the target group even though the app responds when they curl it directly on the instance. How would you diagnose why health checks are failing?", "a": "Check the target group health check settings: path, port, protocol, and expected status codes match what the app returns. Verify the instance security group allows inbound traffic from the ALB's security group on the health check port. Confirm the app listens on 0.0.0.0 not just localhost. Review health check thresholds and timeout, and check ALB access/health logs."}, {"id": 382, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your production API's response times degrade badly every Monday morning when traffic ramps up, but the app pods have plenty of CPU and memory headroom. You notice the database connection pool is exhausted in the app logs, yet the RDS instance itself shows low CPU. How would you diagnose and address this bottleneck?", "a": "The bottleneck is the connection pool, not DB compute. Check pool size vs. concurrent requests and connection hold time (slow queries hold connections). Look at RDS max_connections and active connections. Fixes: tune pool size, add a connection pooler (e.g., PgBouncer/RDS Proxy), reduce query latency with indexes, and ensure connections are released promptly. Load-test to validate."}, {"id": 387, "cat": "AWS", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team runs a GitHub Actions pipeline that deploys to AWS using long-lived IAM access keys stored as repo secrets. Security flags this as a risk and wants the keys removed entirely, but the pipeline still needs to deploy to AWS. How would you let the pipeline authenticate to AWS without storing static credentials?", "a": "Use OIDC federation. Configure GitHub's OIDC provider as an IAM identity provider, create an IAM role with a trust policy scoped to your repo/branch (via the token's sub/aud claims), then use aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials with role-to-assume. GitHub issues a short-lived OIDC token per run that's exchanged for temporary STS credentials—no static keys stored."}]}, {"name": "Linux", "slug": "linux", "icon": "🐧", "color": "#f0b400", "blurb": "Disk, permissions, processes, logs, networking commands. Basics asked in every round.", "diagrams": [{"t": "Disk full: find and fix", "steps": ["df -h (which mount?)", "du -h --max-depth=1 /var", "Find big files: find -size +100M", "lsof | grep deleted", "Rotate / vacuum / clean", "df -h again + add alert"]}, {"t": "Process or service not working", "steps": ["systemctl status svc", "journalctl -u svc -n 100", "ss -tlnp (is it listening?)", "Check config / permissions / SELinux", "Restart + verify"]}, {"t": "Linux permission check", "steps": ["ls -l file", "Owner / Group / Others (rwx)", "id user (groups)", "Parent dir execute bit?", "ACL / SELinux / mount noexec"]}], "cheats": [{"c": "df -h / du -sh *", "d": "Disk usage"}, {"c": "top / htop / ps aux --sort=-%mem", "d": "Find heavy processes"}, {"c": "free -m / vmstat 1", "d": "Memory and swap health"}, {"c": "ss -tlnp", "d": "Listening ports and owning process"}, {"c": "journalctl -u <svc> -f", "d": "Follow service logs"}, {"c": "chmod / chown / setfacl", "d": "Permissions and ownership"}, {"c": "lsof -i :80", "d": "Who uses port 80"}, {"c": "tail -f / grep / awk / sed", "d": "Log analysis basics"}], "follow": [{"k": ["disk", "df", "full", "space", "inode"], "q": "df shows space free but you still cannot create files. Why?", "a": "Inodes are exhausted. I check df -i and find directories with huge numbers of tiny files such as sessions or mail queues, then clean them up."}, {"k": ["deleted", "lsof", "log"], "q": "You deleted a huge log but df still shows 100%. Why?", "a": "A running process still holds the file open, so the space is not released. lsof | grep deleted shows it. Restarting the process, or truncating with > file instead of rm, frees the space."}, {"k": ["permission", "chmod", "chown", "sudo"], "q": "Explain chmod 755 vs 644 and when you use each.", "a": "755 is rwx for owner and r-x for others, used for directories and scripts. 644 is rw- for owner and read-only for others, used for normal files. Never 777 in production."}, {"k": ["cpu", "load", "top", "slow", "memory"], "q": "Load average is 20 on a 4-core machine. What do you check?", "a": "I check whether it is CPU-bound or I/O-bound. top shows %wa and %us, iostat shows disk waits, and ps shows the top processes. Load includes processes waiting on I/O, so high load with low CPU usually means a disk or NFS problem."}, {"k": ["process", "kill", "zombie", "systemctl", "service"], "q": "Difference between kill, kill -9 and systemctl stop?", "a": "kill sends SIGTERM so the app can shut down cleanly. kill -9 sends SIGKILL which cannot be handled and may leave corruption. systemctl stop handles the whole service properly. I try graceful first and use -9 as the last option."}, {"k": ["ssh", "key", "login", "connect"], "q": "SSH says Permission denied (publickey). Steps?", "a": "Verify the correct user and key with ssh -v, check that authorized_keys has permissions 600 and the .ssh directory 700, and confirm the home directory is not group-writable. The server log /var/log/auth.log or secure explains the exact reason."}, {"k": ["memory", "oom", "swap"], "q": "What does the OOM killer do and how do you confirm it ran?", "a": "When memory runs out, the kernel kills a process with a high oom score. I confirm with dmesg -T | grep -i oom or journalctl -k. The fix is to limit memory, tune the app, or add RAM or swap."}, {"k": ["cron", "schedule", "job"], "q": "A cron job works manually but not from cron. Why?", "a": "Cron has a minimal PATH and environment. I use absolute paths, set environment variables in the script, redirect output to a log file, and check /var/log/cron or syslog."}, {"k": ["network", "port", "connect", "listening", "dns"], "q": "App is running but you cannot reach it from another host. Steps?", "a": "ss -tlnp to see if it listens on 0.0.0.0 and not only 127.0.0.1, then firewall rules with iptables or ufw or firewalld, then cloud security groups, and test with curl or nc from the client."}], "edge": [{"q": "Production server is at 100% disk and the app has stopped. You cannot even run commands properly. What is your first move?", "a": "Free a small amount of space safely first so the system can work: clear package cache, vacuum journal logs, or truncate a known big rotated log. Then find the real cause with du and fix it. I never delete database or application data to buy space.", "fu": [{"q": "What if /var is a separate small partition?", "a": "I find what fills it, and long term extend the volume with lvextend or EBS modify-volume plus growpart and resize2fs."}]}, {"q": "A server is slow only at 2 AM every day. How do you find the cause?", "a": "I look at cron jobs and timers around that time, backups, log rotation and AV scans, and check sar history or CloudWatch/Grafana for that window. Then I stagger or limit the job with nice and ionice.", "fu": []}, {"q": "You accidentally ran chmod -R 777 / on a server. What now?", "a": "I stay calm and stop further changes. Best option is restore from snapshot or rebuild from IaC since permissions across the system are unknown. If not possible, restore permissions from the package manager using rpm --setperms or dpkg reinstall. I also write a post-incident note.", "fu": [{"q": "How do you prevent that?", "a": "No direct prod root access, peer review for commands, and immutable infrastructure where servers are rebuilt not hand-edited."}]}, {"q": "Server time is wrong and certificates and logs look broken. What is wrong?", "a": "NTP or chrony is not syncing. I check timedatectl and chronyc tracking, fix the time source, and make sure all servers use the same one. Time drift breaks TLS, tokens, and log correlation.", "fu": []}, {"q": "Only one user cannot write to a shared directory while others can. What do you check?", "a": "Group membership with id, which needs a new login to apply, directory permissions and setgid bit, ACLs with getfacl, and umask of the user.", "fu": []}], "qa": [{"id": 2, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "General", "q": "A user reports that a shared Linux server is running out of disk space and can't write logs. df shows /var at 100%, but you're not sure what's consuming it. Walk through how you'd find and free up space safely.", "a": "Run df -h to confirm the full filesystem, then du -sh /var/* to find large directories. Use find /var/log -type f -size +100M to spot big files. Check for deleted-but-open files with lsof | grep deleted (space held by processes). Rotate/compress or truncate logs (truncate -s 0), clear old journal logs (journalctl --vacuum-size). Set up logrotate to prevent recurrence."}, {"id": 10, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "General", "q": "You need to give a new team member read-only access to a Linux server so they can view logs but not modify anything. How would you set this up securely?", "a": "Create a dedicated user account (no root). Add them to a group with read access to log directories, or use ACLs ('setfacl') for granular read permissions on /var/log. Avoid adding to sudoers. Use SSH key-based auth, disable password login. Optionally restrict via a restricted shell. Verify they can read but not write/delete, following least-privilege principles."}, {"id": 14, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "General", "q": "You SSH into a Linux server to debug an app, and every command like 'ls' or 'top' returns 'command not found', even though the tools exist. What could be wrong and how do you get working?", "a": "Most likely the PATH environment variable is broken or empty. Verify with 'echo $PATH'. Run commands with full paths like '/bin/ls' or '/usr/bin/top'. Reset PATH temporarily: 'export PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin'. Then check shell startup files (~/.bashrc, /etc/profile) for a bad edit that overwrote PATH and fix it."}, {"id": 19, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "General", "q": "A cron job on a Linux server is supposed to run a backup script every night, but the backups aren't being created. When you run the script manually it works perfectly. How do you troubleshoot why cron isn't running it correctly?", "a": "Cron runs with a minimal environment and different PATH than your shell. Check /var/log/syslog or cron logs to confirm it triggered. Common causes: relative paths, missing env vars, wrong user, or no output redirection. Use absolute paths in the script, source needed env, and add logging (redirect stdout/stderr to a file) to capture errors."}, {"id": 23, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your team's app writes logs to /var/log/app.log. The file keeps growing until the disk fills up, causing outages every few weeks. How would you set up a permanent fix on the Linux server?", "a": "Configure logrotate: create a config in /etc/logrotate.d/app defining rotation (daily/size-based), compress, maxage, and rotate count. Use copytruncate or send the app a signal/postrotate to reopen the file so it doesn't keep writing to the deleted inode. Verify with 'logrotate -d' (dry run) then '-f'. Ideally also ship logs to a central system so disk isn't the single point of failure."}, {"id": 29, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "General", "q": "You SSH into a Linux server and notice a process is consuming 100% CPU, slowing everything down. You need to identify it and safely stop it without taking down critical services. Walk through your steps.", "a": "Run 'top' or 'htop' to identify the PID and CPU usage. Use 'ps -p <pid> -o pid,cmd,%cpu' to confirm what it is and 'ls -l /proc/<pid>/exe' for the binary. Check if it's critical before acting. Optionally 'renice' to lower priority instead of killing. If safe to stop, 'kill <pid>' (SIGTERM) first, then 'kill -9' if unresponsive. Investigate logs to find root cause."}, {"id": 32, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "General", "q": "A new engineer joins and needs to run a specific service on a Linux server that only starts on boot but keeps dying after a few minutes with no clear error in the terminal. They started it manually with './app &'. How do you make it reliable and observable?", "a": "Run it as a systemd service instead of a background job. Create a unit file with Restart=on-failure, define the ExecStart, user, and working directory. Enable it with systemctl enable --now. Logs go to journald, viewable via journalctl -u service. This gives auto-restart, boot persistence, and centralized logging instead of a fragile detached process."}, {"id": 37, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "General", "q": "You need to copy a 50GB file from one Linux server to another over the network, but the transfer keeps failing partway and you have to restart from scratch each time, wasting hours. How would you make this reliable and resumable?", "a": "Use rsync with --partial and --progress (rsync -avP), which resumes from where it stopped instead of restarting. Optionally run it inside tmux/screen so a dropped SSH session doesn't kill it. Add --compress for slow links and verify integrity with checksums. rsync's delta transfer avoids re-sending completed data."}, {"id": 55, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "General", "q": "A monitoring alert fires for high memory on a Linux server. You SSH in and free -m shows most memory 'used', but the app itself isn't consuming that much. The team wants to know if they need more RAM. How do you investigate?", "a": "Much of 'used' is often buffers/cache, which Linux reclaims on demand — check the 'available' column in free -m rather than 'used'. Use top/htop and ps to find real consumers, and check for cache with cat /proc/meminfo. If available memory is healthy, no upgrade is needed. Only worry if swap is thrashing or available is truly low."}, {"id": 62, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Need to schedule a script to clean temp files hourly on a Linux server. You add it to crontab but nothing happens. Manually running the script works. The crontab line looks correct. What do you check?", "a": "Verify cron service is running (systemctl status cron/crond). Check that the crontab uses absolute paths for the script and binaries, since cron has a minimal PATH. Look for output redirection issues and check /var/log/syslog or mail for errors. Ensure the script has execute permissions and a proper shebang. Add logging with '>> /tmp/log 2>&1' to capture failures."}, {"id": 70, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your Linux web server suddenly can't accept new connections and users get 'connection refused', though the process is running and CPU/memory are fine. dmesg and app logs show 'too many open files'. How do you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "The process hit its file descriptor ulimit (open sockets + files). Check with 'ulimit -n' and 'ls /proc/<pid>/fd | wc -l'. Raise limits via LimitNOFILE in the systemd unit (or /etc/security/limits.conf) and reload. Also investigate leaked connections/fds in the app. Restart the service to apply and verify fd count stabilizes."}, {"id": 73, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're on call and an alert fires that disk usage on a production Linux server hit 95%. You need to free space fast without deleting anything critical. Walk through how you'd find and safely reclaim space.", "a": "Run 'df -h' to find the full mount, then 'du -sh /*' or 'du -h --max-depth=1' to drill into large dirs. Common culprits: old logs, /tmp, journal logs. Truncate/rotate logs ('journalctl --vacuum-size', logrotate), clear package caches, remove old archives. Verify with df again. Then set up logrotate/monitoring to prevent recurrence."}, {"id": 81, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You're a junior engineer managing a Linux server where a colleague accidentally changed a config file's permissions, and now the web service won't start, logging 'permission denied' when reading /etc/app/config.yml. How do you diagnose and restore correct access without opening it up to everyone?", "a": "Check current perms/owner with 'ls -l /etc/app/config.yml' and 'stat'. Identify the user the service runs as (systemctl show or ps). Compare to a working server. Restore with 'chown appuser:appgroup config.yml' and 'chmod 640' so owner reads/writes and group reads. Avoid 777. Verify SELinux/AppArmor context if still failing (ausearch/getenforce), then restart and tail logs."}, {"id": 98, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're on call and a Linux server's load average is over 30 on a 4-core box, but CPU usage in top shows mostly idle. The application is sluggish. Users are complaining. How do you figure out what's driving the high load?", "a": "High load with idle CPU usually means processes blocked on I/O (uninterruptible D-state), not CPU work. Check top/ps for processes in D state, run iostat -x 1 and iotop to find disk saturation, and vmstat 1 to see the b (blocked) column and wait. Investigate the disk/storage layer, a hung NFS/network mount, or a runaway I/O process, then throttle or fix that workload."}, {"id": 107, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague asks you to copy a large log file off a remote Linux server, but scp keeps failing partway through because the connection drops on the flaky network. How would you transfer the file reliably so it resumes instead of restarting?", "a": "Use rsync over SSH with the partial/resume options: 'rsync -avP --partial user@host:/path/file .'. rsync only transfers the missing portion on retry and -P shows progress while keeping partial data. Alternatively compress first (gzip) to shrink transfer size, or use rsync in a loop until it exits 0. This avoids restarting the whole scp from zero."}, {"id": 118, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague reports a Linux process is consuming all the memory on a server, and the app is being killed by the OOM killer. You need to confirm which process is the culprit and why. How do you investigate?", "a": "Check 'dmesg' or /var/log/syslog for 'Out of memory' entries naming the killed process. Use 'top' or 'ps aux --sort=-%mem' to see current memory hogs, and 'free -h' for overall usage. Confirm whether it's a real leak or normal load, review the app's memory settings, and consider adding limits, tuning the app, or scaling resources."}, {"id": 123, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague can SSH into a Linux server but every command like ls or cat hangs for 10+ seconds before responding, then works. Login itself was also slow. CPU and memory look fine. How do you track down the delay?", "a": "Slow-but-eventual behavior often points to DNS or reverse-lookup timeouts. Check /etc/resolv.conf for unreachable nameservers. Test with 'time nslookup' or run commands with strace to spot where they block (often on network/DNS calls). For SSH login lag specifically, set 'UseDNS no' in sshd_config. Also check /etc/nsswitch.conf and any hung NFS mounts causing filesystem stalls."}, {"id": 128, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A user reports they can't reach an internal web service on port 8080 on a Linux host, though the app process is running. Pinging the host works. How would you confirm whether the app is actually listening and reachable?", "a": "Check the process is bound to the port with ss -tlnp or netstat -tlnp — confirm it's on 0.0.0.0:8080, not 127.0.0.1. Test locally with curl localhost:8080. If local works but remote fails, check firewall (iptables -L, ufw status, security groups). Verify the app isn't bound only to loopback. Use tcpdump to see if SYN packets arrive if still stuck."}, {"id": 135, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Users report a Linux server hosting a web app has become read-only — the app throws 'Read-only file system' errors when writing files, though it was working earlier and disk space is available. How do you diagnose and recover?", "a": "A filesystem usually remounts read-only when the kernel detects I/O errors or corruption, protecting data. Check 'mount | grep ro' and 'dmesg' or /var/log/syslog for I/O errors or a failing disk. Run 'df -h' and 'lsblk' to confirm the device. Try 'fsck' on the unmounted filesystem to repair, then remount read-write with 'mount -o remount,rw'. If it's a failing disk, escalate for replacement and restore from backup rather than forcing writes."}, {"id": 141, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer reports that after adding a new user to a Linux server, that user can log in but cannot run any commands with sudo — they get 'user is not in the sudoers file'. Other admins can use sudo fine. How would you grant this user sudo access properly?", "a": "Add the user to the sudo group (Debian/Ubuntu) or wheel (RHEL): usermod -aG sudo username. The user must log out and back in for group membership to apply. Avoid editing /etc/sudoers directly; if needed use visudo (validates syntax) or drop a file in /etc/sudoers.d/. Confirm with groups username and test sudo whoami."}, {"id": 148, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer wants to see the last 50 lines of a growing log file on a Linux server and keep watching new lines as they're written, without repeatedly re-running a command. How would you do this?", "a": "Use tail -n 50 -f /path/to/logfile — -n 50 shows the last 50 lines and -f follows the file, printing new lines as they're appended. For rotated logs use tail -F which reopens the file if it's rotated. Combine with grep, e.g. tail -f app.log | grep ERROR, to watch only matching lines."}, {"id": 158, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague reports that their Linux server's load average is very high, but they can't tell whether it's CPU-bound, waiting on disk I/O, or something else. How would you investigate what's driving the high load?", "a": "Run top/htop to see CPU usage and identify hungry processes. Check uptime for load trends. Use vmstat 1 — high wa (I/O wait) points to disk bottleneck, high us/sy to CPU. Use iostat -x to confirm disk saturation and iotop to find the process. High load with low CPU usually means I/O or blocked processes in uninterruptible sleep (D state)."}, {"id": 165, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Monitoring alerts fire that a Linux server is running out of memory, but free -h shows most memory is 'used' by buff/cache and very little is 'free'. A senior tells you not to panic. Why isn't low free memory necessarily a problem, and how do you tell if there's a real memory issue?", "a": "Linux uses spare RAM for buffers/cache to speed up I/O; this memory is reclaimable on demand, so low 'free' is normal and healthy. Look at the 'available' column in free -h — that's memory truly available to apps. A real problem shows as low 'available', rising swap usage (si/so in vmstat), and OOM-killer events in dmesg/journalctl. Monitor 'available' and swap, not 'free'."}, {"id": 175, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Users report a Linux web server returns intermittent errors. You check and find the application can't write new files, failing with 'No space left on device', yet df -h shows the disk is only 40% full. How would you diagnose this?", "a": "The disk has free space but is likely out of inodes. Run df -i to check inode usage — if it's 100%, too many small files exhausted the inodes. Find the culprit directory (e.g. find / -xdev -type f | cut -d/ -f2 | sort | uniq -c), often session files, logs, or cache. Clean up old files and add rotation/cleanup to prevent recurrence."}, {"id": 180, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Users report a Linux server is refusing new SSH connections with 'Too many open files' errors, though CPU and memory look fine. The service has been running for weeks without a restart. How would you diagnose and resolve this?", "a": "The process hit its file descriptor limit. Find the PID, check cat /proc/<pid>/limits for open files and count usage with ls /proc/<pid>/fd | wc -l. Likely a leak (unclosed sockets/files). Immediate fix: restart the service. Longer term: raise limits via ulimit/systemd LimitNOFILE, and fix the code leaking descriptors. Use lsof -p <pid> to identify what's held open."}, {"id": 183, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "After a coworker edited the crontab on a Linux server, a backup job that should run nightly at 2 AM never executes, though running the script manually works fine. crontab -l shows the entry. How would you diagnose why the cron job isn't running?", "a": "Check /var/log/syslog or /var/log/cron to see if cron even fired. Common causes: cron uses a minimal PATH and no user environment, so scripts relying on binaries not in /usr/bin fail—use absolute paths. Ensure the schedule syntax and correct crontab (user vs root) are right. Redirect output to a log (>> /tmp/backup.log 2>&1) to capture errors. Verify the cron daemon is running (systemctl status cron) and the script has execute permis…"}, {"id": 188, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Users report a Linux web server sometimes returns slow responses. You suspect a specific process is spawning too many threads. How would you identify which process is consuming the most CPU and inspect its threads in real time?", "a": "Use top (press 1 to see per-core, H to show threads) or htop to spot the top CPU consumer and its PID. Drill into threads with top -H -p <PID> or ps -T -p <PID>. For deeper insight use pidstat -t -p <PID> 1 to watch per-thread CPU over time, then check the app's logs/config for a runaway loop or misconfigured thread pool."}, {"id": 198, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer says a shell script that loops over filenames breaks whenever a file has spaces in its name — it treats each word as a separate file. The script uses for f in $(ls). How would you fix the script to handle filenames with spaces safely?", "a": "Never parse ls output; word-splitting breaks on spaces/newlines. Use a glob instead: for f in *; do ...; done, and always quote the variable: \"$f\". For recursive/complex cases use find ... -print0 piped to while IFS= read -r -d '' f. Quoting and avoiding command substitution on filenames prevents the splitting."}, {"id": 203, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer reports that their newly created Linux user can't write to a shared project directory owned by a 'devteam' group, even though you added them to that group. They're still getting 'Permission denied'. How would you fix this?", "a": "Group membership changes don't apply to existing login sessions. The user must log out and back in (or run 'newgrp devteam') to pick up the new group. Verify with 'id username' and 'groups'. Also confirm the directory has group write permission (chmod g+w) and consider setting the setgid bit (chmod g+s) so new files inherit the group."}, {"id": 208, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "After a coworker changed a Linux server's network config, the machine can ping IP addresses directly (like 8.8.8.8) but can't resolve any domain names — curl google.com fails with 'could not resolve host'. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "IP works but names don't, so it's a DNS resolution issue. Check /etc/resolv.conf for valid nameserver entries; it's likely empty or wrong after the config change. Test with nslookup google.com 8.8.8.8 to confirm an external resolver works. Fix by adding a working nameserver (via netplan/NetworkManager/systemd-resolved so it persists) and verify systemd-resolved status. Confirm with dig or getent hosts."}, {"id": 214, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Monitoring shows a Linux server's root filesystem filling up fast. You delete a large log file that a running application is still writing to, but df -h shows no space was freed. Why, and how do you actually reclaim the space?", "a": "On Linux, deleting a file only removes the directory entry; the space isn't freed while a process holds an open file descriptor to it. Find the holder with lsof | grep deleted. Reclaim space by truncating it in place (truncate -s 0 /path/log or > file) before deletion, or restart/signal the process (e.g. logrotate copytruncate) so it releases the descriptor."}, {"id": 218, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "During an incident you need to find out which process on a Linux server has a specific TCP port (8080) already bound, because your app fails to start with 'address already in use'. How would you identify and stop the offending process?", "a": "Use sudo ss -ltnp | grep :8080 (or sudo lsof -i :8080 / netstat -tlnp) to see the PID and process bound to the port. Confirm what it is, then stop it gracefully or kill it with kill <pid>. Afterward, verify the port is free with ss -ltnp | grep :8080 before restarting your app, and check why the old process lingered (e.g., a stale instance not shut down properly)."}, {"id": 225, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "During an incident the on-call asks you to quickly check the last 50 lines of a growing application log and keep watching it live for new errors on a Linux server. You also need to filter for lines containing 'ERROR' as they appear. How would you do this?", "a": "Use tail -n 50 -f /path/to/app.log to see the last 50 lines and follow new output live. To filter live for errors, pipe it: tail -f /path/to/app.log | grep --line-buffered ERROR. The --line-buffered flag ensures grep flushes matches immediately. Press Ctrl+C to stop. For multiple rotated files, tail -F follows re-created files too."}, {"id": 240, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "During an incident you're told disk usage on a Linux server is at 100%, but df -h shows the root filesystem at only 60%. Users still get 'no space left on device' errors. How would you diagnose the real cause?", "a": "The filesystem likely ran out of inodes, not blocks. Check df -i — if inode usage is 100%, too many small files exist. Find offenders with find / -xdev -type f | cut -d/ -f2 | sort | uniq -c | sort -n or check dirs like /tmp, mail spools, session files. Delete/rotate the small files to free inodes. Consider log rotation to prevent recurrence."}, {"id": 245, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You've been asked to copy a 5GB file from your Linux workstation to a remote server, but the transfer keeps failing partway through due to an unstable connection, forcing you to restart from zero each time. How would you transfer it reliably?", "a": "Use rsync with resume support: rsync -avP --partial file user@host:/path/ — the -P/--partial flag keeps partially transferred data so reruns continue where it left off instead of restarting. rsync also verifies via checksums. For very flaky links, wrap it in a retry loop or use --append-verify. Alternatively scp/sftp lack good resume, so rsync (over SSH) is the right tool here."}, {"id": 248, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You need to schedule a backup script to run every night at 2 AM on a Linux server, but a cron job you added earlier isn't running at all. The script works when you run it manually. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Check crontab -l to confirm the entry exists and the schedule is correct (0 2 * * *). Common causes: cron uses a minimal PATH, so use absolute paths for the script and binaries. Ensure the script is executable and has a proper shebang. Capture output by redirecting to a log (>> /var/log/backup.log 2>&1) to see errors. Check /var/log/syslog or /var/log/cron for cron execution, and confirm the right user's crontab is used."}, {"id": 253, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague asks you to find all files larger than 100MB under /var that were modified in the last 7 days, because disk is filling up. How would you do this on a Linux server?", "a": "Use find: find /var -type f -size +100M -mtime -7 -exec ls -lh {} \\;. The -size +100M filters large files, -mtime -7 filters modified in last 7 days. Pipe to sort for readability, or use du -ah /var | sort -rh | head. Combine with du for directory totals. Act carefully before deleting anything in use."}, {"id": 255, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "During a traffic surge users report the app is slow, and a senior asks you to quickly check which processes are consuming the most CPU and memory on a Linux server right now. How would you investigate?", "a": "Use top or htop and sort by CPU (P) or memory (M). Use ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head and ps aux --sort=-%mem | head for a snapshot. Check load average with uptime, and free -h for memory pressure. Look at vmstat 1 for trends. Identify the offending PID before deciding to throttle, restart, or scale."}, {"id": 260, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A teammate says they can reach a web service on a Linux server via its IP but want to verify that a firewall rule is actually allowing traffic on port 443 and see which ports the server is currently listening on. How would you check this?", "a": "Use ss -tlnp (or netstat -tlnp) to list listening TCP ports and the owning processes—confirm 443 is bound. Check firewall rules with sudo iptables -L -n or sudo firewalld-cmd --list-all / ufw status. From a client, test with curl -v https://ip:443 or nc -zv ip 443. For cloud servers also verify the security group allows inbound 443."}, {"id": 265, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "During an incident a senior asks you to confirm whether a Linux service managed by systemd is actually running, and if it crashed, to see why it failed and when. How would you investigate?", "a": "Run systemctl status <service> to see active/failed state, PID, and recent log lines. Use journalctl -u <service> --since '1 hour ago' to view detailed logs and crash errors. Check systemctl is-enabled <service> for boot behavior. If it keeps restarting, inspect exit codes in the status output and the journal to find the root cause before restarting."}, {"id": 275, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You are a junior engineer observing that a Linux server's disk usage is 99% full in the /var/log directory. You need to clear space immediately. How do you do this safely without breaking running services?", "a": "Do not delete files directly with 'rm' if the service is still writing to them, as the space won't be reclaimed until the process restarts. Instead, truncate the files using 'truncate -s 0 filename' or echo an empty string into the file. Check for rotated logs that can be compressed or moved to off-server storage to free up space permanently."}, {"id": 277, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You need to investigate why a Linux service is unable to write to a specific directory. You verify the permissions on the directory allow your user to write, but it still fails. What do you check next?", "a": "I would check for disk space using 'df -h' and inode exhaustion using 'df -i', as either can prevent file creation despite correct permissions. If space is available, I would check if the filesystem is mounted as read-only ('mount | grep <dir>') or if SELinux or AppArmor policies are enforcing access restrictions by checking '/var/log/audit/audit.log' or running 'getenforce'."}, {"id": 284, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You are a junior engineer. A service running as a background Linux process is suddenly consuming 100% of the CPU. Before killing the process, what command-line tools can you use to see what the process is actually doing?", "a": "Use 'top' or 'htop' to identify the PID. Use 'strace -p <PID>' to attach to the process and observe the system calls; this reveals if it is stuck in a loop reading a file or waiting on a network socket. You can also use 'lsof -p <PID>' to see which files or sockets the process has open, which often points to the root cause of the hang."}, {"id": 289, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A Linux server has high disk I/O wait times. You suspect a rogue process is writing massive amounts of data to a hidden file. How do you find which process is responsible?", "a": "Use 'iotop' to identify the process causing high I/O in real-time. Once the PID is found, use 'lsof -p <PID>' to list all files currently opened by that process. This will reveal the path of the file being written to. Do not kill the process until you verify if it is a critical system task or a leak."}, {"id": 291, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You need to perform an emergency patch on a Linux server, but you are worried about conflicting updates from another user. How do you check if anyone else is currently logged into the server and what sessions they are running?", "a": "Use the 'w' command to see a list of logged-in users, their idle times, and the command they are currently executing. Use 'who' for a simple list or 'last' to view historical login data. If you see others, communicate via a team channel before applying changes to avoid service disruption."}, {"id": 297, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "You are a mid-level engineer monitoring a Linux server that is experiencing intermittent network drops. You suspect a full connection tracking table. How do you check the current state of the connection tracking table and its maximum limit using the sysctl interface?", "a": "Use 'sysctl net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_count' to see current connections and 'sysctl net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max' to see the limit. If count approaches max, existing connections may be dropped. You can investigate current socket states using 'ss -tan' or examine dmesg for 'nf_conntrack: table full' errors to confirm the diagnosis."}, {"id": 303, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A Linux service is failing to write to a log file despite 'ls -l' showing your user has write permissions to the parent directory. What could be the issue?", "a": "Check the filesystem for 'immutable' attributes using 'lsattr'. If the 'i' attribute is set, the file cannot be modified even by root until the attribute is removed with 'chattr -i'. Additionally, check for SELinux or AppArmor constraints using 'ausearch -m avc -ts recent' or 'dmesg | grep -i deny' to see if a mandatory access control policy is blocking the write operation."}, {"id": 309, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "You are a mid-level engineer monitoring a Linux server that periodically runs out of file descriptors, causing application crashes. How do you identify which process is consuming the most file descriptors?", "a": "Use 'lsof -n | awk '{print $2}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head' to identify the PID with the highest number of open file handles. Once identified, use 'ls -l /proc/<PID>/fd' to inspect exactly which files or sockets that process is holding open. This allows you to distinguish between legitimate high usage and a potential resource leak."}, {"id": 314, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A Linux server's load average is high, but CPU usage is low. You suspect Disk I/O wait. How do you identify the specific process and file causing the saturation?", "a": "Use 'iostat -x 1' to confirm disk utilization. Then use 'iotop' to see which process is performing high amounts of I/O. If a process is identified, use 'lsof -p <pid>' to list the specific files that the process has open, which will pinpoint the culprit without needing to terminate the service immediately."}, {"id": 317, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A Linux server's SSH access is slow. You suspect someone is using the server for high-bandwidth tasks. How do you identify which specific network connections are currently established and consuming the most bandwidth?", "a": "Use 'nethogs' to see bandwidth per process in real-time. Alternatively, use 'ss -tap' to list active TCP connections and their states. If you suspect traffic volume, use 'iftop' to see real-time bandwidth usage by IP. Compare these outputs against known legitimate services. Check for unexpected processes using 'lsof -i -P -n' to map network ports to process IDs before taking further action like checking service logs."}, {"id": 325, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A Linux server's disk space is full. You suspect a deleted file is still being held open by a process, preventing the disk space from being reclaimed. How do you find and safely address this?", "a": "Run 'lsof +L1' to identify open files that have been unlinked (deleted) but are still held by a process. The output will show the PID and the file descriptor. Instead of killing the process, check if the application has a mechanism to reload its configuration or logs (e.g., SIGHUP). If not, notify stakeholders before restarting the service to release the file handle."}, {"id": 329, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A Linux server is unresponsive under high load. How do you distinguish if the bottleneck is due to CPU context switching versus actual CPU processing saturation?", "a": "Run 'top' or 'htop' to check the 'wa' (I/O wait) and 'hi/si' (hardware/software interrupts). Use 'vmstat 1' to check 'cs' (context switches) and 'in' (interrupts). High context switches with low CPU usage suggest thread contention; high CPU usage with low idle time suggests the application is truly saturated and needs optimization or more resources."}, {"id": 335, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A CI pipeline fails during the 'build' stage with a 'no space left on device' error on the runner. What command should you run to confirm if this is disk capacity or inode exhaustion?", "a": "Run 'df -h' to check disk space usage percentage. Run 'df -i' to check inode usage. If inodes are full, even with available space, files cannot be created. Identify large build directories or excessive small temp files using 'du -sh *' or 'find . -type f | wc -l' to clean up before restarting the pipeline."}, {"id": 340, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A junior engineer is asked to troubleshoot why a Linux service fails to start due to 'too many open files'. How do you verify the current per-process limit versus the system-wide limit?", "a": "Run 'ulimit -n' to see the current shell's soft limit, and 'cat /proc/sys/fs/file-max' to see the system-wide limit. Use 'lsof -p <PID> | wc -l' to count the open files for the specific service process to confirm it is actually hitting the limit."}, {"id": 343, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A junior engineer suspects a Linux process is causing high latency. How would you determine if the process is being throttled by CPU quota (CFS) rather than just being CPU-bound?", "a": "Check the cgroup statistics for the process. Navigate to '/sys/fs/cgroup/cpu/<cgroup-path>/' and inspect 'cpu.stat'. Look for 'nr_throttled' and 'throttled_time'. If 'nr_throttled' is increasing, the process is hitting its defined CPU limit (quota) and being paused by the kernel, even if overall system load isn't 100%. This helps confirm if you need to adjust resource requests rather than optimizing application code."}, {"id": 349, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're on call and a production Linux server suddenly reports 'No space left on device', but df -h shows the root filesystem is only 60% full. Writes are still failing. How would you diagnose this contradiction?", "a": "Disk blocks aren't the only limit—check inode exhaustion with df -i. Many tiny files (logs, sessions, cache) can exhaust inodes while blocks remain free. Also check for deleted-but-open files holding space via lsof | grep deleted, and verify you're checking the right mount, since the failing path may be a different filesystem. Clean up small files or restart the process holding deleted files."}, {"id": 352, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "After copying an SSH private key to a new Linux jump host, connecting fails with 'Permissions are too open ... it is required that your private key files are NOT accessible by others'. The key is valid and works elsewhere. How do you fix it?", "a": "SSH refuses to use private keys readable by group/others for security. Restrict the file: chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_rsa and ensure the .ssh directory is chmod 700. Confirm ownership belongs to your user (chown user:user). After tightening permissions, retry the connection — it should now succeed."}, {"id": 358, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You need to run a command on a Linux server that will keep running after you disconnect your SSH session. Currently when you close the terminal, the long-running process dies. How would you keep it running in the background?", "a": "The process dies because it's tied to the session (SIGHUP on disconnect). Use a terminal multiplexer like tmux or screen to detach/reattach, or nohup with '&' (nohup ./script.sh &) and redirect output to a file. For services, better to run it as a systemd service so it's managed and survives reboots."}, {"id": 363, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague reports that a cron job on a Linux server that should run every night at 2 AM isn't running, though the script works fine when run manually. How would you diagnose why the cron job isn't executing?", "a": "Check 'crontab -l' to confirm the entry and schedule syntax. Inspect cron logs (/var/log/syslog or journalctl -u cron). Common causes: cron uses a minimal PATH so absolute paths are needed for binaries; the script relies on env vars not present in cron; or it lacks execute permission. Redirect output to a log file in the crontab line to capture errors."}, {"id": 368, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague reports that a Bash deployment script fails silently — it continues past a failed command and reports success even when a step (like a file copy) actually failed. How would you make the script fail fast and behave more safely?", "a": "By default Bash ignores command failures. Add 'set -euo pipefail' at the top: -e exits on any non-zero exit, -u errors on unset variables, and pipefail catches failures in piped commands. Also check critical commands' exit codes explicitly and add error traps (trap ... ERR) for cleanup/logging. This ensures the script stops and signals failure instead of falsely succeeding."}, {"id": 375, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague reports that a Linux server's disk usage keeps growing and they can't figure out which directory is responsible. df shows the partition nearly full. How would you track down what's consuming the space?", "a": "Start at the full mount and drill down: du -h --max-depth=1 /path | sort -rh | head repeatedly, or use ncdu for interactive exploration. Watch for large log files and deleted-but-open files (lsof | grep deleted) that still hold space. Clean logs, rotate them, or truncate open files after restarting the holding process."}, {"id": 380, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A teammate reports that a Linux service they started with systemctl start myapp works, but after a server reboot the service doesn't come back up automatically and they have to start it manually every time. How would you fix this?", "a": "The unit is started but not enabled for boot. Run 'systemctl enable myapp' (or 'enable --now' to enable and start together) so a symlink is created in the target's wants directory. Verify with 'systemctl is-enabled myapp'. Also check the unit has a proper [Install] section with WantedBy, otherwise enable won't persist it across reboots."}, {"id": 383, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "After cloning a Git repo to a Linux build server and running your deploy script, it fails with 'permission denied' when executing ./deploy.sh, even though the file is present and the content looks correct. Running bash deploy.sh works. Why does ./deploy.sh fail and how do you fix it properly?", "a": "The file lacks the executable bit, so running it directly fails, while bash deploy.sh works because bash reads it as an argument. Fix with chmod +x deploy.sh. To make it permanent, commit the executable bit (git update-index --chmod=+x deploy.sh) so clones retain it. Also confirm a proper shebang line (#!/bin/bash) is present at the top."}, {"id": 386, "cat": "Linux", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer asks you to copy a large directory of files from your local Linux machine to a remote server, but the transfer keeps getting interrupted by flaky WiFi and you have to start over each time from the beginning. How would you transfer the files so an interrupted transfer resumes where it left off instead of restarting?", "a": "Use rsync instead of scp. rsync -avzP --partial user@host:/dest/ local/ resumes partial transfers, only sends changed/missing data, and shows progress. The --partial flag keeps partially transferred files so a re-run continues rather than restarting. You can loop it or use --append-verify for very large single files. rsync's delta algorithm makes re-runs fast."}]}, {"name": "Networking", "slug": "networking", "icon": "🌐", "color": "#06b6d4", "blurb": "DNS, SSL/TLS, load balancers, ports, troubleshooting connectivity step by step.", "diagrams": [{"t": "Request journey", "steps": ["Browser", "DNS lookup", "TCP + TLS handshake", "Load balancer", "Server / app", "Response"]}, {"t": "Cannot reach a website", "steps": ["DNS: dig / nslookup", "Ping / traceroute", "Port: nc -zv host 443", "TLS: openssl s_client", "HTTP: curl -v", "App logs"]}, {"t": "TLS certificate expired", "steps": ["Confirm expiry: openssl s_client", "Which cert / where installed (LB, ingress)?", "Renew (ACM / cert-manager / certbot)", "Reload / redeploy", "Add expiry alert"]}], "cheats": [{"c": "dig example.com +short", "d": "DNS answer"}, {"c": "curl -vI https://host", "d": "Headers, TLS, redirects"}, {"c": "nc -zv host 443", "d": "Is port open?"}, {"c": "traceroute / mtr", "d": "Path and packet loss"}, {"c": "openssl s_client -connect host:443", "d": "Inspect certificate"}, {"c": "ss -tulpn", "d": "Local listening ports"}, {"c": "tcpdump -i any port 80", "d": "Packet capture"}, {"c": "ip route / ip a", "d": "Routes and interfaces"}], "follow": [{"k": ["dns", "resolve", "nslookup", "dig"], "q": "Site works by IP but not by name. Steps?", "a": "It is DNS. I run dig against the default resolver and then against a public one, check A and CNAME records, TTL and propagation, and /etc/resolv.conf on the client."}, {"k": ["ssl", "tls", "certificate", "cert", "https"], "q": "How does the TLS handshake work in short?", "a": "Client says hello with supported ciphers, server sends its certificate, client validates the chain and expiry, then both agree on session keys. After that traffic is encrypted."}, {"k": ["load balancer", "alb", "nlb", "layer"], "q": "Layer 4 vs Layer 7 load balancer?", "a": "L4 such as NLB balances TCP/UDP by IP and port and is very fast. L7 such as ALB understands HTTP, so it can route by path or host header and terminate TLS."}, {"k": ["502", "503", "504", "gateway"], "q": "502 vs 503 vs 504?", "a": "502 means the gateway got an invalid response from the backend, 503 means no healthy backend is available, and 504 means the backend timed out. I read the LB and app logs accordingly."}, {"k": ["timeout", "refused", "connect"], "q": "Connection refused vs connection timed out?", "a": "Refused means the host replied but nothing listens on that port, so I check the service. Timeout means packets are dropped, so I check firewalls, security groups and routing."}, {"k": ["cidr", "subnet", "ip"], "q": "What does /24 mean and how many hosts?", "a": "/24 means 24 network bits, so 256 addresses and 254 usable. In AWS, 5 addresses per subnet are reserved, so 251 usable."}], "edge": [{"q": "Users in one country cannot open the site but others can. Where do you look?", "a": "DNS or CDN edge issues, geo blocking in WAF or security rules, an ISP routing problem, or a certificate chain problem on some clients. I test from that region using external tools and compare responses.", "fu": []}, {"q": "Intermittent 504s through the load balancer only under load. Why?", "a": "Backend slower than the LB idle or request timeout, connection pool exhaustion, or too few instances. I compare timeouts across layers and look at backend saturation.", "fu": []}, {"q": "Certificate was renewed but browsers still show the old one. Why?", "a": "Another server or listener is still serving the old cert, for example multiple LBs or an ingress not reloaded, or a CDN caching it. I check each endpoint with openssl s_client.", "fu": []}], "qa": [{"id": 35, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "General", "q": "Users report intermittent connection failures to your service running on EC2 behind a load balancer. Ping to the instances works, but some HTTP requests hang and time out randomly. CPU and memory are normal. How do you investigate the networking side?", "a": "Check LB health checks and target group health for flapping targets. Inspect security group and NACL rules for asymmetric or blocked ports. Look at connection/file-descriptor limits (ulimit), ephemeral port exhaustion, and conntrack table limits on instances. Use ss/netstat for stuck connections and tcpdump to see where packets drop. Check LB idle timeout vs keep-alive settings."}, {"id": 50, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "General", "q": "Users report a service is returning stale data. It runs behind an Nginx reverse proxy that you configured with caching. Backend data updates correctly when you hit it directly, but through the proxy users see old responses. How do you investigate and fix?", "a": "Nginx is serving cached responses past their intended freshness. Check proxy_cache settings, cache keys, and the Cache-Control/Expires headers the backend sends. Verify proxy_cache_valid and whether it ignores backend cache headers. Fix by setting correct cache TTLs, honoring backend headers, adding cache-busting for dynamic content, or purging the cache. Confirm via X-Cache-Status header."}, {"id": 77, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Users report that your website loads slowly only for visitors in Asia, while it's fast in the US where your single EC2 server lives. The server CPU and network look healthy. How would you approach reducing latency for far-away users?", "a": "The latency is due to physical distance to the single-region origin. Put a CDN (CloudFront) in front to cache and serve static assets from edge locations near users. For dynamic content, consider multi-region deployment or edge caching. Measure with tools like curl timing or a global latency test to confirm the geographic gap, then verify improvement after enabling the CDN."}, {"id": 93, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "General", "q": "A developer reports that their web app on a Linux server can reach some external hosts by IP but domain names fail to resolve intermittently, breaking API calls. Direct IP curls work. How do you diagnose the DNS issue?", "a": "Test resolution with dig/nslookup against configured resolvers; check /etc/resolv.conf and /etc/nsswitch.conf. Confirm the DNS server is reachable and not rate-limiting. Look for intermittent timeouts suggesting a flaky/unreachable resolver or missing fallback. Fix by pointing to reliable resolvers, adding a secondary nameserver, or running a local caching resolver (systemd-resolved/dnsmasq) to stabilize lookups."}, {"id": 102, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "General", "q": "A colleague set up an Nginx reverse proxy in front of your backend app, but the backend logs show every request coming from the proxy's IP instead of the real client IPs. Rate limiting and audit logs are now useless. How do you fix this so the backend sees real client IPs?", "a": "Configure Nginx to forward the client IP using proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for; and X-Real-IP $remote_addr;. Then configure the backend (or its framework/trusted-proxy setting) to read those headers instead of the direct connection IP, ensuring it only trusts the proxy's address."}, {"id": 160, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "During an incident a web service is unreachable from outside, but the app process is running and healthy locally. You suspect the host firewall is blocking the port. How would you verify and confirm whether the firewall is the problem?", "a": "First confirm the app is listening with ss -tlnp (or netstat). Test local access with curl localhost:<port> — if that works but remote fails, suspect firewall/security group. Check firewall rules with sudo iptables -L -n or sudo firewall-cmd --list-all/ufw status. On cloud, also verify the security group/NACL allows the port. Add the allow rule if missing and retest."}, {"id": 170, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Users report a website is intermittently unreachable. ping to the domain sometimes resolves to different IPs and sometimes fails. You suspect a DNS issue. How would you investigate whether DNS is the problem?", "a": "Use dig <domain> or nslookup to see what records are returned and from which nameserver. Compare results against the authoritative server (dig @<authoritative-ns> domain) to rule out stale caches. Check TTLs and whether multiple A records point to unhealthy hosts. Flush local resolver cache and test from another network. If one IP is bad, remove it from DNS or fix the failing backend."}, {"id": 190, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A developer says their new microservice can't connect to an external payment API — the connection just hangs and times out. Other services in the same cluster reach the internet fine. How would you diagnose the connectivity issue?", "a": "Exec into the pod and test layers: nslookup api.host (DNS), curl -v https://api.host or nc -zv host 443 (TCP reachability). If DNS fails, check CoreDNS/service config; if TCP fails, suspect a NetworkPolicy blocking egress, a missing route, or the external firewall not allowlisting your egress/NAT IP. Compare working pods to spot policy or namespace differences."}, {"id": 200, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "During an incident you need to confirm whether a remote database on port 5432 is reachable from an app server, but the server has no database client installed. The app keeps logging connection timeouts. How would you test network connectivity to that port from the Linux host?", "a": "Test the TCP port directly. Use nc -zv dbhost 5432 or nc -vz, or bash's timeout 5 bash -c '</dev/tcp/dbhost/5432' && echo open. telnet dbhost 5432 also works if installed. If it hangs/refuses, it's a network/security-group/firewall issue, not the app. Combine with ping/traceroute and DNS resolution checks to isolate where it fails."}, {"id": 234, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Users report your web app is slow only for customers in one region. Your monitoring shows backend latency and error rates are normal everywhere. Requests from that region take 3-4s just to establish a connection. How would you investigate?", "a": "Since backend metrics are fine, focus on the network path and edge. Test from that region using tools like curl -w timing breakdown (DNS, connect, TLS, TTFB) or a synthetic monitor. Large connect/TLS time points to routing/latency or a missing CDN edge near that region. Check DNS resolution (geo-routing), CDN edge coverage, and any regional firewall/peering issues. Fix by adding a CDN/edge PoP, enabling geo-DNS, or correcting misrouted traffic."}, {"id": 243, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Users report a website is returning an SSL certificate error saying the certificate has expired. The site was working fine last week and nothing was deployed. How would you diagnose and fix this?", "a": "Confirm with openssl s_client -connect host:443 -servername host or curl -vI https://host and check the notAfter date. The cert simply expired — likely auto-renewal failed. Check the renewal mechanism (certbot/ACM/cert-manager) logs and status. Renew the cert (e.g. certbot renew, reissue in ACM, or check cert-manager Certificate resource), redeploy/reload the server (nginx -s reload), then verify the new expiry. Add expiry monitoring/ale…"}, {"id": 270, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "During an incident you need to check whether a remote service on another host is reachable on TCP port 5432 from your Linux server, since an app can't connect to its database. Ping to the host works. How would you test just the port connectivity?", "a": "Ping only tests ICMP, not the TCP port. Use nc -zv <host> 5432 or telnet <host> 5432 to test the TCP handshake. You can also use curl -v telnet://<host>:5432 or timeout 5 bash -c '</dev/tcp/<host>/5432'. If it fails, check firewall rules, security groups, the DB listen address, and whether the service is actually running on that port."}, {"id": 290, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "You are a mid-level engineer managing a private-subnet microservice that needs to connect to an external API. The traffic is failing. How do you diagnose if the issue is a NAT Gateway or a Network ACL (NACL)?", "a": "Check the route table associated with the private subnet; verify if the default route (0.0.0.0/0) points to a NAT Gateway. If it does, verify the NAT Gateway is in a public subnet with an IGW route. Then, check the NACLs for the private subnet to ensure both outbound traffic and inbound ephemeral ports (1024-65535) are explicitly allowed, as NACLs are stateless."}, {"id": 321, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "An AWS EC2 instance in a public subnet cannot be reached via SSH. You have confirmed the security group allows port 22. What is the most likely networking component misconfiguration in the VPC?", "a": "Check the route table associated with the public subnet. Ensure it has a route to 0.0.0.0/0 pointing to an Internet Gateway (IGW). If the route is missing or points to a NAT Gateway (which is for private egress only), inbound traffic cannot be returned to the public internet, causing connection failures."}, {"id": 330, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A microservice intermittently fails to connect to a Redis cache in another K8s namespace. How do you debug the network path between these two services?", "a": "Check K8s NetworkPolicies to ensure egress from the source namespace and ingress to the destination are allowed. Use 'kubectl exec' to run 'nc -zv <service-dns> <port>' to test connectivity. If it fails, check CoreDNS logs for resolution issues. If DNS works, use 'istioctl' or 'tcpdump' on the pod interfaces to verify if packets are being dropped by the CNI or iptables rules."}, {"id": 339, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "A mid-level engineer finds that an AWS Network Load Balancer (NLB) in a private subnet is showing 'unhealthy' targets for a group of instances. How do you diagnose if the issue is the target group health check or the internal firewall?", "a": "Check the NLB 'TargetGroupHealth' metrics to see if health check requests are reaching the instances. Then, verify the security group rules on the target instances to ensure they allow traffic on the health check port specifically from the NLB's VPC CIDR or specific private IPs."}, {"id": 355, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Users on a specific region report your website is unreachable, while others load it fine. DNS resolves correctly everywhere and your servers are healthy. How would you diagnose where the traffic is being dropped for that region?", "a": "Run traceroute/mtr from an affected-region vantage (or a test node there) to see where packets stall — a specific hop or ISP peering issue. Check if resolved IPs differ by region (GeoDNS/CDN edge). Test the CDN/edge PoP serving that region for outages. Compare TLS handshake and HTTP from that region. If it's a bad edge or upstream path, failover to another PoP or engage the CDN/ISP; the drop is usually network path or edge-specific, not or…"}, {"id": 385, "cat": "Networking", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague reports that two EC2 instances in different VPCs need to communicate privately, but connections between them time out even though both have correct security groups allowing the traffic. There's no VPN or peering yet. Why can't they talk, and what would you set up?", "a": "Instances in separate VPCs can't reach each other over private IPs by default—there's no route between the VPCs. Security groups only matter once a network path exists. Set up VPC peering (or Transit Gateway), add routes in each VPC's route tables pointing to the peering connection for the other VPC's CIDR, ensure CIDRs don't overlap, and confirm security groups/NACLs allow the traffic."}]}, {"name": "Monitoring", "slug": "monitoring", "icon": "📈", "color": "#e6522c", "blurb": "Prometheus, Grafana, alerts, logs, SLOs. Shows maturity beyond just deploying.", "diagrams": [{"t": "Prometheus + Grafana stack", "steps": ["Apps / node_exporter expose /metrics", "Prometheus scrapes and stores", "Alertmanager routes alerts", "Grafana dashboards", "Slack / PagerDuty"]}, {"t": "Alert fired: what next?", "steps": ["Acknowledge alert", "Open dashboard: scope and start time", "Correlate with deploys / changes", "Check logs / traces", "Mitigate then fix root cause"]}, {"t": "Golden signals", "steps": ["Latency", "Traffic", "Errors", "Saturation"]}], "cheats": [{"c": "rate(http_requests_total[5m])", "d": "Requests per second"}, {"c": "histogram_quantile(0.99, ...)", "d": "p99 latency"}, {"c": "up == 0", "d": "Targets that are down"}, {"c": "node_filesystem_avail_bytes", "d": "Disk free"}, {"c": "alert for: 5m", "d": "Avoid noisy alerts"}, {"c": "Loki / ELK / CloudWatch Logs", "d": "Log search"}, {"c": "SLI / SLO / error budget", "d": "Reliability targets"}, {"c": "kubectl top", "d": "Quick resource check"}], "follow": [{"k": ["alert", "noise", "fatigue", "firing"], "q": "How do you reduce alert fatigue?", "a": "Alert on symptoms that affect users, not every cause. Use for: durations, severity levels, grouping and inhibition in Alertmanager, and a runbook link in each alert. I delete or tune alerts nobody acts on."}, {"k": ["prometheus", "scrape", "target", "metric"], "q": "Prometheus target shows DOWN. What do you check?", "a": "Whether the endpoint is reachable from Prometheus, the port and path, network policy or security group, TLS or auth config, and the scrape error message in the targets page."}, {"k": ["latency", "slow", "p99", "percentile"], "q": "Why p99 instead of average latency?", "a": "Averages hide slow outliers. p99 shows what the slowest 1% of users experience, which is where problems show first."}, {"k": ["slo", "sli", "error budget", "sla"], "q": "SLI, SLO and SLA in simple words?", "a": "SLI is the measurement, like percent of successful requests. SLO is the internal target, like 99.9%. SLA is the contract with penalties. The error budget is the allowed failure that guides how fast we can release."}, {"k": ["log", "logs", "search", "elk", "loki"], "q": "Metrics vs logs vs traces?", "a": "Metrics tell me something is wrong and how much, logs tell me what happened in detail, and traces show where time is spent across services. I use all three together."}, {"k": ["error rate", "5xx", "spike"], "q": "Error rate spiked right after a deploy. First action?", "a": "Check whether the spike began at the deploy time, and if yes, roll back first and investigate after. Stop the user impact first, then find root cause with logs and diff."}], "edge": [{"q": "Grafana shows no data for a panel that worked yesterday. How do you debug?", "a": "Check the time range and variables, run the query in Prometheus directly, verify the target is up, check if metric or label names changed after a release, and the data source health.", "fu": []}, {"q": "Disk alert fires but df on the server looks fine. Why?", "a": "The alert may use the wrong mountpoint or filesystem type, or the metric is stale because the exporter is down. I compare the query labels with the real mounts and check the exporter.", "fu": []}, {"q": "You get paged at 3 AM for CPU 85% that fixes itself. What do you change?", "a": "The alert is not actionable. I add a for: duration, move to a warning level, and alert on user-facing symptoms like latency instead. Pages must require human action.", "fu": []}, {"q": "Prometheus is running out of memory. Options?", "a": "High cardinality labels are the usual cause, so I drop or reduce them. Then shorter retention, increase scrape interval, shard or use remote storage like Thanos or Mimir.", "fu": []}], "qa": [{"id": 4, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your monitoring dashboard shows CPU alerts firing constantly for a service, but the team ignores them because most are false alarms. Leadership wants alert noise reduced without missing real issues. What's your approach?", "a": "Review alert thresholds and add duration (for: 5m) so brief spikes don't fire. Alert on symptoms/SLOs (latency, error rate) rather than raw CPU. Group and deduplicate alerts, add severity levels, and route only actionable ones to on-call. Silence known-noisy alerts, tune based on historical data, and ensure each alert has a clear runbook."}, {"id": 9, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "General", "q": "An application in production started responding slowly. Your monitoring shows normal CPU and memory, but response latency has doubled. Users are complaining. Where do you start looking beyond CPU and memory metrics?", "a": "Check downstream dependencies: database query times, external API latency, and network. Look at connection pool exhaustion, disk I/O, and thread/queue saturation. Inspect application logs and traces (APM) for slow endpoints. Check for increased request volume or garbage collection pauses. Latency often comes from I/O waits or dependency slowdowns rather than raw CPU/memory usage."}, {"id": 25, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "General", "q": "You set up Prometheus to scrape metrics from your services, but one service's target shows as 'DOWN' in the Prometheus targets page while the app itself is serving user traffic normally. How do you investigate?", "a": "Open Prometheus Targets page and read the error under the DOWN target — commonly connection refused, timeout, or 404. Verify the /metrics endpoint is exposed and reachable from Prometheus (network/firewall/service discovery). Check the scrape config: correct port, path, and scheme. Confirm the metrics port differs from the app's traffic port. curl the endpoint from Prometheus's network to confirm, then fix config or expose the exporter."}, {"id": 40, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "General", "q": "You set up alerting so your team gets notified when a service goes down, but during a real outage the on-call engineer never received the page even though the alert clearly fired in your monitoring system. How do you investigate why the notification failed?", "a": "Trace the alert delivery pipeline: confirm the alert fired and routed correctly in Alertmanager (or equivalent), check routing rules matched the right receiver, and verify the notification channel (PagerDuty/Slack/email) integration key and webhook are valid. Look for silences/inhibition rules suppressing it, on-call schedule gaps, and delivery logs. Test end-to-end with a synthetic alert."}, {"id": 45, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "General", "q": "Users report your web app is intermittently slow. Your Grafana dashboard shows p50 latency is fine but p99 has spiked dramatically. CPU and memory are healthy across pods. How do you approach finding the cause of the tail latency?", "a": "Tail latency often points to a subset of slow requests. Investigate: uneven load balancing hitting a hot pod, GC pauses, a slow downstream dependency (DB, cache, external API), connection pool exhaustion, or noisy-neighbor issues. Use distributed tracing (Jaeger/OpenTelemetry) to find slow spans, check DB slow-query logs, and look at per-pod latency to isolate outliers."}, {"id": 65, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "You're setting up Prometheus and Grafana for a new service. Alerts fire correctly, but your team complains the CPU graph in Grafana shows values above 100%. A junior asks if the server is broken. How do you explain and fix the dashboard?", "a": "Values over 100% usually mean the metric is per-core aggregated across multiple cores (e.g., node_cpu summed), so a 4-core box can show up to 400%. It's not a fault. Fix the query to normalize: use rate() over the counter and divide by the core count, or use 'avg by (instance)' to get percentage of total. Confirm units and adjust the panel to display 0-100%."}, {"id": 85, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "General", "q": "You configured Prometheus alerts for a service, but the on-call team is getting flooded with alerts every time a pod briefly restarts during normal deploys, causing alert fatigue. How would you reduce the noise while keeping real incidents visible?", "a": "Add a 'for:' duration so alerts only fire if the condition persists (e.g. for: 5m), filtering transient blips. Tune thresholds to symptom-based SLOs rather than every restart. Use inhibition rules and grouping in Alertmanager so related alerts collapse, add maintenance/deploy silences, and route low-severity alerts to a channel instead of paging."}, {"id": 92, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "General", "q": "Your team stores application logs only on each EC2 instance's local disk. During an incident an auto-scaling event terminated the affected instance, and the logs needed for the postmortem were gone. Product wants logs to survive instance termination. How would you approach this?", "a": "Ship logs off-host to centralized storage. Install an agent (CloudWatch Logs agent/Fluent Bit) to stream logs to CloudWatch Logs or an ELK/OpenSearch stack. Configure log groups with retention. This decouples logs from instance lifecycle so they persist after termination, are searchable, and support alerting. Avoid relying on ephemeral local disk for anything needed post-incident."}, {"id": 121, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "Your team's Grafana dashboard for a service went completely blank overnight — no data points after 2 AM, but the application is clearly serving traffic and users aren't complaining. Prometheus itself is up. How do you figure out why the metrics stopped flowing?", "a": "Check Prometheus Targets page (Status > Targets) for the service — likely showing 'down' or scrape errors. Confirm the exporter/metrics endpoint (curl :port/metrics) is reachable. Check if the pod/instance IP changed and service discovery didn't update. Look at Prometheus logs for scrape timeouts. Also verify the dashboard's time range and datasource. Common cause: exporter crashed or endpoint moved without SD updating."}, {"id": 140, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Monitoring shows your service's p99 latency spiking every night at the same time, though average latency and request volume look normal. No deploys happen then. How would you investigate the recurring latency spike?", "a": "Correlate the spike time with scheduled jobs: cron tasks, backups, log rotation, batch/ETL jobs, or CronJobs competing for CPU/IO/DB locks. Check node/host metrics (CPU, disk IO, network) and DB slow-query logs during the window. Use tracing to find which downstream call slows. Fix by rescheduling/throttling the batch job, isolating resources, or provisioning headroom. full july ."}, {"id": 144, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "During a production incident your monitoring shows CPU and memory look normal on the app servers, yet users report slow page loads. Your dashboards only track infrastructure metrics, not the user experience. How would you improve observability to catch this class of issue?", "a": "Add application-level and user-facing metrics: request latency (p50/p95/p99), error rate, and throughput (RED/USE method). Instrument the app with metrics and distributed tracing to see where time is spent across services/DB. Add SLOs based on latency/errors and alert on those, not just CPU/memory. Correlate logs, metrics, and traces to pinpoint the slow component."}, {"id": 154, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "During a production incident an app is returning 500s. You have logs, metrics, and traces but the team wastes time guessing where the failure is. You need to quickly pinpoint which downstream dependency is failing. How would you approach this using observability tooling?", "a": "Start from the symptom in metrics (error rate/latency by endpoint), then use distributed tracing to follow a failing request across services and see which span errors or spikes in latency — that isolates the failing dependency (DB, cache, external API). Correlate with that service's logs using the trace ID. Check recent deploys and dependency health/dashboards to confirm root cause before mitigating."}, {"id": 184, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your Prometheus setup alerts constantly with false positives during rolling deploys—pods restarting trigger 'target down' and 'high error rate' alerts even though the deploy is healthy. On-call is getting alert fatigue. How would you make alerts more reliable without hiding real problems?", "a": "Add for: durations to alert rules so transient blips during deploys don't page. Use rate-based/SLO error alerts over sustained windows rather than instantaneous spikes. Silence or inhibit alerts during known deploy windows via Alertmanager silences or deploy annotations. Scope 'target down' to sustained absence, and rely on multi-signal alerts (error budget burn) instead of single restarts. Tune scrape/health grace periods so normal pod chur…"}, {"id": 204, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your team's Grafana dashboards show request latency spiking every night at the same time, correlating with a batch job. You need to prove the batch job is the cause and reduce user impact without stopping the job. How would you approach this?", "a": "Correlate metrics: overlay batch job start/end timestamps with latency, DB connections, CPU/IO on shared resources. Confirm resource contention (e.g., DB CPU or disk IO saturation) using traces/query metrics. Mitigate by scheduling the job during lower-traffic windows, throttling its concurrency/batch size, moving reads to a replica, or isolating it onto separate resources. Add alerting on the leading indicator."}, {"id": 220, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A monitoring alert fires that an application's p99 latency jumped, but overall request rate and error rate look normal. A senior asks you to determine whether the slowness affects all endpoints or just some. How would you investigate using your metrics/observability tools?", "a": "Break down latency by endpoint/route label in Prometheus/Grafana instead of viewing an aggregate. Query p99 grouped by path or handler to isolate which endpoints regressed. Correlate with downstream dependency latency (DB, external API) and traces (if available) to find where time is spent. Check whether the spike aligns with a deploy, a specific host, or increased payload size, then focus remediation on the affected endpoint."}, {"id": 249, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After onboarding a new team, your Prometheus server starts hitting high memory usage and occasionally OOMs, and dashboards begin loading slowly. Many new services were added with high-cardinality labels. How would you diagnose and reduce the load?", "a": "High cardinality (e.g., labels with user IDs, request IDs) explodes time series count. Use prometheus_tsdb_head_series and the /status page to find top metrics by series. Drop or relabel offending labels via metric_relabel_configs, reduce scrape frequency, and shorten retention or offload to remote storage. Educate teams to avoid unbounded label values, and add recording rules to precompute heavy queries for dashboards."}, {"id": 280, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "Your monitoring system (Grafana/Alertmanager) triggers a firing alert for high CPU on a specific node. You confirm it is not a temporary spike. Before taking any action like restarting the pod, what diagnostic steps do you take?", "a": "I would use 'kubectl top pods' to identify the specific pod consuming the most resources and then 'kubectl logs' to look for error loops or application crashes. I would also check the Kubelet logs on the node to ensure it is not a system-level issue. If the pod is clearly identified, I would capture a thread dump or stack trace if possible before restarting to understand the root cause of the high CPU usage."}, {"id": 369, "cat": "Monitoring", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After adding a new microservice to your Kubernetes cluster, its Prometheus metrics never appear in Grafana. The service exposes /metrics on port 9090 and returns data when you curl it from inside a pod, but Prometheus shows no target for it. How would you diagnose why Prometheus isn't scraping it?", "a": "Check Prometheus targets page (Status > Targets) to see if the endpoint is discovered. Likely the ServiceMonitor/PodMonitor or scrape config doesn't match the service's labels or port name. Verify label selectors, the named port matches, and correct namespace. Confirm the Prometheus Operator watches that namespace. Fix the ServiceMonitor labels/port to match the service so Prometheus discovers and scrapes it."}]}, {"name": "Incident Response", "slug": "incident-response", "icon": "🚨", "color": "#ef4444", "blurb": "On-call, outages, RCA, communication. Where seniors are separated from juniors.", "diagrams": [{"t": "Incident lifecycle", "steps": ["Detect (alert / report)", "Triage severity", "Mitigate (rollback / scale / failover)", "Communicate status", "Root cause analysis", "Postmortem + action items"]}, {"t": "First 10 minutes", "steps": ["Acknowledge and declare", "Check what changed (deploys)", "Scope: who and what is affected", "Stop the bleeding", "Update stakeholders"]}, {"t": "Blameless postmortem", "steps": ["Timeline", "Impact", "Root cause and contributing factors", "What went well / badly", "Action items with owners"]}], "cheats": [{"c": "Rollback first, investigate later", "d": "Stop user impact quickly"}, {"c": "Check recent changes", "d": "Most incidents follow a change"}, {"c": "Severity levels SEV1-SEV3", "d": "Decides who is paged"}, {"c": "Incident commander role", "d": "One person coordinates"}, {"c": "MTTR / MTTD", "d": "Recovery and detection time"}, {"c": "Runbooks", "d": "Documented repeatable steps"}, {"c": "Status page updates", "d": "Keep users informed"}, {"c": "Blameless culture", "d": "Fix systems, not people"}], "follow": [{"k": ["deploy", "release", "rollback"], "q": "Would you roll back or fix forward?", "a": "If the cause is a recent change and rollback is safe, I roll back because it is fastest. I fix forward only when rollback is risky, for example after a database migration."}, {"k": ["key", "secret", "leak", "committed"], "q": "Secret leaked. What are the immediate steps?", "a": "Revoke and rotate it immediately, check logs for misuse, remove it from the repository, assess the impact, and add prevention such as secret scanning."}, {"k": ["mttr", "recovery", "reduce"], "q": "How do you reduce MTTR?", "a": "Better alerts and dashboards, runbooks, automated rollback, practice with game days, and clear on-call ownership."}, {"k": ["rca", "root", "postmortem", "after"], "q": "How do you run a good postmortem?", "a": "Blameless, factual timeline, root and contributing causes using five whys, and action items with owners and dates that are actually tracked."}, {"k": ["communicate", "stakeholder", "manager", "customer"], "q": "How do you communicate during an outage?", "a": "Short regular updates in a single channel and status page: what is impacted, what we are doing, and the next update time. No guessing in public."}], "edge": [{"q": "You are the only one online and a SEV1 starts. You are junior. What do you do?", "a": "Acknowledge and start mitigation using the runbook, such as rollback. I escalate immediately to the on-call senior or manager rather than waiting, and keep notes. Escalating early is the right behaviour.", "fu": [{"q": "When do you escalate?", "a": "When impact is high, I am not making progress in 15 minutes, or the fix needs access or authority I do not have."}]}, {"q": "You made the change that caused the outage. How do you handle it?", "a": "I tell the team immediately, help roll back, and share facts in the postmortem without hiding. Owning it and proposing a guardrail that prevents repeats is what builds trust.", "fu": []}, {"q": "Alert fires but dashboards look normal. Real or false positive?", "a": "I verify using a second source such as logs, synthetic checks or user reports before dismissing. If it is a false positive I fix the alert, and if the monitoring itself failed, that becomes the incident.", "fu": []}], "qa": [{"id": 58, "cat": "Incident Response", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're on call and a critical payment service starts throwing errors. You suspect the last deploy 20 minutes ago. There's no obvious single cause yet and the pressure is high. Walk through how you'd handle the incident in the first 15 minutes.", "a": "First mitigate: roll back to the last known-good version to restore service before deep debugging. Acknowledge the alert, declare an incident, and communicate status. Check dashboards/logs to confirm impact. Once stable, capture evidence for a blameless postmortem. Prioritize recovery over root cause during the outage."}, {"id": 103, "cat": "Incident Response", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're on call and Prometheus alerts you that a service's error rate is climbing. Grafana confirms 5xx errors started right after an autoscaling event added new pods. The old pods are healthy; only new pods return errors. How do you triage this quickly?", "a": "Check logs of the new pods (kubectl logs) for the errors—likely a config/env mismatch, missing dependency, or a new image pulled. Compare the new pods' image tag and config against healthy ones. If a bad rollout, run kubectl rollout undo to revert. Optionally scale down or cordon while investigating to stop routing traffic to broken pods."}, {"id": 113, "cat": "Incident Response", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're on call and users report a web app is intermittently slow. Your load balancer fronts three EC2 instances, and one instance is responding much slower than the others but still passes health checks. How would you identify and take the bad instance out of rotation?", "a": "Check per-target latency in ALB CloudWatch/access logs to pin the slow instance. SSH in and inspect top, iostat, and app logs for CPU, disk, or memory pressure or GC pauses. Temporarily deregister it from the target group (or set to draining) so users stop hitting it, then investigate root cause—consider tightening health check thresholds to catch degraded latency, not just up/down."}, {"id": 120, "cat": "Incident Response", "lvl": "General", "q": "You're on call and monitoring shows a sudden drop in traffic reaching your backend service, while the load balancer reports all targets healthy. Users say the site returns blank pages. How would you triage where requests are being lost?", "a": "Trace the request path: check LB access logs and metrics for 4xx/5xx or dropped requests, then app logs for errors. Verify DNS resolves correctly and TLS certs are valid. Test directly against a backend instance to isolate the LB vs app. Check recent deploys or config changes. Correlate the drop's start time with any change to pinpoint the layer failing."}, {"id": 163, "cat": "Incident Response", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "A colleague accidentally committed an AWS access key into your Git repo and pushed it to GitHub. They deleted the line and committed again, thinking it's gone. Why is the secret still exposed and what should you do immediately?", "a": "The key still lives in Git history (previous commits), so anyone with repo access can retrieve it. Deleting a line doesn't purge history. Immediately: rotate/deactivate the leaked key in AWS IAM (treat it as compromised). Then scrub history using git filter-repo or BFG and force-push, or better, create a fresh repo. Add the file to .gitignore and use secret scanning (git-secrets/GitHub push protection) going forward."}, {"id": 194, "cat": "Incident Response", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "During a production incident an EC2-hosted service becomes unresponsive. Restarting fixes it temporarily, but it recurs every few hours. Logs show the app slowly consuming more memory until it's OOM-killed. How would you investigate and stabilize this?", "a": "Confirm the pattern via CloudWatch/metrics and dmesg for OOM kills. Profile heap growth (language-specific tools, e.g. heap dumps) to find the leak. Short-term: add a memory-based restart/health check and set memory limits so failures are graceful. Add monitoring and alerting on memory trend. Long-term: fix the leak in code. Consider autoscaling and readiness probes to reduce user impact."}, {"id": 229, "cat": "Incident Response", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After an incident your team wants to reduce mean-time-to-recovery. Currently when a service degrades, engineers spend 30+ minutes just figuring out what changed and which dashboards to look at. How would you improve incident response readiness?", "a": "Create runbooks per service linking key dashboards, logs, and common failure modes. Set up correlated alerting that includes context (recent deploys, related metrics). Add deploy markers/annotations to dashboards so changes are visible. Define clear on-call roles and an incident channel/template. Track MTTR and run blameless postmortems, feeding fixes back into runbooks and alerts."}, {"id": 244, "cat": "Incident Response", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "During a production incident your primary application starts throwing 500s. You need to quickly determine whether a recent deploy caused it. Multiple teams deploy to shared services and there's no single changelog. How would you approach identifying the trigger and mitigating impact?", "a": "First mitigate: check if rolling back the most recent deploy stops the errors. To find the trigger, correlate the error spike start time against deployment timestamps (CI/CD history, kubectl rollout history, ArgoCD), config changes, and feature flag toggles. Check dashboards/logs for the first failing request. If a deploy aligns, roll it back. Long term: enforce a centralized deploy/change feed and annotate Grafana with deploy events for fast …"}, {"id": 259, "cat": "Incident Response", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "During a deployment your service's error rate spiked, and you suspect a dependency microservice is failing. You want to confirm the blast radius and prevent cascading failures while the team fixes the root cause. How would you approach this using DevOps/SRE practices?", "a": "Check dashboards/traces to confirm which upstream dependency is failing and how many requests depend on it. Mitigate by enabling circuit breakers, timeouts, and retries with backoff so the failing dependency doesn't exhaust threads/connections. Use graceful degradation or fallbacks for non-critical paths. Isolate with bulkheads/rate limiting. Communicate blast radius via SLOs, then roll back or scale the failing service."}, {"id": 353, "cat": "Incident Response", "lvl": "Mid", "q": "After a new deploy, your service's error budget is burning fast — Prometheus shows a spike in 5xx errors. You need to decide quickly whether to roll back. What signals do you check to confirm the deploy is the cause before rolling back?", "a": "Correlate the error spike timestamp with the deploy time. Check if errors are isolated to new pods/version (filter by version label or pod). Inspect application logs for stack traces or config errors, and dependency health (DB, downstream). Compare request rate/latency to rule out load. If errors clearly track the new version and healthy old pods exist, roll back immediately, then investigate root cause offline."}, {"id": 390, "cat": "Incident Response", "lvl": "Junior", "q": "During an incident a service is down and you need to quickly find which process is holding port 443 on a Linux host so you can restart or kill it, but you're not sure how to identify it. How would you find and stop the process listening on that port?", "a": "Use sudo ss -ltnp 'sport = :443' (or lsof -i :443) to list the listening process and its PID. Confirm the process name, then gracefully stop it via its service manager (sudo systemctl restart <service>) rather than kill -9 when possible. Only kill the PID directly (kill <pid>) if there's no managed service. Verify with ss again that the port is freed or re-bound."}]}], "prep": {"intro": {"title": "Your 60-second self introduction (edit with your real details)", "text": "Hi, I'm <NAME>, a DevOps engineer with 1+ year of hands-on experience. In my current role I work on CI/CD pipelines using <Jenkins/GitHub Actions>, containerizing applications with Docker and deploying them on Kubernetes. I have worked with AWS services like EC2, VPC, IAM, S3 and RDS, and I manage infrastructure using Terraform. I also set up monitoring and alerts with Prometheus and Grafana and I take part in on-call troubleshooting. One thing I am proud of is <ONE REAL ACHIEVEMENT with a number, e.g. cut pipeline time from 25 to 10 minutes>. I am now looking for a role where I can own bigger systems and grow in Kubernetes and cloud automation."}, "star": [["Situation", "Describe the system in one line: \"We ran X microservices on Kubernetes on AWS.\""], ["Task", "What was your responsibility: \"Deployments were slow and failing sometimes, I had to fix it.\""], ["Action", "Give 3 technical actions in order, with tool names: caching, probes, Terraform module, alert, etc."], ["Result", "Numbers: time saved, downtime reduced, cost saved, tickets reduced."]], "hr": [["Why do you want to switch?", "I have learned the fundamentals in my current role and now want more ownership and exposure to larger scale systems, such as production Kubernetes and cloud architecture. I am looking for a place where I can keep growing and contribute more."], ["What is your current CTC and expected CTC?", "My current CTC is 2.88 LPA, but I have grown a lot technically since then and my skills are now closer to market level for a DevOps engineer. Based on my skills in Kubernetes, Terraform, AWS and CI/CD, I am expecting in the range of 6 to 8 LPA, and I am open to discuss based on the role and responsibilities."], ["Can you tell me about a production issue you handled?", "Use STAR: system, what broke, how you diagnosed it (commands and tools), the fix, and the prevention you added. Pick one real incident and rehearse it."], ["What is your biggest weakness?", "I used to jump into fixing before understanding the full picture. Now I follow a checklist: check recent changes, logs and metrics first, and then act. It has made my troubleshooting faster and safer."], ["What is your notice period?", "State your real notice period, and if negotiable say so. Having a short or negotiable notice helps in hiring."], ["Where do you see yourself in 3 years?", "As a strong DevOps or SRE engineer who owns infrastructure design and reliability, mentors juniors, and maybe holds a cloud or Kubernetes certification."], ["How do you handle disagreement with a developer?", "I bring data like logs or metrics, understand their constraint, and propose a solution that meets both speed and stability. If needed I involve the lead. It is about the system, not the person."]], "ask": ["What does the on-call rotation look like and how are incidents handled?", "What does your deployment pipeline look like today and what do you want to improve?", "Which cloud and tools does the team use, and is there scope to learn Kubernetes/Terraform in depth?", "How is success measured in the first 6 months for this role?"], "plan": [["Week 1", "Revise Linux + Networking basics. Practise 10 questions per day from this site and say answers out loud."], ["Week 2", "Docker + CI/CD. Build one pipeline: GitHub Actions to build, scan and push image."], ["Week 3", "Kubernetes + AWS. Deploy a sample app on a cluster with probes, HPA and an Ingress, using Terraform for the VPC and cluster."], ["Week 4", "Monitoring + incidents + mock interviews. Add Prometheus and Grafana to your project, then use the Mock mode daily. Apply to 10-15 companies per week."]], "tips": ["Have 2 solid projects on GitHub with a README and architecture diagram (Terraform + Kubernetes + CI/CD + monitoring). Interviewers at 6+ LPA expect hands-on proof.", "Quantify your resume: \"reduced build time from 25 to 9 minutes with caching\", not \"worked on CI/CD\".", "Learn one scripting language well: Bash plus basic Python.", "A certification such as AWS Cloud Practitioner/SAA or CKA gives a strong signal for your experience level.", "Apply to product companies, startups and service companies that hire DevOps in the 5 to 9 LPA band, and use LinkedIn referrals. Hike jumps of 100%+ are possible but depend on skills and market, so keep applying widely.", "Never lie about experience. Be honest, and explain what you actually did and how you would do the rest."], "neg": ["Do not give a number first if you can avoid it. Ask: \"What is the budget for this role?\"", "If asked current CTC, say it truthfully and immediately explain your growth and current market value.", "Give a range with your target at the bottom, like 7 to 9 LPA if you want 6+.", "Compare offers politely. Competing offers are your best leverage.", "Check total package: fixed vs variable, notice buyout, joining bonus, on-call allowance."]}, "generic": [{"q": "How would you prevent this from happening again?", "a": "I would add monitoring and an alert for the early warning signal, automate the fix or a guard in the pipeline, update the runbook, and do a blameless review so the team learns."}, {"q": "How would you explain this issue to a non-technical manager?", "a": "I would say what was impacted, since when, what we did to restore it, and what we are doing so it does not repeat, in simple words with no jargon."}, {"q": "Have you faced something like this in real life?", "a": "Use a real example if you have one, using STAR. If not, say honestly that you practised it in a lab or project and explain exactly what you did and learned."}, {"q": "What would you do if the fix does not work?", "a": "I would escalate early, rollback to the last known good state if possible, and keep stakeholders updated while I try the next hypothesis."}]};